hadiths[910]{hadithnumber,text}:
"10804","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated: The Prophet ﷺ would not explain anything of the Quran by his own opinion, except for a limited number of verses which Jibril, peace be upon him, had taught him. > **""The Prophet did not interpret the Quran by personal judgment but only conveyed what was taught to him by revelation in those specific verses.""**"
"10805","Al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim al-Hilali narrated: Nafi ibn al-Azraq and Najdah ibn Uwaymir came with a group of Kharijite leaders seeking knowledge until they reached Makkah. They found Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, sitting near Zamzam wearing a red cloak and shirt, while people stood around asking him about Tafsir, saying, O Abu Abbas, what do you say about such-and-such, and he would answer, it is such-and-such. Nafi said, how bold you are, O Ibn Abbas, in what you convey today. Ibn Abbas replied, may your mother lose you, O Nafi, shall I tell you who is bolder than me. Nafi asked who. He said, a man who speaks about what he has no knowledge of, or who conceals knowledge he possesses. Nafi said, you spoke truthfully, I came to ask you. Ibn Abbas said, bring it, O son of Azraq, and ask. He asked about the following words, and Ibn Abbas answered each, citing pre-Islamic Arab poetry as proof that Arabs knew the meanings before the Book was revealed to Muhammad ﷺ: 1. Shuwaz in: There will be sent against you a flame of fire - Ibn Abbas said it is blazing flame with no smoke, citing Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt about kindling the flame of shuwaz. 2. Nuhas in: And smoke, and you will not defend yourselves - it is smoke with no flame, citing al-Nabighah al-Dhubyani about a lamp with no smoke in it. 3. Amshaj in: Mixed drops to test him - the fluid of the man and the fluid of the woman when gathered in the womb become a mixture, citing Abu Dhuayb al-Hudhali about an arrow mixed. 4. The joining of shank with shank - it means war and hardship, citing Abu Dhuayb about war baring its shank. 5. Sons and grandsons - your sons crowd around you, your grandsons are your servants, citing Umayyah about servants hastening around. 6. You are only of those bewitched - meaning of those created, citing Umayyah al-Thaqafi about sparrows of bewitched mankind. 7. Mulim in: We cast him into the sea while he was blameworthy - meaning sinful, citing Umayyah that the evildoer is mulim. 8. Al-Falaq - the light of morning, citing Labid ibn Rabiah that dawn splits and removes darkness. 9. Asa in: That you grieve not over what escapes you - meaning do not grieve, citing Labid about one with little grief. 10. Yahur in: He thought he would never return - meaning return, citing Labid about man like a shooting star returning to ash. 11. An in: They will circulate between it and scalding water intensely heated - that whose heat has reached its peak, citing al-Nabighah about blood of the belly intensely hot. 12. Al-Sarim in: So it became as a dark barren night - the intensely dark night, citing al-Nabighah about night mixing barren tracts. 13. Ghasaq al-layl - when night grows dark, citing al-Nabighah about intense darkness. 14. Muqit in: Allah is over all things Powerful - meaning Able, citing Imru al-Qays that he is capable in requiting harm. 15. Asasa in: And the night when it settles - the coming of its blackness, citing Imru al-Qays about night settling with a gleam of light. 16. Zaim in: And I will guarantee it - the guarantor, citing Imru al-Qays that he guarantees a march if restored to kingship. 17. Fum in: And its wheat - wheat, citing Abu Dhuayb about coming to Madinah for wheat farming. 18. Azlam - divining arrows, citing al-Hutayiah that no lot is raised for him with divining arrows. 19. Companions of the left - companions of the north side, citing Zuhayr ibn Abi Sulma about grey hair descending on the left. 20. When the seas are merged - its water mixes with the water of the earth, citing Zuhayr about their seas merging with his seas in ancient rivalry. 21. Hubuk in: By the sky full of paths - pathways, citing Zuhayr about the north wind weaving with star roots having no paths. 22. Exalted is the grandeur of our Lord - our Lord's greatness is exalted, citing Tarafah ibn al-Abd to al-Numan ibn al-Mundhir about exalting his grandeur. 23. Harad in: Until you become worn out - worn and decayed, citing Tarafah about becoming decrepit from remembrance of Layla. 24. Samidun in: While you are heedless - distracted in amusement, citing Huzaylah bint Bakr weeping over Ad about abandoning idle diversion. 25. Ittasaqa in: When it becomes full - when it gathers together, citing Abu Sirmah al-Ansari about well-ordered young she-camels needing a driver. 26. Al-Samad - the One to Whom all matters are directed and sought, citing a woman of Banu Asad about the chief master sought in need. 27. Atham in: He will meet punishment - recompense, citing Bishr ibn Abi Khazim al-Asadi about a standing place calling against them with recompense. 28. Kazim in: While he is repressed - silent, citing Zuhayr ibn Khuzaymah al-Absi about restraining the tongue at the calamity of Shas. 29. Rikzan in: Or hear from them a sound - a voice, citing Khirash ibn Zuhayr about lowering voices and hiding when cavalry descends. 30. When you slew them by His permission - when you killed them by His leave, citing Utbah al-Laythi about striking them with swords splitting skulls like colocynth. 31. O Prophet, when you divorce women, was divorce known in pre-Islamic times - yes, an irrevocable triple divorce, citing al-Asha of Banu Qays ibn Thalabah who divorced his wife thrice with verses about separation being better than the stick, after her kin threatened him for harming her. > **""A man who speaks about what he has no knowledge of, or who conceals knowledge he possesses, is bolder than me.""**"
"10806","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: The Prophet ﷺ, would not recognize the end of a surah until Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim was revealed. When it was revealed, he knew the surah had been sealed and a new surah was begun. > **""One did not recognize the end of the surah until Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim was revealed.""**"
"10807","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: He came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, after he had passed water. He greeted three times, saying peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, but received no reply because of impurity. The Messenger ﷺ walked to his lodging while Jabir went to the mosque and sat grieved. Then the Messenger came out after purification and returned the greeting thrice, saying peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, and asked, shall I not inform you, O Abdullah ibn Jabir, of the best surah in the Quran. Jabir said, yes, O Messenger of Allah. He told him to recite All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, until he completed it. > **""Shall I not inform you, O Abdullah ibn Jabir, of the best surah in the Quran? Recite: All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds.""**"
"10808","Abu Zayd, who was a Companion, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: He was with the Prophet ﷺ, in one of the paths of Madinah when they heard a man praying Tahajjud and reciting Umm al-Quran. The Prophet stood and listened until the man completed it, then said there is nothing in the Quran like it. > **""There is nothing in the Quran like it.""**"
"10809","Abdullah ibn Shaqiq narrated from one who heard the Prophet ﷺ: While the Prophet was in Wadi al-Qura on his horse, a man from Balqayn asked him, who are these. He said, those who earned anger, pointing to the Jews. The man asked again, who are these. He said, the astray, meaning the Christians. Then another man came and said, your freedman or servant so-and-so was martyred. He said, rather he is being dragged to the Fire because of a cloak he stole from spoils. > **""They are those who earned anger. They are the astray. Rather he is being dragged to the Fire because of a cloak he stole.""**"
"10810","A narrator by the same chain narrated: A man from Balqayn asked, O Messenger of Allah, who are those who earned anger, and the Prophet ﷺ, pointed to the Jews, then mentioned the rest similarly. > **""Those who earned anger are the Jews.""**"
"10811","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: He recited before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Maliki Yawm al-Din with an alif, and Ghayri al-Maghdubi alayhim with a kasrah on the ghayn. > **""Maliki Yawm al-Din, Ghayri al-Maghdubi alayhim.""**"
"10812","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding the verse: We have given you seven of the oft-repeated: He said it is Umm al-Kitab, the Opening of the Book. > **""It is Umm al-Kitab.""**"
"10813","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Iblis wailed when the Fatihah of the Book was revealed, and it was revealed in Madinah. > **""Iblis wailed when Fatihah was revealed.""**"
"10814","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said there is no newborn born except that five verses of Fatihah al-Kitab are written in the joints of his head. > **""There is no newborn born except five verses of Fatihah al-Kitab are written in the joints of his head.""**"
"10815","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said whoever recites Umm al-Quran and Say He is Allah, One, it is as if he recited a third of the Quran. > **""Whoever recites Umm al-Quran and Say He is Allah, One, it is as if he recited a third of the Quran.""**"
"10816","Maqil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said al-Baqarah is the hump and peak of the Quran. With every verse of it eighty angels descended. Ayat al-Kursi was taken out from beneath the Throne and joined to Surah al-Baqarah. Yasin is the heart of the Quran. No one recites it seeking Allah and the Hereafter except he is forgiven, so recite it over your dead. > **""Al-Baqarah is the hump of the Quran and its peak. Yasin is the heart of the Quran. No one recites it seeking Allah and the Hereafter except he is forgiven, so recite it over your dead.""**"
"10817","Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said everything has a hump, and the hump of the Quran is Surah al-Baqarah. Whoever recites it in his house at night, Satan will not enter it for three nights. Whoever recites it in his house by day, Satan will not enter it for three days. > **""Everything has a hump, and the hump of the Quran is Surah al-Baqarah. Whoever recites it in his house at night, Satan will not enter it for three nights, and whoever recites it by day, Satan will not enter it for three days.""**"
"10818","Abdullah ibn Mughaffal, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said the house in which Surah al-Baqarah is recited, Satan does not enter it that night. > **""The house in which Surah al-Baqarah is recited, Satan does not enter it that night.""**"
"10819","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Prophet ﷺ, would say he was given these verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah from a treasure beneath the Throne, which no prophet before him was given. > **""I was given these verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah from a treasure beneath the Throne which no prophet before me was given.""**"
"10820","Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said to him, recite the two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, for I was given them from beneath the Throne. > **""Recite the two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, for I was given them from beneath the Throne.""**"
"10821","Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: He heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying upon the pulpit, recite the two verses, then mentioned similarly about the end of al-Baqarah given from beneath the Throne. > **""Recite the two verses.""**"
"10822","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said he was given two verses from a treasure of a house from beneath the Throne, which no prophet before him was given, meaning the two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah. > **""I was given two verses from a treasure from beneath the Throne which no prophet before me was given.""**"
"10823","A narrator narrated in another wording: He was given the closing verses of Surah al-Baqarah from a house of treasure. > **""I was given the closing verses of Surah al-Baqarah from a house.""**"
"10824","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever recites the end of Surah al-Baqarah in a night has done much and well. > **""Whoever recites the end of Surah al-Baqarah in a night has done much and well.""**"
"10825","Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said Allah wrote a Book two thousand years before creating the heavens and the earth, and sent down from it two verses with which He sealed Surah al-Baqarah. They are not recited in a house for three nights without Satan approaching it. > **""Allah wrote a Book two thousand years before creating the heavens and the earth, and sent down from it two verses with which He sealed Surah al-Baqarah. They are not recited in a house for three nights but Satan will not approach it.""**"
"10826","Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Repeat often the two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, from The Messenger ﷺ believes to its end, for Allah chose Muhammad ﷺ, specially with them. > **""Repeat often the two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, for Allah chose Muhammad specially with them.""**"
"10827","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said, let me not find one of you placing one leg upon the other then singing, while abandoning recitation of Surah al-Baqarah. > **""Let me not find one of you placing one leg upon the other then singing, while abandoning recitation of Surah al-Baqarah.""**"
"10828","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said learn the two radiant ones, al-Baqarah and Al Imran, for they will come on the Day of Resurrection as if two clouds, or two shades, or two flocks of birds in ranks, arguing on behalf of their companion. Learn al-Baqarah, for taking it is blessing, leaving it is regret, and the sorcerers cannot master it. > **""Learn the two radiant ones, al-Baqarah and Al Imran, for they will come on the Day of Resurrection as two clouds arguing for their companion. Learn al-Baqarah, for taking it is blessing, leaving it is regret, and the sorcerers cannot master it.""**"
"10829","A narrator said: Al-Tabarani also narrated similar from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, about the two radiant surahs. > **""Learn the two radiant ones.""**"
"10830","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said, let me not find one of you placing one leg upon the other, singing, while abandoning recitation of Surah al-Baqarah. > **""Let me not find one of you placing one leg upon the other, singing, while abandoning recitation of Surah al-Baqarah.""**"
"10831","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: As a downpour from the sky: He said al-Sayyib means rain. > **""Al-Sayyib means rain.""**"
"10832","Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ, saying: When Allah sent Adam down to earth, the angels said, O Lord, will You place therein one who corrupts and sheds blood while we glorify You with praise and sanctify You. He said, I know what you do not know. They said, O Lord, we are more obedient to You than the children of Adam. Allah told the angels to present two angels to be sent down to earth to see how they act. They named Harut and Marut. They were sent down, and al-Zahrah was presented to them as a most beautiful woman. They asked her for herself. She said, not until you utter this word of polytheism. They refused to associate with Allah. She left, then returned carrying a boy and demanded they kill him, and they refused. She left, then returned with a cup of wine and demanded they drink it. They drank, became intoxicated, committed adultery with her and killed the boy. When they awoke she said, by Allah, you did everything you had refused once intoxicated. They were given choice between punishment of this world and the Hereafter, and chose punishment of this world. > **""I know what you do not know. Bring forth two angels to be sent to earth to see how they act.""**"
"10833","Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said regarding His saying: And say Hittah: They said red wheat containing barley, and that is His saying: But those who wronged changed the word to other than what was said to them. > **""They said red wheat containing barley.""**"
"10834","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Prophet ﷺ: He said if Bani Israil had taken the lowest sufficient cow it would have sufficed them. > **""If Bani Israil had taken the lowest cow it would have sufficed them.""**"
"10835","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: Abu Jahl said, if I see Muhammad praying I will step on his neck. It was said, there he is. He said, I do not see him. The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said, had he done so the angels would have seized him openly, and had the Jews wished for death they would have died. > **""Had he done so the angels would have seized him openly, and had the Jews wished for death they would have died.""**"
"10836","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: The Jews would say this world is seven thousand years, and we will be punished for each thousand years one day in the Fire, only seven counted days. So Allah revealed: They say the Fire will touch us only for counted days, until His saying: they will abide therein eternally. > **""They say the Fire will touch us only for counted days.""**"
"10837","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: A group of Jews came to the Prophet ﷺ, saying, O Abu al-Qasim, tell us about traits only a prophet knows. He said, ask whatever you wish, but give me the pledge of Allah and what Yaqub took from his sons, that if I tell you and you recognize it, you will pledge allegiance to me. They agreed. They asked: what food did Israil forbid upon himself before the Torah was revealed, how is the man's fluid distinct from the woman's fluid and how come male and female, how is this unlettered Prophet in sleep, and who is his ally among angels. He took their covenant, then asked them by the One Who revealed the Torah to Musa whether they knew Israil fell severely ill and vowed if healed to forbid his most beloved food and drink, camel meat and camel milk. They said yes. He said, O Allah, bear witness. Then he asked by Allah besides Whom none is worshipped whether they knew the man's fluid is thick and the woman's fluid thin yellow, and whichever prevails the child and resemblance follow by Allah's leave, male if the man's prevails, female if the woman's prevails. They said yes. He said, O Allah, bear witness. Then he asked by the One Who revealed the Torah whether they knew this unlettered Prophet's eyes sleep but his heart does not sleep. They said yes. He said, O Allah, bear witness against them. They then said, tell us who your ally among angels is, upon that we will stay or part. He said, my ally is Jibril, and Allah never sent a prophet except Jibril was his ally. They said, here we part from you; had your ally been other than him we would have followed and believed you. He asked what prevents belief. They said, he is our enemy. Upon that Allah revealed: Whoever is an enemy to Jibril, for he brought it down upon your heart by Allah's leave, confirming what came before, a guidance and glad tidings for believers, and the following verses through chapter al-Baqarah verses 97 to 101, and they returned bearing anger upon anger. > **""Ask whatever you wish, but give me the pledge of Allah that if I tell you and you recognize it, you will pledge allegiance to me. My ally is Jibril, and Allah never sent a prophet except he was his ally.""**"
"10838","Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: Two men of the Ansar would recite a surah taught to them by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. One night they stood praying and could not recall a letter of it. In the morning they came to the Messenger and mentioned it. He said, it is among what was abrogated or caused to be forgotten, so turn away from it. Al-Zuhri would recite: Whatever verse We abrogate or cause to be forgotten, with dammah on the nun. > **""It is among what was abrogated or caused to be forgotten, so turn away from it.""**"
"10839","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Ibrahim restricted his supplication excluding others, so Allah revealed: And whoever disbelieves, meaning I shall also provide them as I provide believers. Shall I create creation and not provide them. I grant them enjoyment a little then compel them to the punishment of Fire. Then Ibn Abbas recited: Each, these and these, We extend from the gift of your Lord, and the gift of your Lord is not restricted. > **""I shall provide them as I provide believers. I grant them enjoyment a little then compel them to the punishment of Fire.""**"
"10840","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Prophet ﷺ, regarding His saying: Thus We made you a middle nation: He said it means just. > **""Just.""**"
"10841","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Messenger of Allah, if only we took from the standing place of Ibrahim a place of prayer. Then was revealed: Take from the standing place of Ibrahim a place of prayer. > **""If only we took from the standing place of Ibrahim a place of prayer.""**"
"10842","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: We shall turn you to a qiblah you are pleased with: He said it is toward the spout of the Kabah. > **""It is toward the spout of the Kabah.""**"
"10843","Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said regarding giving wealth out of love for it: It is to give while you are healthy and miserly, hoping for life and fearing poverty. > **""Give while you are healthy and miserly, hoping for life and fearing poverty.""**"
"10844","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: Then following with kindness and payment to him with excellence: Among Bani Israil, when one was deliberately killed, only retaliation was lawful for them. Blood money was made lawful for this nation. This one was commanded to follow with kindness, and this one to pay with excellence. That is a lightening from your Lord. > **""Follow with kindness and pay with excellence. That is a lightening from your Lord.""**"
"10845","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was asked about His sayings: The month of Ramadan in which the Quran was revealed, and: We sent it down on a blessed night: He said it was sent down in Ramadan on Laylat al-Qadr, the blessed night, all at once, then sent down at the stations of the stars gradually over months and days. > **""It was sent down all at once on Laylat al-Qadr, then gradually over months and days.""**"
"10846","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: Those who, when calamity strikes them, say indeed we belong to Allah and to Him we return, upon them are blessings from their Lord and mercy, and they are guided: Allah informed that when a believing servant submits to Allah's decree and returns, saying the retrieval phrase at calamity, three good traits are written for him: prayer from Allah, mercy, and realization of guidance. The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said, whoever says the retrieval phrase at calamity, Allah will mend his calamity, grant him a good outcome, and give him a replacement he is pleased with. > **""Whoever says the retrieval phrase at calamity, Allah will mend his calamity, grant him a good outcome, and give him a replacement he is pleased with.""**"
"10847","Kab ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: In Ramadan, when a man fasted and evening came and he slept, food, drink and women became forbidden until the next breaking. Umar returned one night from the Prophet ﷺ, after conversing late, found his wife asleep, desired her, she said I slept, he said you did not sleep, then he approached her. Kab ibn Malik did likewise. Umar went to the Prophet in the morning and informed him, so Allah revealed: Allah knows you used to betray yourselves, so He turned to you and pardoned you. > **""Allah knows you used to betray yourselves, so He turned to you and pardoned you.""**"
"10848","Abu Jubayrah ibn al-Dahhak, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Ansar would give charity freely as Allah willed, then a year of drought struck and they withheld. So Allah revealed: Do not cast yourselves with your hands into destruction, and do good, indeed Allah loves the doers of good. > **""Do not cast yourselves with your hands into destruction, and do good, indeed Allah loves the doers of good.""**"
"10849","Al-Numan ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: Do not cast yourselves with your hands into destruction: A man would commit a sin and say Allah will not forgive me. So Allah revealed it along with: Do good, indeed Allah loves the doers of good. > **""Do not cast yourselves with your hands into destruction, and do good, indeed Allah loves the doers of good.""**"
"10850","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: Hajj is known months: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said they are Dhul Qadah and Dhul Hijjah. Regarding: Whoever imposes Hajj therein, Ibn Umar said it means Talbiyah and Ihram. La rafath means intercourse with women. La fusuq means abuse. La jidal means disputing. > **""Dhul Qadah and Dhul Hijjah.""**"
"10851","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said regarding His saying: Hajj is known months, they are Shawwal, Dhul Qadah and Dhul Hijjah. > **""They are Shawwal, Dhul Qadah and Dhul Hijjah.""**"
"10852","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: The Messenger ﷺ of Allah ﷺ, said regarding: No rafath, no fusuq, no jidal in Hajj, that rafath is explicit speech and exposure to women for intercourse, fusuq is sins, and jidal is a man disputing his companion. > **""Rafath is explicit speech and exposure to women for intercourse, fusuq is sins, and jidal is a man disputing his companion.""**"
"10853","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: No rafath means intercourse. No fusuq means sins. No jidal in Hajj means disputing. > **""No rafath means intercourse, no fusuq means sins, no jidal means disputing.""**"
"10854","Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: People used to rely upon one another for provisions, so Allah commanded them to take provisions, saying: Take provisions, indeed the best provision is piety. > **""Take provisions, indeed the best provision is piety.""**"
"10855","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said regarding His saying: Whoever hastens in two days there is no sin upon him, and whoever delays there is no sin upon him: He said it means forgiven. > **""Forgiven.""**"
"10856","Ibn Jurayj said regarding His saying: Among people is one who sells himself seeking Allah's pleasure: It was revealed about Suhayb ibn Sinan and Abu Dharr, and the one who met Suhayb on the road to Madinah, Qunfudh ibn Umayr ibn Judan. > **""It was revealed about Suhayb ibn Sinan and Abu Dharr.""**"
"10857","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: Mankind was one nation: They were all upon Islam. Al-Kalbi said it means they were all upon disbelief. > **""They were all upon Islam.""**"
"10858","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: Between Adam and Nuh were ten generations, all upon a law of truth. When Allah sent the Prophet ﷺ, and revealed His Book, He said: Mankind was one nation. > **""Between Adam and Nuh were ten generations, all upon a law of truth.""**"
"10859","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding: They ask you what they spend, say al-Afw: It means surplus beyond dependents. > **""Surplus beyond dependents.""**"
"10860","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: The verse: Your wives are a tilth for you, was revealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, as a permission for approaching from behind in the front passage. > **""Your wives are a tilth for you.""**"
"10861","Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: A man turned his wife over in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and people said so-and-so turned his wife over. So Allah revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish. > **""Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish.""**"
"10862","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: A man approached his wife in her back passage in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and people disapproved. So Allah revealed: Your wives are a tilth for you. > **""Your wives are a tilth for you.""**"
"10863","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: Umar came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined. He asked, what ruined you. He said, I turned my saddle last night. The Prophet gave no reply until revelation came: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish, meaning approach from front or back, but avoid menstruation and the back passage. > **""Your wives are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish. Approach from front or back, but avoid menstruation and the back passage.""**"
"10864","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated: This verse: Your wives are a tilth for you, was revealed about some Ansar who came to the Prophet ﷺ, and asked him. The Messenger ﷺ said, approach her in any manner when it is in the front passage. > **""Approach her in any manner when it is in the front passage.""**"
"10865","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, regarding His saying: They ask you about menstruation, say it is harm, so keep away from women in menstruation: People said the Jews claimed whoever approaches his wife from behind will have a cross-eyed child. The Ansari women would not let husbands approach them from behind. They came to the Messenger and asked about approaching a menstruating wife, so Allah revealed the verses on menstruation until purification by washing, then: Come to them as Allah commanded, indeed Allah loves the repentant and the purified, and: Your wives are a tilth for you, so come as you wish, meaning tilth is where a child is produced. > **""Tilth is only where a child is produced.""**"
"10866","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from the Prophet ﷺ: He said regarding: The one in whose hand is the marriage tie, it is the husband. > **""The one in whose hand is the marriage tie is the husband.""**"
"10867","Amr ibn Rafi, freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, narrated: He used to write copies of the Quran in the time of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ. Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, asked him to write a copy, saying when you reach this verse of al-Baqarah do not write it until you bring it to me so I dictate as I memorized from the Messenger. When he reached it he brought the sheet, and she said, write: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, the Asr prayer, and stand for Allah devoutly. > **""Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, the Asr prayer, and stand for Allah devoutly.""**"
"10868","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: He said every place in the Quran where qunut is mentioned means obedience. > **""Every place in the Quran where qunut is mentioned means obedience.""**"
"10869","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: And stand for Allah devoutly: They used to speak in prayer; a man's servant would come while he prayed and he would speak to him about his need, so they were forbidden from speech. > **""They were forbidden from speech in prayer.""**"
"10870","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: When was revealed: Who is it that will lend Allah a good loan, Abu al-Dahdah said, O Messenger of Allah, does Allah seek a loan from us. He said, yes, O Abu al-Dahdah. He said, I have lent my Lord a garden with six hundred date palms. Then he walked to the garden where Umm al-Dahdah was with his family, called her, she answered, and he said, come out, for I have lent my Lord a garden with six hundred date palms. > **""Yes, O Abu al-Dahdah. I have lent my Lord a garden with six hundred date palms. Come out, for I have lent my Lord a garden with six hundred date palms.""**"
"10871","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Prophet ﷺ: He said al-Sakinah is a restraining wind. > **""Al-Sakinah is a restraining wind.""**"
"10872","Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Prophet ﷺ, asked him which verse in the Book of Allah is greatest. He said Allah and His Messenger know best. He repeated it, then Ubayy said Ayat al-Kursi. He said, let knowledge delight you, O Abu al-Mundhir, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it has a tongue and two lips sanctifying the Sovereign near the leg of the Throne. > **""Let knowledge delight you, O Abu al-Mundhir. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it has a tongue and two lips sanctifying the Sovereign near the leg of the Throne.""**"
"10873","Abu al-Salil narrated: A Companion would narrate abundantly to people, then climbed a roof to narrate. He said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, asked, which verse in the Quran is greatest. A man said: Allah, none is worshipped but He, the Living, the Sustainer. He placed his hand between my shoulders until I felt its coolness between my breasts, or between my breasts until I felt it between my shoulders, and said, delight, O Abu al-Mundhir. > **""Which verse in the Quran is greatest? Delight, O Abu al-Mundhir.""**"
"10874","Al-Asqa al-Bakri, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Prophet ﷺ, came to them in the Suffah of the Muhajirun, and a man asked which verse in the Quran is greatest. The Prophet said: Allah, none is worshipped but He, the Living, the Sustainer, no slumber nor sleep overtakes Him, until the verse ended. > **""Allah, none is worshipped but He, the Living, the Sustainer, no slumber nor sleep overtakes Him.""**"
"10875","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: It reached him that Muadh ibn Jabal seized Satan in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He went to Muadh and asked. Muadh said yes, the Messenger entrusted charity dates to him, he placed them in his room, found daily decrease, and complained. The Messenger ﷺ said, it is Satan's work, so watch for him. He watched at night; after part of the night one came in the form of an elephant, then entered through the door crack in another form, approached the dates and began stuffing them. Muadh tightened his clothes, seized him, testified there is none worshipped but Allah and Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, and said, O enemy of Allah, you pounced upon charity dates more deserving for others, I will raise you to the Messenger to disgrace you. He pledged not to return. In the morning the Messenger asked, what did your captive do. Muadh said he pledged not to return. He said, he will return, so watch. The second night the same happened and Muadh released him on pledge. The next morning the crier called where is Muadh. The Messenger ﷺ again said he will return. The third night the same happened, and Muadh said, O enemy of Allah, you pledged twice and this is third, I will raise you to disgrace you. He said, I am a devil with dependents, I came only from Nasibayn, had I found anything else I would not have come. We were in this city until your companion was sent, then two verses descended driving us out to Nasibayn. They are not recited in a house except Satan does not enter it for three nights. If you free me I will teach them. Muadh agreed. He said, Ayat al-Kursi and the seal of al-Baqarah from The Messenger ﷺ believes to its end. Muadh freed him, informed the Messenger in the morning, and the Messenger said, the wicked one spoke truthfully though he is a liar. Thereafter Muadh recited them and found no decrease. > **""It is Satan's work, so watch for him. What did your captive do? He will return, so watch. The wicked one spoke truthfully though he is a liar.""**"
"10876","Malik ibn Hamzah ibn Abi Usayd narrated from his father from his grandfather Abu Usayd al-Saidi al-Khazraji, may Allah be pleased with him: He had a well in Madinah called Bir Budaah in which the Prophet ﷺ, had spat, so it was blessed. When Abu Usayd harvested his garden dates he placed them in a room, but a ghul would come to his storeroom stealing and spoiling them. He complained to the Messenger who said, it is the ghul, O Abu Usayd, listen for her, and when you hear her intrusion say, in the Name of Allah, answer the Messenger of Allah. She said, O Abu Usayd, excuse me from being made to go to the Messenger, and I will give you a pledge by Allah never to come to your house nor steal your dates, and guide you to a verse to recite in your house so none comes to your family, and over your vessel so none uncovers it. He accepted the pledge. She said the verse is Ayat al-Kursi, then passed wind. He came to the Prophet and told the story, and the Prophet said, she spoke truthfully though she is a liar. > **""In the Name of Allah, answer the Messenger of Allah. She spoke truthfully though she is a liar.""**"
"10877","Al-Shabi narrated: Masruq and Shutayr ibn Shakl sat in the great mosque, people gathered to them. Shutayr said to Masruq, they came for us to narrate, so either you narrate and I confirm, or I narrate and you confirm. Masruq said, narrate and I will confirm. Shutayr said, Abdullah ibn Masud narrated to us that the greatest verse in the Book of Allah is: Allah, none is worshipped but He, the Living, the Sustainer, to the end of the verse. Masruq said, you spoke truthfully. > **""The greatest verse in the Book of Allah is: Allah, none is worshipped but He, the Living, the Sustainer. You spoke truthfully.""**"
"10878","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding His saying: His Kursi extends over the heavens and the earth: It is the place of the Feet, and none estimates the measure of His Throne except Allah. > **""It is the place of the Feet, and none estimates the measure of His Throne except Allah.""**"
"10879","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse in al-Baqarah about Allah being the Guardian of those who believe, bringing them from darkness to light, and the following verse about the allies of disbelievers being taghut. He said the first refers to a people who had disbelieved in Isa عليه السلام then believed in Muhammad ﷺ, and the second refers to a people who believed in Isa عليه السلام but disbelieved in Muhammad ﷺ when he was sent."
"10880","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the statement in al-Baqarah, look at your food and drink, it has not rotted. He said it means it had not changed or decayed with the passage of time."
"10881","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the phrase in al-Baqarah about a whirlwind containing fire which burned the garden. He said al-isar means a violent, severe wind."
"10882","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said the Companions disliked giving charity to their polytheist relatives, so they asked about it and permission was granted. Then the verse in al-Baqarah was revealed, guidance is not upon you but Allah guides whom He wills, and whatever good you spend is for yourselves, up to His statement, and you shall not be wronged."
"10883","Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Urayb رضي الله عنه reported from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the verse in al-Baqarah about those who spend their wealth by night and day, secretly and openly. > **""It was revealed concerning spending on horses.""**"
"10884","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said the verse in al-Baqarah about those who spend their wealth by night and day, secretly and openly, was revealed concerning Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه. He possessed four dirhams, so he spent one by night, one by day, one secretly, and one openly."
"10885","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning the verse in al-Baqarah, fear a Day in which you will be returned to Allah, that it was the last verse revealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"10886","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say: > **""I was given these verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah from a treasure beneath the Throne; no Prophet before me was given them.""**"
"10887","Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Adam reported that Abu al-Darda, Abu Umamah, Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, and Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنهم said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked who are those firmly rooted in knowledge. He ﷺ said: > **""He whose oath is dutiful, whose tongue is truthful, whose private parts and stomach are chaste — such is the one firmly rooted in knowledge.""**"
"10888","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ often said in supplication: > **""O Allah, Turner of hearts, make my heart firm upon Your religion.""** She asked, O Messenger of Allah, do hearts really turn. He ﷺ said: > **""Yes. There is no human among the children of Adam except that his heart is between two of the fingers of Allah, Exalted is He. If Allah wills He keeps it upright, and if He wills He causes it to deviate. So we ask Allah our Lord not to cause our hearts to deviate after He has guided us, and we ask Him to grant us mercy from Himself. Indeed He is the Bestower.""** She then asked for a supplication for herself, and he ﷺ said: > **""Say: O Allah, Lord of the Prophet, forgive my sin, remove the rancor of my heart, and protect me from misleading trials as long as You keep us alive.""**"
"10889","Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam رضي الله عنه said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite the verse in Al Imran, Allah testifies there is no deity but He, and the angels and people of knowledge, upholding justice, there is no deity but He, the Mighty, the Wise. Then he ﷺ said: > **""And I am among the witnesses to that, O my Lord.""** In another wording, when he ﷺ recited it he said: > **""And I bear witness that there is no deity but He, the Mighty, the Wise.""**"
"10890","Ghalib al-Qattan said he came to Kufah for trade and lodged near al-Amash. At night al-Amash rose for tahajjud and repeatedly recited the verses in Al Imran about Allah's testimony of unity and that the religion with Allah is Islam, saying, I bear witness to what Allah has testified to, and I entrust this testimony with Allah as a deposit with Allah. When asked about its merit, al-Amash waited a year then reported from Abu Wail, from Abdullah رضي الله عنه, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Its companion will be brought on the Day of Resurrection, and Allah the Exalted will say: My servant made a covenant with Me, and I am most entitled to fulfill a covenant. Admit My servant to Paradise.""**"
"10891","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the verse in Al Imran, to Him submits whoever is in the heavens and earth, willingly or unwillingly. > **""As for those in the heavens, they are the angels. As for those on earth, they are those born upon Islam. As for unwillingly, they are the captives of nations brought in chains and fetters, driven toward Paradise while they dislike it.""**"
"10892","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said the verse came to his mind, you shall not attain righteousness until you spend from what you love. He considered what Allah had granted him and found nothing dearer to him than Marjanah, his Roman slave-girl. So he declared her free for the sake of Allah, adding that were he to take back anything he had made for Allah he would have married her."
"10893","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه explained the verse in Al Imran, fear Allah His true fear. He said it means that He is obeyed and not disobeyed, thanked and not denied, remembered and not forgotten."
"10894","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه explained the verse in Al Imran, hold firmly all together to the rope of Allah. He said it means the Quran."
"10895","In another report concerning the same verse, hold to the rope of Allah, he said the rope of Allah means the united community."
"10896","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said this path is attended, devils attend it calling, O servants of Allah, this is the way. Hold to the rope of Allah, for the straight path is the Book of Allah."
"10897","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse in Al Imran, how can you disbelieve while the verses of Allah are recited to you and His Messenger is among you. He said Aws and Khazraj were recalling pre-Islamic days, became angry until war nearly broke out, seized weapons and advanced toward one another. Then the verse was revealed down to His statement, and He rescued you from it."
"10898","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse, you are the best nation brought forth for people, enjoining good and forbidding wrong. He said they are those who emigrated with Muhammad ﷺ."
"10899","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said when Abdullah ibn Salam, Thalabah ibn Shubah, Asad ibn Ubayd, and other Jews embraced Islam, believed, affirmed, and desired Islam, the unbelieving rabbis said none believed in Muhammad except our worst; had they been among our best they would not have left their fathers' religion. So Allah revealed concerning that, they are not alike, from the People of the Book, up to His statement, among the righteous."
"10900","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the verse in Al Imran, O you who believe, do not take intimate associates besides yourselves, they will spare you no ruin, hatred has appeared from their mouths and what their breasts conceal is greater. > **""They are the Khawarij.""**"
"10901","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning His statement, marked: > **""Marked means distinguished. The insignia of the angels on the day of Badr was black turbans, and on the day of Uhud red turbans.""**"
"10902","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked about His statement, and a Paradise whose expanse is the heavens and earth. He asked, then where is Hellfire. He ﷺ said: > **""Consider the night: it was, then there is nothing of it. So where is the day.""** The man replied, where Allah wills. He ﷺ said: > **""So too the Fire is where Allah wills.""**"
"10903","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه explained the verse, and how many a Prophet fought alongside whom were many devotees. He said they numbered in the thousands."
"10904","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said he had not thought any Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ desired this world, until there was revealed concerning them on the day of Uhud, among you are those who desire this world and among you are those who desire the Hereafter."
"10905","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه explained the verse, then He sent down upon you after grief security in slumber. He said slumber was cast upon them on the day of Uhud."
"10906","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, said slumber granting security during fighting is from Allah, Exalted is He, while slumber during prayer is from Satan."
"10907","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse, it is not for a Prophet to commit breach of trust. He said it means it is not for a Prophet that his Companions accuse him of suspicion."
"10908","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said the Prophet ﷺ sent an army whose banner was turned back, then sent again and it was turned back, then sent again and it returned having misappropriated the golden head of a young gazelle. Then the verse was revealed, it is not for a Prophet to commit breach of trust."
"10909","Masruq said they asked Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, about the verse, do not think those killed in the path of Allah are dead, up to they are provided for. He said the souls of martyrs are with Allah like green birds with lamps suspended from the Throne, roaming in Paradise wherever they wish. Their Lord looked upon them and said: > **""Do you desire anything so I may increase it for you.""** They replied, Our Lord, do we not roam in Paradise wherever we wish. He looked upon them a second time and a third time with the same question, until they said, return our souls to our bodies so we may fight in Your path and be killed once more. Then He remained silent toward them."
"10910","Said ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said when Hamzah رضي الله عنه and his companions were struck at Uhud they said, would that those behind us knew what reward Allah granted us, so they would be bolder. Allah said, I shall inform them, and revealed, do not think those killed in the path of Allah are dead."
"10911","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, explained the verse, what they withheld shall be collared upon them on the Day of Resurrection. He said he will be collared with a bald serpent with two spots over its mouth, pecking at his head. The man will say, what is between me and you. It will reply: > **""I am your wealth which you withheld.""**"
"10912","In another report from Abdullah رضي الله عنه, whoever possesses wealth and does not pay its zakah will be collared on the Day of Resurrection with a bald serpent pecking his head, saying: > **""I am your wealth over which you were miserly.""** This is the meaning of, what they withheld shall be collared upon them on the Day of Resurrection."
"10913","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said Quraysh went to the Jews and asked what miracles Musa عليه السلام brought; they said his staff and his shining white hand. They went to the Christians and asked about Isa عليه السلام; they said he healed the blind and leper and revived the dead. Then they came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him to invoke his Lord to turn Safa into gold for them. He supplicated, and the verse was revealed, indeed in the creation of heavens and earth and alternation of night and day are signs for people of understanding. Let them reflect upon it."
"10914","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه explained the verse, those who remember Allah standing, sitting, and upon their sides. He said if one cannot pray standing, then sitting, and if not, then reclining."
"10915","Abu Barzah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah will raise on the Day of Resurrection a people whose mouths blaze with fire.""** It was asked, who are they, O Messenger of Allah. He ﷺ said: > **""Have you not seen that Allah says: Indeed those who consume the wealth of orphans unjustly only consume fire in their bellies.""**"
"10916","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse in al-Nisa concerning women who commit indecency. He said they were confined in houses; if she died she died, if she lived she lived, until the verse in Surah al-Nur was revealed, flog each of them one hundred lashes, and the chapter of prescribed punishments came down. Whoever did such an act was flogged and released. This abrogated confinement."
"10917","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said when Surah al-Nisa was revealed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no confinement after Surah al-Nisa.""**"
"10918","A man of the Ansar said Abu Qays, among the righteous Ansar, died, and his son Qays proposed to his widow. She replied, I count you as a son and you are among the righteous of your people, but I shall consult the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She came to him ﷺ and told him of the death and proposal. He ﷺ spoke kindly to her then said: > **""Return to your house.""** Then the verse was revealed, do not marry those women whom your fathers married."
"10919","Razin al-Jurjani said he asked Said ibn Jubayr رحمه الله about the verse, married women, and he had no knowledge of it. He asked al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, who testified he heard Said ask Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما about it. Ibn Abbas said it was revealed on the day of Khaybar when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered it and Muslims found women of the People of the Book with husbands; when a man wished such a woman she would say, I have a husband. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, and Allah revealed, married women, meaning a captive from polytheists taken is lawful without harm. When this was mentioned to Said ibn Jubayr he said, he spoke truthfully."
"10920","Ali رضي الله عنه and Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه explained the verse, married women except those your right hands possess. Ali said polytheist women when captured become lawful to him, while Ibn Masud said both polytheist and Muslim women are covered by the ruling."
"10921","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه explained the verse, O you who believe, do not consume your wealth among yourselves unjustly except through mutually agreeable trade. He said it is decisive and was never abrogated."
"10922","Anas رضي الله عنه said they had seen nothing like what reached them from their Lord, Blessed and Exalted is He, then they did not forsake for Him family and wealth, for He overlooked for them what is lesser than major sins. Allah says, if you avoid the major sins you are forbidden, We shall expiate your misdeeds and admit you to a noble entrance."
"10923","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, was asked about major sins. He said they are what lies between the beginning of Surah al-Nisa up to about thirty verses."
"10924","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه explained the verse, and the companion by the side. He said it means the wife."
"10925","Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas رضي الله عنه said he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he ﷺ recited the verse, indeed Allah does not love the arrogant boaster, and spoke gravely of arrogance. Thabit wept. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: > **""What makes you weep.""** He replied, O Prophet of Allah, I love beauty so much that I even like the strap of my sandal to be handsome. He ﷺ said: > **""You are among the people of Paradise. Arrogance is not that your mount and saddle are handsome. Arrogance is to reject truth and despise people.""**"
"10926","Muhammad ibn Fadalah al-Zafari رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them in the mosque of Banu Zafar and sat upon the rock there today, with Abdullah ibn Masud, Muadh ibn Jabal, and others رضي الله عنهم. The Prophet ﷺ ordered a reciter who recited until he reached the verse, how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you as witness over these. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wept until his beard trembled and said: > **""O my Lord, I witnessed over those among whom I lived, so how about those I never saw.""**"
"10927","Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Labibah reported from his father, from his grandfather, that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the verse, how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you as witness over these, he ﷺ wept and said: > **""O my Lord, this I witnessed over those among whom I lived, so how about those I never saw.""**"
"10928","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse, do not say raina. He said people used to say to the Prophet ﷺ, lend us your ear, with raina like saying atina. Concerning, and hear, may you not be made to hear, addressed to the Prophet ﷺ, they meant may you not hear. And had they said, we heard and obeyed, and hear, and look upon us, it would have been better for them."
"10929","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said they withheld seeking forgiveness for people of major sins until they heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite, indeed Allah does not forgive association with Him but forgives less than that for whom He wills. He ﷺ said: > **""I have reserved my supplication as intercession for the people of major sins of my nation.""** So they refrained from much of what was in themselves, then spoke thereafter and hoped."
"10930","Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه said a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said his nephew persisted in unlawful acts. He ﷺ asked: > **""What is his religion.""** He replied, he affirms Allah's unity and prays. He ﷺ said: > **""Then ask him for his religion as a gift, and if he refuses, buy it from him.""** The man asked his nephew who refused, and returned saying he found him miserly over his religion. Then the verse was revealed, indeed Allah does not forgive association with Him but forgives less than that for whom He wills."
"10931","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said Huyayy ibn Akhtab and Kab ibn al-Ashraf came to Makkah and allied with them to fight the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Makkans asked them as people of ancient knowledge and the first Book to judge between them and Muhammad. They asked about each party; the Makkans described slaughtering noble camels, giving milk over water, freeing captives, watering pilgrims, and upholding kinship, while describing Muhammad ﷺ as cut off without progeny who severed kinship and was followed by thieves of pilgrims from Ghifar. So the two Jews declared them better guided than him. Allah then revealed, have you not seen those given a portion of the Book believing in idols and taghut."
"10932","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said, we are the people to the exclusion of others, concerning envy toward what Allah granted of His bounty."
"10933","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said the verse was recited before Umar رضي الله عنه, whenever their skins mature We shall replace them with other skins. Umar asked for repetition, and Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said, I have its explanation: > **""It will be changed a hundred times in every hour.""** Umar said, thus did I hear from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"10934","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said Abu Barzah al-Aslami judged among Jews in their disputes, then some Muslims brought disputes to him. Allah then revealed, have you not seen those who claim belief in what was revealed to you and before you, wishing to seek judgment from taghut though commanded to disbelieve in it, up to His statement, we intended only goodness and accord."
"10935","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها said al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه disputed with a man before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who judged for al-Zubayr. The man remarked he judged for him only because he is his aunt's son. Then the verse was revealed, no, by your Lord, they shall not believe."
"10936","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, I love you so much that I remember you, and were I not to come and look at you I feel my soul would depart. Then I recall that if I enter Paradise I shall be below you in rank, and that distresses me, and I wish to be with you in rank. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave no reply, so Allah revealed, whoever obeys Allah and the Messenger shall be with those Allah favored among Prophets. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then called him and recited it to him."
"10937","Aishah رضي الله عنها said a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, you are dearer to me than myself, my family, my wealth, and my child. When I am at home and remember you I cannot be patient until I come and look at you. When I recall my death and yours, I know that when you enter Paradise you will be raised with the Prophets, while if I enter I fear I shall not see you. The Prophet ﷺ gave no reply until Jibril عليه السلام descended with the verse, whoever obeys Allah and the Messenger shall be with those Allah favored among Prophets, the truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous."
"10938","Al-Hasan رحمه الله explained the verse, when you are greeted with a greeting, greet with better than it. He said this applies to people of Islam, while return it applies to people of polytheism."
"10939","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه said some Arabs came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah, embraced Islam, then suffered Madinah's fever, apostatized and left. Some Companions met them and asked why they returned; they said Madinah's climate did not suit them. The Companions said, is there not a good example for you in the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Some said they turned hypocrite, others said they remained Muslims. Then Allah revealed, what is with you that you are two groups concerning the hypocrites, while Allah cast them back for what they earned."
"10940","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse, if he is from an enemy people while he is a believer, then freeing a believing slave. He said a man would come to the Prophet ﷺ, accept Islam, then return to his polytheist people and be killed in a raid, so his killer must free a slave. Concerning, if he is from a people with whom you have a covenant, it refers to a covenanted man whose people hold a treaty, so blood-money is paid to them and his killer frees a slave."
"10941","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning His statement, whoever kills a believer intentionally, his recompense is Hell: > **""If He recompenses him.""**"
"10942","Abdullah ibn Abi Hadrad رضي الله عنه said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent them to Idam with Abu Qatadah al-Harith ibn Ribi and Muhallim ibn Jaththamah رضي الله عنهم. At the valley of Idam, Amir ibn al-Adbat al-Ashjai passed on his camel with provisions and milk, greeted them, but they held back from him while Muhallim attacked and killed him over a prior quarrel, taking his camel and goods. When they returned and informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the Quran was revealed concerning them, O you who believe, when you travel in Allah's path verify, and do not say to one who offers you peace you are not a believer, seeking worldly gain, for with Allah are many spoils, thus were you before and Allah favored you, so verify, indeed Allah is aware of what you do."
"10943","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a detachment including al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad رضي الله عنه. They found the enemy dispersed except one wealthy man who declared, I bear witness there is no deity but Allah. Al-Miqdad attacked and killed him. A Companion protested that he killed a man professing unity and would report it to the Prophet ﷺ. When they returned the Prophet ﷺ summoned al-Miqdad and said: > **""O Miqdad, did you kill a man who says there is no deity but Allah. How will you fare with there is no deity but Allah tomorrow.""** Then Allah revealed the verse about verifying when traveling in Allah's path. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-Miqdad: > **""He was a believer concealing his faith among disbelievers, then he declared it and you killed him. Likewise you once concealed your faith in Makkah.""**"
"10944","Al-Falatan ibn Asim رضي الله عنه said they were with the Prophet ﷺ when revelation descended; his gaze fixed, eyes open, hearing and heart devoted to what came from Allah, which they recognized. He ﷺ told the scribe: > **""Write: Not equal are those who remain among believers and those who strive in Allah's path.""** A blind man stood and asked, O Messenger of Allah, what is our fault in what Allah sent down. Fearing something about him had descended, he stood repeating, I seek refuge from the anger of the Messenger of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ told the scribe: > **""Write: Except those with disability.""** In another wording he stood saying, I repent to Allah."
"10945","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the verse, not equal are those who remain among believers, except those with disability. He said they were people in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who did not campaign with him due to illnesses and pains, alongside healthy others who also stayed back, so the sick were excused unlike the healthy."
"10946","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه said when the verse was revealed, not equal are those who remain among believers and those who strive in Allah's path, Ibn Umm Maktum رضي الله عنه came and asked, O Messenger of Allah, is there no license for me. The Prophet ﷺ said no. Ibn Umm Maktum supplicated, O Allah, I am blind so grant me license. Then Allah revealed, except those with disability, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered it written."
"10947","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said when the verse was revealed, indeed those whom angels take while wronging themselves, some people in Makkah had accepted Islam but disliked emigrating after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emigrated to Madinah out of fear. Allah then revealed it down to His statement, except the oppressed."
"10948","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said some Makkans had accepted Islam secretly; when polytheists marched to Badr they forced them out, and some were struck down with the polytheists. Muslims said they were Muslim brothers forced out, so seek forgiveness for them, and the verse was revealed, indeed those whom angels take while wronging themselves. Muslims wrote this verse to those remaining in Makkah, who set out but were overtaken on the road and turned back by polytheists. Then the verse was revealed, among people are those who say we believe in Allah, but when harmed in Allah's path they treat human trial like Allah's punishment. Muslims wrote that to them and they grieved, then the verse was revealed, then indeed your Lord to those who emigrated after trial, strove and persevered, your Lord after that is Forgiving, Merciful. They wrote that to them as well."
"10949","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said Damrah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه left his house emigrating and told his family, carry me out of polytheist land to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He died on the road before reaching the Prophet ﷺ, so revelation descended, whoever leaves his house emigrating to Allah and His Messenger then death overtakes him, up to, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful."
"10950","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ that when he ﷺ sat with them and wished to rise he would leave his sandals or garment. Once he rose leaving his sandals, and Abu al-Darda followed with water but he ﷺ returned without relieving himself. Asked about his need, he ﷺ said: > **""Yes, but a comer from my Lord came to me with: Whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness shall find Allah Forgiving, Merciful. The previous verse, whoever does evil shall be recompensed for it, had weighed upon me, so I wished to give my Companions glad tidings.""** Abu al-Darda asked, O Messenger of Allah, even if he committed adultery and theft then sought forgiveness, will he be forgiven. He ﷺ said: > **""Yes.""** He repeated thrice, and he ﷺ said: > **""Despite the nose of Abu al-Darda.""** The narrator later saw Abu al-Darda touching his nose with his finger."
"10951","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said when a man of the Children of Israel sinned, by morning it was written on his door, you committed such and such, and its expiation is such an act, perhaps to make him deem it grave and perform it. Ibn Masud said he would not love that Allah had given them that instead of this verse, whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness shall find Allah Forgiving, Merciful."
"10952","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said in the Book of Allah are two verses: no servant commits a sin then recites them and seeks Allah's forgiveness except He forgives him: those who when they commit indecency or wrong themselves remember Allah and seek forgiveness for their sins, and none forgives sins but Allah; and whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness shall find Allah Forgiving, Merciful."
"10953","Ibrahim reported that Abdullah رضي الله عنه said in the Quran are two verses: no servant sins then recites them and seeks Allah's forgiveness except He forgives him. Asked which they were, he did not inform them. Alqamah and al-Aswad رحمهما الله rose to the house, took the mushaf, leafed through al-Baqarah without finding them, then through al-Nisa until they reached, whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness shall find Allah Forgiving, Merciful, and said, this is one. Then they leafed through Al Imran until they reached, those who when they commit indecency or wrong themselves remember Allah and seek forgiveness for their sins, and none forgives sins but Allah, and do not persist knowingly, and said, this is the other. They closed the mushaf, returned to Abdullah and asked, are these the two verses. He said, yes."
"10954","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that in Surat al-Nisa there are five verses dearer to him than the world and all it contains, well known to scholars: avoidance of major sins bringing expiation and noble entry, Allah not wronging even an atom's weight and multiplying good, Allah forgiving all but shirk, acceptance of those who come seeking forgiveness with the Messenger's intercession, and whoever does evil then seeks forgiveness finding Allah Forgiving, Merciful."
"10955","Ubayy ibn Kab رضي الله عنه said concerning His saying {They call upon nothing besides Him but females} [An-Nisa: 117] that with every idol is its female jinn companion."
"10956","Aminah asked Aishah رضي الله عنها, wife of the Messenger ﷺ, about His saying {Whoever does evil will be recompensed for it} [An-Nisa: 123]. Aishah said none had asked her since she asked the Messenger ﷺ, who explained it as Allah's reckoning of the servant through fever, mishap, thorn-prick, even lost goods causing alarm then found, until the believer emerges from sins as purified as red gold from the furnace. > **""O Aishah, this is Allah's reckoning of the servant for what befalls him of fever, mishap, and thorn, even goods placed in his sleeve then lost causing alarm then found in his side, until the believer emerges from his sins as red gold emerges from the furnace.""**"
"10957","Aishah رضي الله عنها, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported a man reciting {Whoever does evil will be recompensed for it} [An-Nisa: 123] asking if all deeds bring recompense then are we ruined. When this reached the Messenger ﷺ he affirmed it, explaining the believer is recompensed in this world through bodily affliction and harm. > **""Yes, the believer is recompensed for it in this world, through affliction in his body and what harms him.""**"
"10958","Hayyan ibn Bistam said he was with Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما when they passed Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr crucified. Ibn Umar invoked mercy upon him as Abu Khubayb, recalling his father al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه reporting the Messenger ﷺ said whoever does evil is recompensed for it in this world. > **""Whoever does evil will be recompensed for it in this world.""**"
"10959","Abdul-Jabbar ibn Abdullah reported a man came to Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash denying Allah spoke to Musa in speech. He declared such a one a disbeliever, tracing his recitation through al-Amash, Yahya ibn Waththab, Abu Abd al-Rahman, Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه, who recited before the Messenger ﷺ: {And Allah spoke to Musa in speech} [An-Nisa: 164]."
"10960","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported the Messenger ﷺ explained His saying {He will pay them their wages and increase them of His bounty} [An-Nisa: 173] as entry into Paradise, with increase meaning intercession for those condemned to Fire among such as had done kindness to them in this world. > **""Their wages: He will admit them to Paradise, and increase them of His bounty: intercession for whomever Fire became due, among those who had done kindness to them in this world.""**"
"10961","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه said the verse of Kalalah descended upon the Prophet ﷺ during a journey. The Prophet halted with Hudhayfah's camel-head near his waist, taught it to Hudhayfah, who taught it to Umar رضي الله عنه. In Umar's caliphate when he examined Kalalah and summoned Hudhayfah, Hudhayfah swore he conveyed exactly as taught, truthfully, adding nothing ever."
"10962","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما said Surat al-Maidah was sent down upon the Messenger ﷺ while he rode his mount, which could not bear him so he dismounted."
"10963","Asma bint Yazid رضي الله عنها said she held the halter of al-Adba, the she-camel of the Messenger ﷺ, when all of al-Maidah descended, nearly breaking the camel's foreleg from its weight."
"10964","In another narration the weight nearly broke the camel. Asma رضي الله عنها reported the descent of the full surah upon the Prophet ﷺ with such heaviness."
"10965","Samurah رضي الله عنه said {This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favor upon you and approved Islam for you as religion} [Al-Maidah: 3] descended on the day of Arafah while the Messenger ﷺ stood at Arafah on a Friday."
"10966","Amr ibn Qays heard Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan رضي الله عنه on the pulpit take up {This day I have perfected for you your religion} [Al-Maidah: 3] to its end, stating it descended on Arafah on Friday, then reciting {Whoever hopes for meeting his Lord let him do righteous deeds and associate none in worship of his Lord} [Al-Kahf: 110]."
"10967","Qatadah رضي الله عنه said it reached them that when {nor in janabah except passers through} [An-Nisa: 43] descended, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ licensed the traveler in janabah finding no water to perform tayammum and pray."
"10968","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {Remember Allah's favor upon you and His covenant which He bound you with when you said: We hear and obey} [Al-Maidah: 7] that it refers to when Allah sent the Prophet ﷺ and revealed the Book, they said we believe in the Prophet and the Book and affirm the Torah, so Allah reminded them of their covenant to fulfill it."
"10969","Utbah ibn Abd al-Sulami رضي الله عنه reported the Prophet ﷺ told his Companions to rise and fight. They answered yes, O Messenger of Allah, not saying as Banu Israil said to Musa {Go you and your Lord and fight, we sit here} [Al-Maidah: 24], but proceed you and your Lord, O Muhammad, we fight with you. > **""Rise and fight.""**"
"10970","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported the Prophet ﷺ said the most wretched of men are three: the hamstringer of Thamud's she-camel, the son of Adam who killed his brother with every blood shed on earth attaching to him as first institutor of killing, and the third lost from the original, apparently the killer of Ali رضي الله عنه. > **""The most wretched of men are three: the hamstringer of Thamud's she-camel, and the son of Adam who killed his brother — no blood is shed on earth except some of it attaches to him, for he was first to institute killing.""**"
"10971","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive in the land in corruption} [Al-Maidah: 33] that some People of the Book bound by covenant broke it and spread corruption, so Allah gave His Prophet ﷺ choice to kill, crucify, or cut hands and feet oppositely, while banishment is flight in the land, and whoever comes repentant entering Islam is accepted without reckoning for the past."
"10972","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said whatever in the Quran uses the intensified form of killing denotes punishment, and whatever uses the lightened form denotes mercy."
"10973","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {If you are given this take it, if not beware} [Al-Maidah: 41] that it concerns the Jews when a woman among them committed adultery though the Torah ruled stoning. They begrudged stoning her, hoping for license from Muhammad ﷺ. Asked by them, the Prophet ﷺ asked their Torah's ruling, then summoned their most learned in Musa's Torah, adjuring by Him Who saved them from Pharaoh and parted the sea, until they confessed the ruling is stoning. > **""How is Allah's ruling in the Torah concerning the adulterer?""**"
"10974","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, asked about suht said it is bribery. Asked if in judgment, he said that is disbelief."
"10975","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said Allah revealed {Whoever does not judge by what Allah revealed, those are the disbelievers} with the wrongdoers and defiantly disobedient, concerning two Jewish factions, one dominant in Jahiliyyah imposing fifty wasqs for its slain and a hundred for the humbled clan's slain. After the Prophet's ﷺ arrival at Madinah and renewed killing, the humbled refused inequality, war nearly erupted, they agreed to arbitrate the Messenger ﷺ while secretly sending hypocrites to test his view, whereupon Allah revealed {O Messenger, let not those grieve you who hasten to disbelief} [Al-Maidah: 41] to {the defiantly disobedient} [Al-Maidah: 47], specifically about them."
"10976","Iyad al-Ashari رضي الله عنه said when {Allah will bring a people He loves and who love Him} [Al-Maidah: 54] descended, the Messenger ﷺ said they are the people of this man, meaning Abu Musa. > **""They are the people of this man.""**"
"10977","Jabir رضي الله عنه said the Messenger ﷺ was asked about {Allah will bring a people He loves and who love Him} [Al-Maidah: 54] and said they are these, a people from Yemen, then from Kindah, then from al-Sakun, then from al-Tajib. > **""They are these, a people from Yemen, then from Kindah, then from al-Sakun, then from al-Tajib.""**"
"10978","Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه said a beggar stood over Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه bowing in voluntary prayer, who removed his ring and gave it. When the Messenger ﷺ was informed, {Your ally is only Allah and His Messenger and those who believe, who establish prayer and give zakah while bowing} [Al-Maidah: 55] descended. He recited it then declared allegiance to Ali with supplication for his allies and against his enemies. > **""Whoever I am his master, Ali is his master. O Allah, ally whoever allies him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"10979","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said a Jew called al-Nabbash ibn Qays said your Lord is miserly not spending, so Allah revealed {The Jews said: Allah's hand is chained. Chained are their hands and cursed for what they said. Rather His two hands are outstretched, He spends as He wills} [Al-Maidah: 64]."
"10980","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه said Abbas, uncle of the Messenger ﷺ, was among his guards until {And Allah will protect you from the people} [Al-Maidah: 67] descended, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ abandoned the guard."
"10981","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said the Prophet ﷺ was guarded, his uncle Abu Talib sending Hashimite men daily until {O Messenger, convey what was revealed to you from your Lord... and Allah will protect you from the people} [Al-Maidah: 67] descended. When his uncle would send guards he said Allah had protected him from jinn and men. > **""O uncle, Allah has protected me from jinn and men.""**"
"10982","Salman رضي الله عنه asked about {That is because among them are priests and monks} [Al-Maidah: 82] said the monks are those in cells, and that this verse descended upon the Messenger ﷺ."
"10983","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {When they hear what was revealed to the Messenger you see their eyes overflow with tears} [Al-Maidah: 83] they were sailors arriving with Jafar ibn Abi Talib from Abyssinia. When the Messenger ﷺ recited Quran they believed and wept. He asked if returning home they might abandon religion, they swore never to turn back, so Allah revealed this about their saying. > **""Perhaps when you return to your land you will turn away from your religion.""**"
"10984","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {Our Lord, we believe, so record us with the witnesses} [Al-Maidah: 83] that it means with Muhammad ﷺ and his nation, who bore witness he conveyed and he bore witness the messengers conveyed."
"10985","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said wine prohibition descended concerning two Ansari clans who drank till drunk and jested harmfully, awaking with marks on face, head and beard accusing brothers though brotherly without malice, then malice entered hearts. So Allah revealed {Wine, gambling, idols and divining arrows are filth of Satan's work, avoid them... will you desist} [Al-Maidah: 90-91]. When objectors cited martyrs of Badr and Uhud with it in their bellies, Allah revealed {No blame upon those who believe and do good for what they tasted} [Al-Maidah: 93]."
"10986","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, said when wine prohibition descended the Jews asked about brothers who died drinking it, so Allah revealed {No blame upon those who believe and do good for what they tasted} [Al-Maidah: 93]. The Messenger ﷺ said it was said to him: you are of them. > **""It was said to me: you are of them.""**"
"10987","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما said this Quranic verse {O you who believe, wine, gambling, idols and divining arrows are filth of Satan's work} [Al-Maidah: 90] is in the Torah: Allah sent truth to remove falsehood and nullify play, lutes, flutes, dancing, instruments and poetry, swearing none drinks after prohibition but He will thirst him on Resurrection, and none abandons it after prohibition but He will quench him at the holy enclosure."
"10988","Abu Amir al-Ashari رضي الله عنه said after a killing among them at Awtas the Messenger ﷺ reproached him, he recited {O you who believe, upon you is yourselves, he who strays harms you not once guided} [Al-Maidah: 105]. The Messenger ﷺ angered asking where they went, explaining it means the disbelievers who stray harm not when guided. > **""Where have you gone? It is only: O you who believe, upon you is yourselves, he who strays among the disbelievers harms you not once guided.""**"
"10989","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said concerning {Upon you is yourselves} [Al-Maidah: 105] its time is not this; enjoin while accepted, and when rejected then upon you is yourselves, he who strays harms you not."
"10990","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported the Prophet ﷺ explained {I was witness over them as long as I remained among them} [Al-Maidah: 117] to mean while he was among them. > **""As long as I was among them.""**"
"10991","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported the Messenger ﷺ said Surat al-Anam descended upon him all at once, escorted by seventy thousand angels with loud glorification and praise. > **""Surat al-Anam descended upon me all at once, escorted by seventy thousand angels, with loud glorification and praise.""**"
"10992","Anas رضي الله عنه reported the Messenger ﷺ said Surat al-Anam descended with a host of angels filling between horizons with loud glorification and sanctification, the earth trembling, while the Messenger ﷺ repeated glory to Allah the Magnificent. > **""Glory be to Allah the Magnificent, glory be to Allah the Magnificent.""**"
"10993","Asma bint Yazid رضي الله عنها said Surat al-Anam descended upon the Prophet ﷺ all at once, nearly breaking the camel's bone from its weight."
"10994","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {They forbid from it and keep away from it} [Al-Anam: 26] it descended about Abu Talib, who forbade harming the Prophet ﷺ yet kept away from following him."
"10995","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {They do not deny you} read lightly, as they recited it, meaning they cannot undo his being Messenger nor the Quran being Quran, though with tongues they deny — that is the denial."
"10996","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reported the Prophet ﷺ said when you see Allah granting the servant in this world what he loves despite sins, it is gradual seizure, then reciting {When they forgot what they were reminded of We opened upon them doors of all things until rejoicing in what given We seized them suddenly, confounded} [Al-Anam: 44]. > **""When you see Allah granting the servant in this world what he loves despite his sins, it is only gradual seizure.""**"
"10997","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said Quraysh chiefs passed the Messenger ﷺ with Khabbab, Suhayb, Bilal and Ammar رضي الله عنهم asking is he pleased with these, or asking are these whom Allah favored among us demanding expulsion for their following. So Quran descended {Warn with it those who fear gathering to their Lord} [Al-Anam: 51] to {Allah knows the wrongdoers}, and {Expel not those who call their Lord morning and evening} [Al-Anam: 52] to {Is not Allah knowing of the grateful} [Al-Anam: 53]."
"10998","Abdur-Rahman ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه said {Be patient with those who call their Lord morning and evening} [Al-Kahf: 28] descended upon the Prophet ﷺ in one of his chambers. He went seeking, found people remembering Allah, disheveled, worn skin, single garment, sat with them praising Allah Who placed in his nation those commanded to keep patience with. > **""Praise be to Allah Who placed in my nation those I was commanded to keep patience with.""**"
"10999","Ubayy ibn Kab رضي الله عنه said concerning {Say: He is Able to send punishment from above you} [Al-Anam: 65] they are four, all punishment, all inevitable. Two passed twenty-five years after the Messenger ﷺ with factional strife tasting each other's might, two remain inevitable: swallowing and stoning."
"11000","Ibrahim said Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, said its repository is in this world and its resting place in the womb."
"11001","Ibrahim from Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said concerning {resting place and repository} [Al-Anam: 98] the resting place is the womb and the repository the earth where one dies."
"11002","Amr ibn Kaysan heard Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما say darasta means you recited, contended, disputed."
"11003","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said concerning {Give its due on harvest day} [Al-Anam: 141] they gave whoever came to them something beyond charity."
"11004","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said concerning {Of cattle carriers and bedding} [Al-Anam: 142] carriers are what bears of camels and bedding are the small."
"11005","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said the Messenger ﷺ drew a line then said this is Allah's path, then lines right and left saying these are scattered paths with a devil calling to each, then reciting {This is My straight path, follow it, follow not paths splitting you from His path} [Al-Anam: 153]. > **""This is the path of Allah. These are scattered paths, upon each path among them is a devil calling to it.""**"
"11006","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said concerning {Do they await but angels come or your Lord comes or signs of your Lord come...} [Al-Anam: 158] it is the rising of sun with moon from its west as two harnessed camels."
"11007","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported the Prophet ﷺ explained {The day some signs of your Lord come} [Al-Anam: 158] as rising of the sun from its setting place. > **""Rising of the sun from its setting place.""**"
"11008","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reported the Messenger ﷺ told Aishah رضي الله عنها concerning {Those who split their religion and became sects} [Al-Anam: 159] they are people of innovation and desires, no repentance for them, he is free of them and they free of him. > **""O Aishah, they are people of innovation and desires, no repentance for them. I am free of them and they are free of me.""**"
"11009","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported the Prophet ﷺ said concerning {Those who split their religion and became sects, you are not of them in anything} [Al-Anam: 159] they are people of innovation and desires in this nation. > **""They are people of innovation and desires in this nation.""**"
"11010","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said this verse {Whoever brings a good deed has ten likes of it} [Al-Anam: 160] descended about bedouins. Asked about emigrants, he said theirs is greater: {Allah wrongs not an atom, if good He doubles it and gives from Him immense reward} [An-Nisa: 40]."
"11011","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said Quraysh circumambulated the House naked, whistling and clapping, so Allah revealed {Say: Who forbade Allah's adornment} [Al-Araf: 32], and they were commanded to wear garments."
"11012","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه recited {Until the camel passes through the needle's eye} [Al-Araf: 40] saying it means the husband of the she-camel."
"11013","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه said the Messenger ﷺ was asked about companions of the Heights and said they are men killed in Allah's path while disobedient to parents; martyrdom barred them from Fire and disobedience barred them from Paradise; they stand on a wall between Paradise and Fire until flesh withers till Allah finishes reckoning, then enfolds them in mercy admitting them to Paradise. > **""They are men killed in the path of Allah while disobedient to their parents. Martyrdom barred them from entering Fire, and disobedience barred them from entering Paradise, and they are upon a wall between Paradise and Fire until their flesh and fat wither, until Allah finishes reckoning creatures; when finished and none remains but them, He enfolds them in mercy and admits them to Paradise by His mercy.""**"
"11014","Umar ibn Abdur-Rahman al-Madani from his father reported the Messenger ﷺ asked about companions of the Heights said they are a people killed in Allah's path disobeying parents; disobedience barred Paradise and killing in Allah's path barred Fire. > **""A people killed in the path of Allah disobeying their parents; disobedience to parents barred them Paradise, and killing in the path of Allah barred them Fire.""**"
"11015","Abdullah ibn Busr رضي الله عنه said leaving Hims night sheltered him at al-Buqayhah where earth-dwellers attended him. He recited this verse of al-Araf {Your Lord is Allah Who created heavens and earth} [Al-Araf: 54] to its end, whereupon they told each other to guard him till morning, and at dawn he mounted."
"11016","Amr ibn Awf رضي الله عنه said they campaigned with the Messenger ﷺ year of conquest over a thousand, Allah opening Makkah and Hunayn, then between Hunayn and Taif seeing a tree hung with weapons called Dhat Anw at worshipped besides Allah. On a hot day the Messenger ﷺ turned to nearer shade when a man asked make for us a Dhat Anw at like theirs. He replied these are the ways, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul you said as Banu Israil said to Musa {Make for us a god as they have gods} [Al-Araf: 138], reciting {Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah while He favored you over worlds} [Al-Araf: 140]. > **""They are the ways. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you have said as Banu Israil said to Musa: Make for us a god as they have gods.""**"
"11017","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported the Messenger ﷺ said when Allah manifested to Musa ibn Imran seven mountains scattered, five in Hijaz and two in Yemen: in Hijaz Uhud, Thabir, Hira, Thawr and Warqan, in Yemen Hasur and Sabir. > **""When Allah manifested to Musa ibn Imran, seven mountains scattered: in Hijaz five of them, and in Yemen two.""**"
"11018","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said Musa عليه السلام was asked a matter and it was given to Muhammad ﷺ: His saying {And Musa chose seventy men of his people} [Al-Araf: 155] to {I shall ordain it for those who fear} [Al-Araf: 156]."
"11019","Ubayy ibn Kab رضي الله عنه said concerning {When your Lord took from children of Adam from their backs their progeny} [Al-Araf: 172] He gathered them, paired them, formed them, made them speak, took covenant and witnessed them {Am I not your Lord? They said: Yes}. He witnessed seven heavens, seven earths and their father Adam lest they claim ignorance, commanding no association, sending messengers reminding and revealing books. They acknowledged Him sole Lord and God. Adam raised above them saw rich, poor, handsome and else asking why not equal, told He loves to be thanked, and saw prophets as lamps singled with messenger covenant per {We took from prophets their covenant} [Al-Ahzab: 7] to Isa son of Maryam عليهما السلام among those spirits, sent to Maryam عليها السلام entering through her mouth."
"11020","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported the Prophet ﷺ said Allah took the covenant from Adam's back at Numan, meaning Arafah, extracting all progeny scattering them as particles before Him, addressing them directly: {Am I not your Lord? They said: Yes, we bear witness, lest you say on Resurrection we were heedless of this, or say our fathers associated before and we were progeny after, will You destroy us for falsifiers' doing} [Al-Araf: 172-173]. > **""Allah took the covenant from the back of Adam at Numan, meaning Arafah, extracting from his loins every progeny He created, scattering them before Him as particles, then addressing them directly: Am I not your Lord? They said: Yes, we bear witness.""**"
"11021","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said concerning {Recite to them the tale of him We gave Our signs} [Al-Araf: 175] he is Bulum, or Balam son of Baura."
"11022","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما said this verse descended about Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt, whom We gave Our signs and he stripped himself of them."
"11023","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said concerning this verse {Take pardon} [Al-Araf: 199] Allah commanded His Prophet to take pardon from people's conduct."
"11024","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه said they went with the Messenger ﷺ witnessing Badr. Allah routed the enemy; one party pursued killing, one collected camp spoils, one surrounded the Messenger ﷺ fearing surprise. At night disputing shares, each claiming greater right, {They ask you about spoils, say: Spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger, fear Allah and amend among you} [Al-Anfal: 1] descended. The Messenger ﷺ divided it promptly among Muslims. When raiding he gave a fourth, returning exhausted a third, disliking spoils saying the strong believer should return to the weak. > **""The strong of the believers should return to their weak.""**"
"11025","In his narration asked Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه about spoils, he said concerning us fighters of Badr it descended when we differed over extra share with ill manners, so Allah removed it to the Messenger ﷺ who divided it equally among Muslims."
"11026","Abdur-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه said Islam came with dislike and hardship, and they found best good in dislike. They left Makkah with the Messenger ﷺ granted loftiness and victory, and marched to Badr in the state Allah described {A party of believers were averse} [Al-Anfal: 5] {Disputing truth after clarity as driven to death looking} [Al-Anfal: 6] {When Allah promised you one of two parties yours, wishing the unarmed be yours} [Al-Anfal: 7], thorn meaning Quraysh, granted loftiness and victory, finding best good in dislike."
"11027","Mutarrif said they asked al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه, O Abu Abdullah, what brought you, neglecting the caliph till killed then seeking his blood. Al-Zubayr said they recited in time of the Messenger ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman رضي الله عنهم {Fear a fitnah afflicting not only wrongdoers among you} [Al-Anfal: 25], never reckoning themselves its people till it befell them."
"11028","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning {When disbelievers plotted against you to confine you} [Al-Anfal: 30] Quraysh conferred by night at Makkah: some to bind him in fetters at dawn meaning the Prophet ﷺ, some to kill, some to expel. Allah informed His Prophet, so Ali رضي الله عنه slept on his bed, the Messenger ﷺ leaving to the cave. Polytheists guarding Ali thinking him the Prophet ﷺ rushed at dawn finding Ali, Allah repelling their plot. Asked where his companion, he said he knew not. They tracked to the mountain confused, climbing past the cave seeing spider web at its mouth, where he remained three nights."
"11029","عن ابن مسعود في قوله: {يوم الفرقان يوم / التقى الجمعان} [الأنفال: 41] قال: كانت بدر لسبع / عشرة مضت من رمضان. Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) explained the verse about the Day of Criterion, when the two hosts met, as referring to Badr, and he dated that battle to the seventeenth night past of Ramadan."
"11030","عن عريب المليكي «عن النبي - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - في قوله {وآخرين من دونهم / لا تعلمونهم الله يعلمهم} [الأنفال: 60] أنهم الجن، / قال النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: / """" لا تخبل بيتا فيه عتيق من الخيل». Urayb al-Mulayki (may Allah be pleased with him) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) concerning the verse about others besides them whom you do not know but Allah knows, identifying them before giving a ruling about houses sheltering horses. > **""They are the jinn. Do not ruin a house in which there is a noble horse.""**"
"11031","عن عبد الله - يعني ابن مسعود - / في قول الله - عز وجل -: {لو / أنفقت ما في الأرض جميعا ما ألفت بين / قلوبهم ولكن الله ألف بينهم} [الأنفال: 63] قال: / نزلت في المتحابين في الله. Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) said the verse declaring that even spending all on earth could not unite hearts, while Allah united them, came down about those who love one another for the sake of Allah."
"11032","عن ابن عباس قال: «أسلم مع النبي - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - تسعة وثلاثون رجلا / وامرأة، وأسلم عمر تمام الأربعين، فأنزل الله - / تبارك وتعالى - {يا أيها النبي حسبك الله / ومن اتبعك من المؤمنين} [الأنفال: 64]». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that thirty-nine men and women had embraced Islam alongside the Prophet (peace be upon him), and when Umar accepted Islam the number forty was completed, upon which the verse promising Allah and the faithful followers as sufficient was revealed."
"11033","عن ابن عباس قال: «افترض عليهم أن يقاتل / كل رجل منهم عشرة، فثقل ذلك عليهم وشق / عليهم، فوضع عنهم إلى أن يقاتل الرجل الرجلين، / فأنزل الله في ذلك: {إن يكن منكم عشرون / صابرون يغلبوا مائتين} [الأنفال: 65] إلى آخر الآيات. / ثم قال: {لولا كتاب من الله سبق لمسكم / فيما أخذتم عذاب عظيم} [الأنفال: 68] يقول: لولا / أني لا أعذب من عصاني حتى أتقدم إليه. / ثم قال: {يا أيها النبي قل لمن في / أيديكم من الأسرى} [الأنفال: 70] فقال العباس: في / والله نزلت حين أخبرت رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - بإسلامي، وسألته أن يحاسبني / بالعشرين الأوقية التي وجدت معي، فأعطاني بها عشرين / عبدا كلهم تاجر بمال في يده، مع ما / أرجو من مغفرة الله - جل ذكره». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) explained that the believers were first obliged to face ten each, which weighed heavily on them, so the burden was lightened to one facing two, and he linked this to the verses on patience in fighting, the captives, and the words of Abbas about his own ransom and the twenty slaves granted him."
"11034","عن ابن عباس «أن رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - آخى بين أصحابه، فجعلوا / يتوارثون بذلك حتى نزلت {وأولو الأرحام بعضهم أولى / ببعض} [الأنفال: 75] فتوارثوا بالنسب». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) had joined his Companions in brotherhood so that they inherited from one another, until the verse giving blood relatives greater claim was revealed, after which inheritance followed lineage."
"11035","عن حذيفة قال: التي تسمون سورة التوبة هي / سورة العذاب، وما يقرؤون منها مما كنا نقرأ / إلا ربعها. Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him) said the chapter people call Surat al-Tawba is in truth the sura of punishment, and what is recited of it now is only a quarter of what they used to recite."
"11036","عن سمرة «عن النبي - صلى الله عليه / وسلم - قال: """" يوم الحج الأكبر يوم / حج أبو بكر بالناس». Samura (may Allah be pleased with him) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) an identification of the day of the Greater Pilgrimage with a well-known leading of the Hajj. > **""The day of the Greater Hajj is the day Abu Bakr led the people in Hajj.""**"
"11037","وعن سمرة بن جندب «أن رسول الله - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - قال زمن الفتح: / """" إن هذا عام الحج الأكبر """". قال: / """" اجتمع حج المسلمين وحج المشركين في ثلاثة / أيام متتابعات، واجتمع النصارى واليهود في ثلاثة أيام / متتابعات، فاجتمع حج المسلمين والمشركين والنصارى واليهود العام / في ستة أيام متتابعات، ولم يجتمع منذ خلقت / السماوات والأرض كذلك قبل العام ولا يجتمع بعد / العام حتى تقوم الساعة». Samura ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him) conveyed a report, voiced at the time of the Conquest, describing that year as the Greater Hajj with a succession of pilgrim groups, a text whose chain was vouched for but whose content was judged rejected. > **""This is the year of the Greater Hajj.""**"
"11038","وعن عبد الله بن عمر قال: «كان العرب / يحلون عاما شهرا وعاما شهرين، ولا يصيبون الحج / إلا في كل ستة وعشرين سنة مرة، وهو / النسيء الذي ذكر الله - عز وجل - / في كتابه، فلما كان عام حج أبي بكر / بالناس، وافق ذلك العام الحج، فسماه الله الحج / الأكبر، ثم حج رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - من العام المقبل، فاستقبل الناس / الأهلة، فقال رسول الله - صلى الله عليه / وسلم -: """" إن الزمان قد استدار كهيئته / يوم خلق الله السماوات والأرض». Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) described how the Arabs used to declare months lawful or postponed, rarely coinciding with the true Hajj, until the year of Abu Bakr's Hajj aligned correctly and was named the Greater Hajj, after which the Prophet (peace be upon him) performed Hajj the next year and announced the restoration of time. > **""Time has returned to its form as it was the day Allah created the heavens and the earth.""**"
"11039","وعن علي قال: «لما نزلت عشر آيات من / (براءة) على النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - دعا النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - أبا بكر [فبعثه بها] ليقرأها على أهل / مكة، ثم دعاني النبي - صلى الله عليه / وسلم - فقال: """" أدرك أبا بكر، فحيث / ما لقيته فخذ الكتاب منه فاقرأه على أهل / مكة """". فلحقته، فأخذت الكتاب منه، ورجع أبو / بكر إلى النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - فقال: يا رسول الله، نزل في شيء؟ / قال: """" لا، ولكن جبريل جاءني فقال: لن / يؤدي عنك إلا أنت أو رجل منك». Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that after ten verses of Baraa came down, Abu Bakr was first sent with them to Mecca, then Ali was ordered to overtake him, take the document, and proclaim it himself, with the reason given being a message brought by Gabriel. > **""Catch up with Abu Bakr; wherever you meet him, take the document from him and recite it to the people of Mecca. None shall discharge on my behalf except me or a man from me.""**"
"11040","عن عبد الله - يعني ابن مسعود - / قال: لا يكوى رجل يكنز فيمس درهم درهما / ولا دينار دينارا، يوسع جلده حتى يوضع كل / دينار ودرهم على حدته. Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) said the miser who hoards will not be branded coin by coin in succession; rather his skin will be stretched so that every dinar and dirham is placed upon its own spot at once."
"11041","وعن عبد الله بن عباس قال: «لما نزلت / هذه الآية {والذين يكنزون الذهب والفضة ولا ينفقونها / في سبيل الله فبشرهم بعذاب أليم} [التوبة: 34] / قال: كبر ذلك على المسلمين، وقالوا: ما يستطيع / أحد منا لولده مالا يبقى بعده، فقال: أنا / أفرج عنكم، فانطلقوا وانطلق عمر، واتبعه ثوبان، فأتى / النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم - فقال: / يا نبي الله، إنه قد كبر على أصحابك / هذه الآية، فقال نبي الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم -: """" إنا لم نفرض الزكاة / إلا لما بقي من أموالكم، وإنما فرض المواريث / في الأموال لتبقى بعدكم """". فكبر عمر، فقال / له النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: / """" ألا أخبرك بما يكنز المرء؟ المرأة الصالحة / إذا نظر إليها سرته، وإذا أمرها أطاعته، وإذا / غاب عنها حفظته». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that when the verse threatening those who hoard gold and silver alarmed the Muslims, Umar and Thawban asked the Prophet (peace be upon him), who explained the wisdom of zakah and inheritance and then named the finest treasure a person can keep. > **""We imposed zakah only so that what remains of your wealth becomes wholesome, and inheritance was prescribed so wealth endures after you. Shall I tell you what a person should hoard? A righteous wife: when he looks at her she pleases him, when he commands her she obeys him, and when he is absent she guards him.""**"
"11042","عن أبي راشد قال: رأيت المقداد فارس رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - جالسا / على تابوت من توابيت الصيارفة بحمص، قد فضل / عليها من عظمه يريد الغزو، فقلت له: لقد / أعذر الله إليك، قال: أتت علينا سورة البعوث / {انفروا خفافا وثقالا} [التوبة: 41]. Abu Rashid reported seeing Miqdad (may Allah be pleased with him), the horseman of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), seated at Hims upon a money-changer's chest despite his great age, intending to go out for battle; when reminded that Allah had excused him, he cited the sura of mobilization commanding all to march forth, light or heavy."
"11043","عن ابن عباس قال: «لما أراد النبي - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - أن يخرج إلى / غزوة تبوك قال للجد بن قيس: """" ما / تقول في مجاهدة بني الأصفر؟ """". قال: يا / رسول الله، إني امرؤ صاحب نساء، ومتى أرى / نساء بني الأصفر أفتتن، أفتأذن لي في الجلوس / ولا تفتني؟ فأنزل الله: {ومنهم من يقول ائذن / لي ولا تفتني ألا في الفتنة سقطوا} [التوبة: / 49]». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that when the Prophet (peace be upon him) prepared to march to Tabuk and invited Jadd ibn Qays to strive against Banu al-Asfar, the man pleaded his weakness for women and asked permission to stay behind without being tried, upon which the verse exposing that excuse was revealed. > **""Grant me leave and do not put me to trial; yet into trial they have already fallen.""**"
"11044","وعن ابن عباس أن النبي - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - قال: «اغزوا تغنموا بنات بني / الأصفر """". فقال رجل من المنافقين: إنه ليفتنكم / بالنساء، فأنزل الله - عز وجل -: {ومنهم / من يقول ائذن لي ولا تفتني} [التوبة: 49]». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that the Prophet (peace be upon him) urged fighting with a promise of spoils, whereupon a hypocrite warned that this was merely tempting them through women, and the verse about seeking leave lest one be tried was revealed. > **""Fight and you will gain the daughters of Banu al-Asfar as spoils.""**"
"11045","عن الحسن قال: «لقيت عمران بن حصين وأبا / هريرة فسألتهما عن تفسير هذه الآية {ومساكن طيبة / في جنات عدن} [التوبة: 72] قالا: على الخبير / سقطت، سألنا عنها رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - فقال: """" قصر من درة، / في ذلك القصر سبعون ألف دار من زمردة / خضراء، في كل بيت منها سبعون سريرا، على / كل سرير سبعون فراشا من كل لون، على / كل فراش امرأة من الحور العين، في كل / بيت مائدة على كل مائدة سبعون لونا، في / كل بيت سبعون وصيفا أو وصيفة، يعطى من / القوة ما يأتي على ذلك كله في غداة / واحدة». Hasan al-Basri said he asked Imran ibn Husayn and Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with them both) about the fine dwellings in the Gardens of Eden, and they replied that he had come to one well informed, for they had asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) himself. > **""It is a palace of pearl, containing seventy thousand doors of green emerald; in every home are seventy couches, upon every couch seventy spreads of every color, upon every spread a wife of the wide-eyed maidens; in every home a table bearing seventy dishes, and seventy servants; and he shall be granted strength to enjoy all of that in a single morning.""**"
"11046","عن ابن عباس في قوله - عز وجل / -: {وهموا بما لم ينالوا} [التوبة: 74] قال: / هم رجل يقال له الأسود بقتل رسول الله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said the verse about those who intended what they could not attain refers to a man called al-Aswad, who resolved to kill the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."
"11047","عن أبي أمامة «أن ثعلبة بن حاطب أتى / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - / فقال: يا رسول الله، ادع الله أن يرزقني / مالا. قال: """" ويحك يا ثعلبة، قليل تؤدي / شكره خير من كثير لا تطيقه، أما تريد / أن تكون مثل رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - لو سألت الله - عز / وجل - أن تسيل لي الجبال ذهبا وفضة / لسالت """". ثم رجع إليه فقال: يا رسول / الله، ادع الله أن يرزقني مالا، والله لئن / آتاني الله مالا لأوتين كل ذي حق حقه. / فقال رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم / -: """" اللهم ارزق ثعلبة مالا، اللهم ارزق / ثعلبة مالا، اللهم ارزق ثعلبة مالا """". قال: / فاتخذ غنما، فنمت كما ينمو الدود حتى ضاقت / عليه أزقة المدينة، فتنحى بها، وكان يشهد الصلاة / مع رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - ثم يخرج إليها، ثم نمت حتى تعذرت / عليه مراعي المدينة، فتنحى بها، فكان يشهد الجمعة / مع رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - ثم يخرج إليها، ثم نمت فتنحى بها، / فترك الجمعة والجماعات، فيتلقى الركبان فيقول: ماذا عندكم / من الخبر؟ وما كان من أمر الناس؟ وأنزل / الله - تعالى - على رسول الله - / صلى الله عليه وسلم -: {خذ من أموالهم / صدقة تطهرهم وتزكيهم بها} [التوبة: 103]، واستعمل رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - على / الصدقات رجلين من الأنصار ورجلا من بني سليم، / فكتب لهم سنة الصدقة وأسنانها وأمرهم أن يصدقا / الناس، وأن يمرا بثعلبة فيأخذا منه صدقة ماله، / ففعلا حتى دفعا إلى ثعلبة، فأقرآه كتاب رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - فقال: / صدقا الناس، فإذا فرغتم فمروا بي، ففعلا، فقال: / [والله] ما هذه إلا أخية الجزية. فانطلقا حتى / لحقا برسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - فأنزل الله على رسوله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم -: {ومنهم من عاهد الله لئن / آتانا من فضله لنصدقن ولنكونن من الصالحين - / فلما آتاهم من فضله بخلوا به وتولوا وهم / معرضون} [التوبة: 75 - 76] إلى قوله: """" / يكذبون """" قال: فركب رجل من الأنصار قريب / لثعلبة راحلته حتى أتى ثعلبة، فقال: ويحك يا / ثعلبة، هلكت، قد أنزل الله فيك من القرآن / كذا، فأقبل ثعلبة وقد وضع التراب على رأسه / وهو يبكي ويقول: يا رسول الله يا رسول / الله، فلم يقبل منه رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم -[صدقته حتى قبض الله رسوله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم -] ثم أتى / أبا بكر بعد رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - فقال: يا أبا بكر قد / عرفت موضعي من قومي ومكاني من رسول الله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم - فاقبل مني، / فأبى أن يقبل منه، ثم أتى عمر فلم / يقبل منه، ثم أتى عثمان فلم يقبل منه، / ثم مات ثعلبة في خلافة عثمان». Abu Umama (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated the full account of Thalaba ibn Hatib, who repeatedly begged for wealth despite warnings, was granted flocks that multiplied until congregational prayer slipped away from him, refused the collectors of charity as though it were a tribute, and was then named by revelation about those who pledged and broke faith, after which neither the Prophet (peace be upon him) nor Abu Bakr, Umar, or Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them) accepted anything from him, and he died during the caliphate of Uthman. > **""Woe to you, Thalaba! A little for which you render thanks is better than much you cannot bear. Would you not be like the Messenger of Allah? Had I asked Allah that mountains of gold and silver flow with me, they would flow. O Allah, grant Thalaba wealth.""**"
"11048","عن أبي سلمة، وعن أبي هريرة قال: قال / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: / «تصدقوا فإني أريد أن أبعث بعثا """". قال: / فجاء عبد الرحمن بن عوف فقال: يا رسول / الله، عندي أربعة آلاف، ألفان أقرضتهما ربي وألفان / لعيالي؟ فقال رسول الله - صلى الله عليه / وسلم -: """" بارك الله لك فيما أعطيت، / وبارك لك فيما أمسكت """". وبات رجل من / الأنصار فأصاب صاعين من تمر فقال: يا رسول / الله، إني أصبت صاعين من تمر، صاع لربي / وصاع لعيالي؟ قال: فلمزه المنافقون وقالوا: ما أعطى / مثل الذي أعطى ابن عوف إلا رياء. أو / قالوا: ألم يكن الله ورسوله غنيين عن صاع / هذا؟ فأنزل الله {الذين يلمزون المطوعين من المؤمنين / في الصدقات والذين لا يجدون إلا جهدهم} [التوبة: / 79] إلى آخر الآية». Abu Salama and Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) called for charity before an expedition, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf brought half his fortune while an Ansari labored through the night for two measures of dates, and the hypocrites mocked both the large gift as show and the small one as needless, until the verse about ridiculing willing givers was revealed. > **""Give charity, for I intend to dispatch an expedition. May Allah bless for you what you gave and what you kept.""**"
"11049","وعن أبي عقيل أنه «بات يجر الحرير على / ظهره على صاعين من تمر، فانقلب بأحدهما إلى / أهله ينتفعون به، وجاء بالآخر يتقرب به إلى / الله - عز وجل - فأتى به رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - فأخبره، / فقال له رسول الله - صلى الله عليه / وسلم -: """" انثره في الصدقة """". فقال / فيه المنافقون وسخروا منه: ما كان أغنى هذا / أن يتقرب إلى الله بصاع من تمر. فأنزل / الله - عز وجل -: {الذين يلمزون المطوعين / من المؤمنين في الصدقات والذين لا يجدون إلا / جهدهم} [التوبة: 79] الآيتين». Abu Aqil (may Allah be pleased with him) spent the night hauling loads on his back for two measures of dates, keeping one for his family and bringing the other to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who ordered it placed among the charity, whereupon the hypocrites sneered at so small an offering and the verse about mocking the striving givers came down. > **""Spread it among the charity.""**"
"11050","«وعن عميرة بنت سهل صاحب الصاعين الذي لمزه / المنافقون، أنه خرج بركابه بصاع من تمر وبابنته / عميرة حتى أتى النبي - صلى الله عليه / وسلم - فصبه، ثم قال: يا رسول الله، / إن لي إليك حاجة، قال: """" وما هي؟ / """". قال: تدعو الله لي ولها بالبركة، وتمسح / برأسها، فإنه ليس لي ولد غيرها. قالت: فوضع / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - / يده علي، فأقسم بالله لكأن برد يد رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - على / كبدي». Umayra bint Sahl reported from her father Sahl, the man of the two measures whom the hypocrites ridiculed, that he came with a measure of dates and his daughter, asked the Prophet (peace be upon him) to bless them both and pass his hand over her head since she was his only child, and she swore she felt the coolness of that hand upon her innermost being. > **""And what is it?""**"
"11051","عن ابن عباس قال: «لما مرض عبد الله / بن أبي مرضه الذي مات فيه جاءه النبي / - صلى الله عليه وسلم - فتكلما بكلام / بينهما، فقال عبد الله: قد فهمت ما يقول، / امنن علي فكفني في قميصك، وصل علي. فكفنه / النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم - في / قميصه وصلى عليه». قال ابن عباس: والله أعلم / أي صلاة كانت، وما خادع محمد - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - إنسانا قط. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that when Abdullah ibn Ubayy lay dying, the Prophet (peace be upon him) visited him, and the sick man asked as a favor to be shrouded in the Prophet's own shirt and prayed over; the Prophet (peace be upon him) did so, while Ibn Abbas added that Allah knows best what prayer that was, and that Muhammad never deceived any person."
"11052","عن سلمة بن الأكوع «أن رسول الله - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - قرأ {فسيرى الله / عملكم ورسوله والمؤمنون} [التوبة: 105]». Salama ibn al-Akwa (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) recited the verse announcing that Allah, His Messenger, and the believers will witness their deeds. > **""Allah will see your deeds, and His Messenger and the believers.""**"
"11053","عن ابن عباس «في قوله تعالى: {وممن حولكم / من الأعراب منافقون ومن أهل المدينة مردوا على / النفاق لا تعلمهم نحن نعلمهم سنعذبهم مرتين ثم / يردون إلى عذاب عظيم} [التوبة: 101] قال: قام / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - / يوم جمعة خطيبا، فقال: """" قم يا فلان / فاخرج فإنك منافق، اخرج يا فلان، فإنك منافق / """". فأخرجهم بأسمائهم ففضحهم، ولم يكن عمر بن / الخطاب شهد تلك الجمعة لحاجة كانت له، فلقيهم / عمر وهم يخرجون من المسجد، فاختبأ منهم استحياء / أنه لم يشهد الجمعة، وظن أن الناس قد / انصرفوا واختبؤوا هم من عمر، وظنوا أنه قد / علم بأمرهم، فدخل عمر المسجد، فإذا الناس لم / ينصرفوا، فقال له رجل: أبشر يا عمر، فقد / فضح الله المنافقين اليوم، فهذا العذاب الأول، والعذاب / الثاني عذاب القبر». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported concerning the verse about Bedouin and Medinan hypocrites whom only Allah knows, and the twofold punishment, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) once stood on Friday and ordered named men to rise and leave as hypocrites; Umar, delayed by an errand, met them leaving and each side hid from the other in embarrassment until he learned that Allah had exposed the hypocrites that day — the first punishment — with the punishment of the grave to follow. > **""Stand, so-and-so, and leave, for you are a hypocrite.""**"
"11054","عن سهل بن سعد قال: «اختلف رجلان على / عهد رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - في المسجد الذي أسس على التقوى، فقال / أحدهما: هو مسجد الرسول - صلى الله عليه / وسلم - وقال الآخر: هو مسجد قباء. فأتيا / النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم -، فسألاه، / فقال: "" هو مسجدي هذا. Sahl ibn Sad (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that two men disputed whether the mosque founded upon piety meant the Messenger's mosque or Quba, and when both asked, the Prophet (peace be upon him) settled the matter in favor of one of them. > **""It is this mosque of mine.""**"
"11055","وفي رواية: كان رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - إذا سئل عن المسجد الذي / أسس على التقوى قال: """" هو مسجدي». In another wording, whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was asked about the mosque founded upon piety, his answer named the same mosque. > **""It is my mosque.""**"
"11056","وعن زيد بن ثابت «أن رسول الله - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - سئل عن المسجد / الذي أسس على التقوى قال: """" هو مسجدي / هذا». Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) reported being asked about the mosque founded upon piety and answering with the same identification; Urwa ibn al-Zubayr added that the Messenger's mosque is better than Quba, though the verse came down concerning Quba — a remark explained as uttered without knowledge of the raised report. > **""It is this mosque of mine.""**"
"11057","عن ابن عباس قال: «لما نزلت هذه الآية / {فيه رجال يحبون أن يتطهروا} [التوبة: 108] بعث / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - / إلى عويم بن ساعدة، فقال: """" ما هذا / الطهور الذي أثنى الله عليكم؟ """". فقالوا: يا / رسول الله، ما خرج منا رجل ولا امرأة / من الغائط إلا غسل فرجه - أو قال: / مقعدته - فقال النبي - صلى الله عليه / وسلم -: """" هو هذا». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that when the verse praising men who love to purify themselves came down, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) sent to Uwaym ibn Saida asking what purification had earned Allah's praise, and they answered that every man and woman among them washed after relieving themselves, which the Prophet (peace be upon him) confirmed as its cause. > **""What is this purification for which Allah has praised you? It is this.""**"
"11058","عن عبد الله - يعني ابن مسعود - / قال: السائحون: الصائمون. Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) said the wanderers named in the verse are the fasting people."
"11059","عن زر قال: سئل ابن مسعود عن الأواه، / قال: الدعاء. Zirr reported that Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him), when asked about the word Awwah, defined it as the one who supplicates abundantly."
"11060","وعن أبي العبيدين العامري، وكان ضرير البصر وكان / عبد الله بن مسعود يدنيه، فقال لعبد الله / بن مسعود: من نسأل إذا لم نسألك؟ فرق / له فقال: ما الأواه؟ قال: الرحيم. قال: فما / الأمة؟ قال: الذي يعلم الناس الخير. قال: فما / القانت؟. قال: المطيع. قال: فما الماعون؟ قال: ما / يتعاون الناس بينهم. قال: فما التبذير؟ قال: إنفاق / المال في غير حقه، وفي رواية: في غير / حله. Abu al-Ubaydayn al-Amiri, the blind man whom Abdullah ibn Masud drew near, asked him the meanings of tender-hearted devotion, a guiding community, obedient devotion, mutual aid, and squandering; Ibn Masud answered each in turn, defining waste as spending wealth where it has no right, in one wording where it is unlawful."
"11061","وفي رواية: كان عبد الله بن مسعود يحدث / الناس كل يوم، فإذا كان يوم الخميس انتابه / الناس من الرساتيق والقرى، فجاءه رجل أعمى، فذكر / نحوه. In another wording, Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) used to address the people daily, with crowds from the towns gathering on Thursdays, when a blind man came and the same exchange of questions and answers followed."
"11062","عن عباد بن عبد الله بن الزبير قال: / «أتى الحارث بن خزمة بهاتين الآيتين من آخر / سورة (براءة) {لقد جاءكم رسول من أنفسكم} [التوبة: / 128] إلى عمر بن الخطاب - رضي الله / عنه - فقال: من معك على هذا؟ قال: / لا أدري، والله إني أشهد لسمعتها من رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - ووعيتها / وحفظتها. فقال عمر: وأنا أشهد لسمعتها من رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم»، ثم قال: / لو كانت ثلاث آيات لجعلتها سورة على حدة، / فانظروا سورة من القرآن فضعوها فيها، فوضعتها في / آخر سورة (براءة). Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr reported that Harith ibn Khazama brought the two closing verses of Baraa to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), swearing he had heard and retained them from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him); Umar bore the same witness and ordered them placed with their sura, at the end of Baraa, saying had they been three verses he would have made them a sura of their own."
"11063","وعن أبي بن كعب أنهم جمعوا القرآن في / المصاحف في خلافة أبي بكر - رحمه الله / - وكان رجال يكتبون، ويملي عليهم أبي، «فلما / انتهوا إلى هذه الآية من سورة (براءة) {ثم / انصرفوا صرف الله قلوبهم بأنهم قوم لا يفقهون} / [التوبة: 127]، فظنوا أن هذا آخر ما نزل / من القرآن، فقال لهم أبي بن كعب: إن / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - / أقرأني بعدها آيتين: {لقد جاءكم رسول من أنفسكم / عزيز عليه ما عنتم حريص عليكم بالمؤمنين رءوف / رحيم} [التوبة: 128] إلى قوله: {وهو رب العرش / العظيم} [التوبة: 129]» ثم قال: هذا آخر ما / نزل من القرآن، قال: فختم بما فتح به / بالله الذي لا إله إلا هو وهو قول / الله - تبارك وتعالى - {وما أرسلنا من / قبلك من رسول إلا نوحي إليه أنه لا / إله إلا أنا فاعبدون} [الأنبياء: 25] Ubayy ibn Kab (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that when the Quran was gathered into codices under Abu Bakr (may Allah have mercy on him), the scribes halted at the verse ending with hearts turned away, supposing it the close of revelation, but Ubayy told them the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) had taught him two further verses after it, closing with the Lord of the Mighty Throne, and that revelation opened and closed with the oneness of Allah."
"11064","وعن أبي - يعني ابن كعب - رحمه / الله - قال: آخر آية نزلت {لقد جاءكم / رسول من أنفسكم} [التوبة: 128]- الآية. Ubayy ibn Kab (may Allah have mercy on him) said the final verse to be revealed was the one beginning with the coming of a Messenger from among yourselves."
"11065","عن ابن عمر «، عن النبي - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - أنه كان يقرأ: {فبذلك / فليفرحوا هو خير مما يجمعون} [يونس: 58]». Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he used to recite the verse commanding rejoicing in Allah's bounty as better than all accumulation. > **""In that let them rejoice; it is better than what they accumulate.""**"
"11066","وعن البراء قال: {قل بفضل الله وبرحمته فبذلك / فليفرحوا هو خير مما يجمعون} [يونس: 58] قل / بفضل الله: القرآن، ورحمته: أن جعلكم من أهله. Bara (may Allah be pleased with him) said of the verse commanding rejoicing in Allah's grace and mercy that grace means the Quran and mercy means being made among its people."
"11067","عن ابن عباس «عن النبي - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - {ألا إن أولياء الله لا / خوف عليهم ولا هم يحزنون} [يونس: 62] قال: / يذكر الله بذكرهم». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) concerning the verse that no fear shall come upon the friends of Allah nor shall they grieve, explaining their distinguishing mark. > **""Allah is remembered when they are mentioned.""**"
"11068","عن عبد الله بن عمرو «عن النبي - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - أنه قال: لهم / البشرى في الحياة الدنيا قال: """" الرؤيا الصالحة / يبشرها المؤمن» """". Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that the glad tidings promised in this life mean a specific kind of vision. > **""They are the righteous dream by which the believer is given glad tidings.""**"
"11069","وعن جابر بن عبد الله بن رئاب «عن / النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم - في / قول الله - تبارك وتعالى -: ({لهم البشرى / في الحياة الدنيا وفي الآخرة} [يونس: 64] قال: / """" هي الرؤيا يراها المسلم أو ترى له». Jabir ibn Abdullah ibn Riab (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) concerning the glad tidings in this life and the next that they consist of nocturnal visions in either form. > **""They are the dream a Muslim sees, or which is shown to him.""**"
"11070","عن أبي هريرة «، عن النبي - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - قال: """" قال لي / جبريل - عليه السلام - ما كان على / وجه الأرض شيء أبغض إلي من فرعون، فلما / آمن جعلت أحشو فاه حمأة خشية أن تدركه / الرحمة» """". Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) a saying of Gabriel (peace be upon him) about Pharaoh's final moment of professed faith and the fear that mercy might overtake him. > **""Nothing on earth was more hateful to me than Pharaoh; when he professed faith, I filled his mouth with mud lest mercy reach him.""**"
"11071","وعن أبي بكر الصديق قال: أخبرت أن فرعون / كان أثرم. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) said he was informed that Pharaoh's front teeth were missing."
"11072","«عن أبي بكر قال: قلت: يا رسول الله، / لقد أسرع إليك الشيب، قال: """" شيبتني الواقعة / وعم يتساءلون وإذا الشمس كورت». Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said he remarked to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) how swiftly grey hair had come upon him, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) named the chapters responsible. > **""Al-Waqia, Amma yatasaalun, and Idha al-shamsu kuwwirat have turned me grey.""**"
"11073","وعن عقبة بن عامر «أن رجلا قال: يا / رسول الله، قد شبت، قال: """" شيبتني هود / وأخواتها» """". Uqba ibn Amir (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that when a man remarked upon grey hair, the Prophet (peace be upon him) attributed it to a chapter and its like. > **""Hud and its sisters have turned me grey.""**"
"11074","وعن عبد الله - يعني ابن مسعود - / «أن أبا بكر سأل النبي - صلى الله / عليه وسلم -: ما شيبك يا رسول الله؟ / قال: """" شيبتني هود والواقعة». Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that Abu Bakr asked the Prophet (peace be upon him) what had turned him grey, and he named two chapters. > **""Hud and al-Waqia have turned me grey.""**"
"11075","وعن سهل بن سعد قال: قال رسول الله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: """" «شيبتني / هود وأخواتها: الواقعة والحاقة وإذا الشمس كورت» """". Sahl ibn Sad (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) named Hud, its sisters, and three of them explicitly as the cause of his grey hair. > **""Hud and its sisters have turned me grey: al-Waqia, al-Haqqa, and Idha al-shamsu kuwwirat.""**"
"11076","عن محمد بن علي بن أبي طالب قال: / قلت لعلي بن أبي طالب: إن الناس يزعمون / في قول الله - جل ذكره -: """" / ويتلوه شاهد منه """" أنك أنت التالي؟ فقال: / وددت أني أنا هو، ولكنه لسان محمد - / صلى الله عليه وسلم. Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib said he asked Ali whether the witness reciting from Him named in the verse meant Ali himself; Ali replied that he wished it were so, but it means the tongue of Muhammad (peace be upon him)."
"11077","عن سعيد بن جبير قال: قلت لابن عمر: / حدثني حديثا سمعته من رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - فقال: سمعت رسول الله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم - يقول: «يأتي / الله بالعبد يوم القيامة حتى يجعله في حجابه / فيقول له: اقرأ صحيفتك، فيقرأ ويقرره بذنب ذنب، / ويقول: أتعرف؟ أتعرف؟ فيقول: نعم يا رب، فيقرأ، / فيلتفت يمنة ويسرة، فيقول: لا بأس عليك يا / عبدي، إنك في ستري، ليس بيني وبينك أن / يطلع على ذنوبك غيري، اذهب فقد غفرتها لك. / فيقال له: ادخل الجنة. وأما الكافر فيقال على / رؤوس الأشهاد: {هؤلاء الذين كذبوا على ربهم ألا / لعنة الله على الظالمين} [هود: 18]». Said ibn Jubayr said he asked Ibn Umar for a saying heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and Ibn Umar described the believer brought near on the Last Day, made to read his record and acknowledge each sin, then pardoned in concealment and admitted to the Garden, while the disbeliever is exposed before witnesses with the verse of the curse. > **""Read your record. Do you recognize it? No harm upon you, My servant; you are in My concealment, and none besides Me has seen your sins. Go, I have forgiven them for you. Enter Paradise. These are those who lied against their Lord; indeed the curse of Allah is upon the wrongdoers.""**"
"11078","عن جابر «أن رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - لما نزل الحجر في غزوة / تبوك قام فخطب الناس، فقال: """" يا أيها / الناس لا تسلوا نبيكم عن الآيات، هؤلاء قوم / صالح سألوا نبيهم أن يبعث لهم ناقة ففعل، / فكانت ترد من هذا الفج فتشرب ماءهم يوم / وردها، ويحلبون من لبنها مثل الذي كانوا يصيبون / من غبها، ثم تصدر من هذا الفج، فعقروها، / فأجلهم الله ثلاثة أيام، وكان وعد الله غير / مكذوب، ثم جاءتهم الصيحة فأهلك الله من كان / منهم بين السماء والأرض إلا رجلا كان في / حرم الله، فمنعه حرم الله من عذاب الله / """". قيل: يا رسول الله، من هو؟ قال: / أبو رغال». Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) alighted at al-Hijr during Tabuk, he warned the people against demanding signs, recounting how the people of Salih were given the she-camel with its appointed watering, hamstrung it, were granted three days, then destroyed by the Blast except one man sheltered by the sanctuary, whom he then named. > **""O people, do not ask your Prophet for signs. Ask instead about Abu Righal: he is the one the sanctuary of Allah shielded from Allah's punishment.""**"
"11079","عن ابن عباس أن رجلا جاء إلى عمر / قال: امرأة جاءت تبايعه فأدخلتها الدولج، فأصبت منها / ما دون الجماع؟ فقال: ويحك، لعلها مغيبة في / سبيل الله؟ قال: نعم. قال: فائت أبا بكر / فاسأله، فقال: لعلها مغيبة في سبيل الله؟ قال: / فقال مثل قول عمر. «ثم أتى النبي - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - فقال له مثل / ذلك، فقال: لعلها مغيبة في سبيل الله؟ ونزل / القرآن {أقم الصلاة طرفي النهار وزلفا من الليل} / [هود: 114] إلى آخر الآية، فقال: يا رسول / الله، ألي خاصة أم للناس عامة؟ فضرب عمر / صدره بيده، فقال: لا ولا نعمة عين، بل / للناس عامة، فقال رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم -: """" صدق عمر». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that a man confessed to Umar, then to Abu Bakr, then to the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he had taken a trading woman into a storeroom and done with her everything short of intercourse; each asked whether she was one whose husband was absent in Allah's path, then the verse commanding prayer at the ends of the day came down, and when the man asked whether it was for him alone, Umar struck his chest declaring it for all people, which the Prophet (peace be upon him) confirmed. > **""Perhaps she is one whose husband is absent in the path of Allah? Umar has spoken the truth.""**"
"11080","وفي رواية عند أحمد: أن امرأة أتت رجلا / تشتري منه شيئا، فقال: ادخلي الدولج حتى أعطيك. / فدخلت فقبلها وغمزها، فقالت: إني مغيب، فتركها. In Ahmad's wording, a woman came to buy something, the man lured her into the storeroom, kissed and embraced her, and when she protested her protected status he released her."
"11081","وعن ابن عباس «أن رجلا من أصحاب النبي / - صلى الله عليه وسلم - كان تحته / امرأة، فاستأذن النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - في حاجة فأذن له، فانطلق في يوم / مطير، فإذا بالمرأة على غدير ماء تغتسل، فلما / جلس منها مجلس الرجل من المرأة ذهب يحرك / ذكره، فإذا به هدبة، فقام فأتى النبي - / صلى الله عليه وسلم فذكر ذلك له، فقال / له النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: / """" صل أربع ركعات """". فأنزل الله - / تبارك وتعالى - {أقم الصلاة طرفي النهار وزلفا / من الليل إن الحسنات يذهبن السيئات} [هود: 114]» / - الآية. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that a Companion, permitted leave for an errand, came upon a woman bathing at a rain-filled pool on a wet day, drew near her as a man draws near a woman, then found himself impotent; he confessed to the Prophet (peace be upon him), who commanded prayer, and then Allah revealed the verse that good deeds remove evil ones. > **""Pray four rakahs.""**"
"11082","وعن ابن عباس عن النبي - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - قال: """" «لم أر شيئا / أحسن طلبا ولا أسرع إدراكا من مصيبة حديثة / لذنب قديم {إن الحسنات يذهبن السيئات ذلك ذكرى / للذاكرين} [هود: 114]» """". Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) a comparison of how swiftly a new act of obedience overtakes an old offense, tied to the verse that good deeds remove evil deeds. > **""I have never seen anything that seeks so well nor overtakes so swiftly as a fresh affliction for an old sin. Indeed good deeds remove evil deeds; that is a reminder for the mindful.""**"
"11083","عن جرير قال: لما نزلت {وما كان ربك / ليهلك القرى بظلم وأهلها مصلحون} [هود: 117] قال: / وأهلها ينصف بعضهم بعضا. Jarir (may Allah be pleased with him) said when the verse declaring that the Lord does not destroy towns unjustly while their people set things right came down, he explained its meaning as mutual fairness among the inhabitants."
"11084","عن جابر - يعني ابن عبد الله - / قال: «جاء بشنان اليهودي إلى رسول الله - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - فقال: يا محمد، / أخبرني عن أسماء النجوم التي رآها يوسف تسجد / له؟ قال: """" الخرتان وطارق والذيال وقابس والمصح / والصروح وذو الكنفين وذو الفرغ والفيلق ووثاب والعمودان / رآها يوسف تسجد له، فقصها على أبيه، فقال: / هذا أمر متفرق، ولعل الله يجمعه بعد» """". Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported that a Jewish man called Bashnan asked the Prophet (peace be upon him) for the names of the stars Yusuf saw prostrating, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) enumerated eleven before noting that Yusuf told his father, who called it a scattered matter Allah might gather. > **""Al-Khartan, Tariq, al-Dhayyal, Qabis, al-Musihh, al-Saruh, Dhu al-Kanafayn, Dhu al-Fargh, al-Faylaq, Watthab, and the two Pillars: these Yusuf saw prostrating to him.""**"
"11085","عن عبد الله - يعني ابن مسعود - / رضي الله عنه - قال: كان ما اشتري / به يوسف عشرين درهما، وكان أهله حين أرسل / إليهم وهم بمصر ثلاثة وتسعين إنسانا، رجالهم أنبياء / ونساؤهم صديقات، والله ما خرجوا مع موسى حتى / بلغوا ستمائة ألف وسبعين ألفا. Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) said Yusuf was bought for twenty dirhams, that his household when summoned to him in Egypt numbered ninety-three souls whose men were prophets and whose women were truthful believers, and that by the exodus with Musa they had grown to six hundred and seventy thousand."
"11086","عن ابن عباس - رضي الله عنهما - / في قوله: {أضغاث أحلام} [يوسف: 44] قال: هي / الأحلام الكاذبة. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said the confused dreams named in the verse are false dreams."
"11087","عن ابن عباس عن رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم -: """" «عجبت لصبر أخي / يوسف وكرمه والله يغفر له، حيث أرسل إليه / ليستفتى في الرؤيا، ولو كنت أنا لم أفعل / حتى أخرج. وعجبت لصبره وكرمه والله يغفر له / حتى أتي ليخرج، فلم يخرج حتى أخبرهم بعذره، / ولو كنت أنا لبادرت الباب، ولولا الكلمة لما / لبث في السجن حيث يبتغي [الفرج] من عبد / غير الله، قوله: """" اذكرني عند ربك». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) an expression of marvel at the forbearance and nobility of his brother Yusuf (peace be upon him), contrasting his restraint with human haste, and noting the single word that prolonged his prison stay. > **""I marvel at the patience and nobility of my brother Yusuf, may Allah forgive him. Had I been asked to interpret the dream, I would not have done so before being released; and had I been summoned to leave, I would not have waited to declare my innocence — I would have hastened to the door. But for the word, Mention me with your master, he would not have remained in prison seeking relief from a servant instead of Allah.""**"
"11088","وعن أبي هريرة «عن النبي - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - في قوله - عز وجل / - للرسول: ما {بال النسوة اللاتي قطعن أيديهن} / [يوسف: 50] قال رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم -: """" لو كنت أنا لأسرعت / الإجابة وما ابتغيت العذر». Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) concerning the messenger's question about the women who cut their hands that swiftness would have better suited the occasion. > **""Had I been in his place, I would have hastened to answer and would not have sought an excuse.""**"
"11089","عن أنس بن مالك - رضي الله عنه / - قال: قال رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم -: """" «كان ليعقوب أخ مؤاخ، / فقال له ذات يوم: ما الذي أذهب بصرك؟ / وما الذي قوس ظهرك؟ فقال: أما الذي أذهب / بصري فالبكاء على يوسف، وأما الذي قوس ظهري / فالحزن على ابني يامين. فأتاه جبريل - عليه / السلام - فقال: يا يعقوب، إن الله - / عز وجل - يقرئك السلام ويقول لك: أما / تستحي أن تشكوني إلى غيري؟ فقال يعقوب: إنما / أشكو بثي وحزني إلى الله. فقال جبريل - / عليه السلام -: الله أعلم بما تشكو يا / يعقوب. ثم قال يعقوب: أي رب أما ترحم / الشيخ الكبير، أذهبت بصري وقوست ظهري، فاردد علي / ريحاني يوسف أشمه [شمة] قبل الموت، ثم اصنع / بي يا رب ما شئت. فأتاه جبريل - / عليه السلام - فقال: يا يعقوب، إن الله / - عز وجل - يقرأ عليك السلام ويقول / لك: أبشر وليفرح قلبك، فوعزتي وجلالي لو كانا / ميتين لنشرتهما لك، فاصنع طعاما للمساكين، فإن أحب / عبادي إلي المساكين، وتدري لم أذهبت بصرك وقوست / ظهرك وصنع إخوة يوسف بيوسف ما صنعوا؟ لأنكم / ذبحتم شاة فأتاكم مسكين وهو صائم فلم تطعموه / منها. فكان يعقوب بعد ذلك إذا أراد الغداء / أمر مناديا فنادى: ألا من أراد الغداء من / المساكين فليتغد مع يعقوب، فإذا كان صائما أمر / مناديا فنادى: من كان صائما من المساكين، فليفطر / مع يعقوب». Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) the account of Yaqub (peace be upon him) questioned by his devoted brother about his vanished sight and bent back, his complaint carried to Allah, the reassurance sent through Gabriel, and the lesson of the unfed fasting pauper, after which Yaqub never ate alone but summoned the poor to his table. > **""Rejoice and let your heart be glad; by My might and majesty, had they both been dead I would have raised them for you. Prepare food for the poor, for the poor are the most beloved of My servants to Me.""**"
"11090","عن علي - رضي الله عنه - «في / قوله: {إنما أنت منذر ولكل قوم هاد} [الرعد: / 7] قال رسول الله - صلى الله عليه / وسلم -: """" المنذر والهادي رجل من بني / هاشم». Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) reported concerning the verse declaring the Prophet a warner with a guide for every people that the two offices belong to one household. > **""The warner and the guide is a man of Banu Hashim.""**"
"11091","عن ابن عباس «أن أربد بن قيس بن / جزي بن خالد بن جعفر بن كلاب وعامر / بن الطفيل بن مالك قدما المدينة على رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - فانتهيا / إلى رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - وهو جالس، فجلسا بين يديه، فقال عامر: / يا محمد، ما تجعل لي إن أسلمت؟ فقال / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: / """" لك ما للمسلمين وعليك ما عليهم """". / فقال عامر: أتجعل لي الأمر إن أسلمت من / بعدك؟ فقال رسول الله - صلى الله عليه / وسلم -: """" لك ما للمسلمين وعليك وما / عليهم """". قال عامر: أتجعل لي الأمر إن / أسلمت من بعدك؟ فقال رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم -: """" لك ما للمسلمين / وعليك ما عليهم """". قال عامر: أتجعل لي / الأمر إن أسلمت من بعدك؟ فقال رسول الله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: """" ليس / ذلك لك ولا لقومك، ولكن لك أعنة الخيل / """". فقال: أنا الآن على أعنة خيل نجد، / اجعل لي الوبر ولك المدر، قال رسول الله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: """" لا / """". فلما خرج أربد وعامر قال عامر: يا / أربد، إني أشغل عنك وجه محمد بالحديث فاضربه / بالسيف فإن الناس إذا قتلته لم يزيدوا على / أن يرضوا بالدية ويكرهوا الحرب، فسنعطيهم الدية، قال / أربد: أفعل. قال: فأقبلا راجعين إليه، فقال عامر: / يا محمد، قم معي أكلمك. فقام معه رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - فخليا / إلى الجدار، ووقف معه رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - يكلمه، وسل أربد السيف، / فلما وضع يده على قائم السيف يبست على / قائم السيف وأبطأ أربد على عامر بالضرب، فالتفت / رسول الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - / فرأى ما يصنع فانصرف عنهما، فلما خرج عامر / وأربد من عند رسول الله - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - مضيا حتى كانا بالحرة - / حرة بني واقم - نزلا، فخرج إليهما سعد / بن معاذ وأسيد بن حضير فقال: اشخصا يا / عدوي الله. فقال عامر: من هذا يا سعد؟ / قال: هذا أسيد بن حضير الكاتب. فخرجا حتى / إذا كان بالرقم أرسل الله على أربد صاعقة / فقتلته، وخرج عامر حتى إذا كان بالخريم أرسل / الله عليه قرحة فأخذته، فأدركه الليل في بيت / امرأة من بني سلول، فجعل يمس القرحة بيده / ويقول: غدة كغدة الجمل في بيت سلولية، يرغب / أن يموت في بيتها، ثم ركب فرسه فأركضه / حتى مات عليه راجعا، فأنزل الله فيهما {الله / يعلم ما تحمل كل أنثى وما تغيض الأرحام / وما تزداد} [الرعد: 8] إلى قوله: {وما لهم / من دونه من وال} [الرعد: 11] قال: المعقبات / من أمر الله يحفظون محمدا - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - ثم ذكر أربد وما قتله / فقال: {هو الذي يريكم البرق خوفا وطمعا} [الرعد: / 12] إلى قوله: {وهو شديد المحال} [الرعد: 13]». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated at length how Arbad ibn Qays and Amir ibn al-Tufayl came to Medina, haggled over rule after Islam, were refused all but equality or the cavalry's bridles, then conspired to assassinate the Prophet (peace be upon him) while talking by a wall, only for Arbad's hand to freeze upon his sword; after their threats they departed, Arbad was struck by a thunderbolt at al-Raqm, Amir was seized by an ulcer and died upon his horse returning from a Sulami woman's house, and the verses on the wombs, the guardians, and the thunderbolts were revealed about them. > **""You shall have what the Muslims have, and upon you shall be what is upon them. Rule after me is not for you nor for your people, but you shall have the bridles of the horses. No. Allah will prevent you.""**"
"11092","وعن أنس قال: «بعث رسول الله - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - رجلا من أصحابه إلى / رجل من عظماء الجاهلية يدعوه إلى الله - / تبارك وتعالى - فقال: أيش ربك الذي تدعوني؟ / من حديد هو؟ من نحاس هو؟ من فضة / هو؟ من ذهب هو؟ فأتى النبي - صلى / الله عليه وسلم - فأخبره، فأعاده النبي - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - الثانية، فقال مثل / ذلك، فأتى النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم / - فأخبره، فأرسل إليه الثالثة، فقال مثل ذلك، / فأتى النبي - صلى الله عليه وسلم - / فأخبره، فقال رسول الله - صلى الله عليه / وسلم -: """" إن الله - تبارك وتعالى / - قد أنزل على صاحبك صاعقة فأحرقته """". / فنزلت هذه الآية {ويرسل الصواعق فيصيب بها من / يشاء وهم يجادلون في الله وهو شديد المحال} / [الرعد: 13]». Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Prophet (peace be upon him) sent a Companion to a grandee of the pagan age who mocked the call by asking whether this Lord were of iron, copper, silver, or gold; after three such answers the Prophet (peace be upon him) announced his fate, and the verse about sending thunderbolts upon whom He wills came down, with variant wordings describing a cloud, thunder, lightning, and the crown of his head carried away. > **""Allah has sent a thunderbolt upon your man and it consumed him.""**"
"11093","«عن ابن عباس {ولو أن قرآنا سيرت به / الجبال أو قطعت به الأرض أو كلم به / الموتى} [الرعد: 31] قال: قالوا للنبي - صلى / الله عليه وسلم -: إن كان كما تقول / فأرنا أشياخنا الأول من الموتى نكلمهم، وافتح لنا / هذه الجبال جبال مكة التي قد ضمتنا. فنزلت / {ولو أن قرآنا سيرت به الجبال أو قطعت / به الأرض أو كلم به الموتى} [الرعد: 31]». Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said the disbelievers challenged the Prophet (peace be upon him) to show them their dead ancestors to converse with and to open the enclosing mountains of Mecca, whereupon the verse about a Quran moving mountains, cleaving earth, or speaking to the dead was revealed."
"11094","عن ابن عمر قال: سمعت رسول الله - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - يقول: """" «يمحو / الله ما يشاء """" إلا الشقوة والسعادة والحياة / والموت» """". Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) recite the verse on effacing what He wills with a stated exception. > **""Allah effaces what He wills — except wretchedness, felicity, life, and death.""**"
"11095","عن أبي ذر قال: قال رسول الله - / صلى الله عليه وسلم -: """" «لم يبعث / الله نبيا إلا بلغة قومه» """". Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stated the universal pattern of revelation and language. > **""Allah never sent a prophet except in the language of his people.""**"
"11096","عن عبد الله - يعني ابن مسعود - / في قوله: {فردوا أيديهم في أفواههم} [إبراهيم: 9] / قال: عضوا أصابعهم غيظا. Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) said the verse about thrusting their hands into their mouths means they bit their fingers in fury."
"11097","عن كعب بن مالك رفعه إلى النبي - / صلى الله عليه وسلم - فيما أحسب - / «في قوله تعالى: {سواء علينا أجزعنا أم صبرنا / ما لنا من محيص} [إبراهيم: 21] قال: """" / يقول أهل النار هلموا فلنصبر """". قال: """" / فصبروا خمسمائة عام، فلما رأوا ذلك لا ينفعهم / قالوا: هلموا فلنجزع """". قال: """" فيبكون خمسمائة / عام، فلما رأوا ذلك لا ينفعهم قالوا: {سواء / علينا أجزعنا أم صبرنا ما لنا من محيص} / [إبراهيم: 21]». Kab ibn Malik, raising it to the Prophet (peace be upon him) as he recalled, reported concerning the verse that wailing and patience are alike with no escape that the people of the Fire will first agree to endure, then, seeing no benefit, agree to wail, each for centuries, before uttering that very verse. > **""Come, let us endure. Come, let us wail. It is the same for us whether we wail or endure; we have no escape.""**"
"11098","عن ابن عمر، عن النبي - صلى الله / عليه وسلم - «في قوله تعالى: {كشجرة طيبة} / [إبراهيم: 24] قال: """" هي التي لا تنفض / ورقها [وظننت أنها النخلة]». Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported from the Prophet (peace be upon him) concerning the parable of the good tree that it denotes the tree whose leaves do not fall, with the narrator inclining to the date-palm. > **""It is the tree whose leaves never fall.""**"
"11099","عن أبي سعيد الخدري قال: «سمعت رسول الله / - صلى الله عليه وسلم - يقول في / هذه الآية {يثبت الله الذين آمنوا بالقول الثابت / في الحياة الدنيا وفي الآخرة} [إبراهيم: 27] قال: / """" في الآخرة في القبر» """". Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) said he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) explain the verse on keeping believers firm in this life and the next as referring to a specific abode. > **""In the Hereafter — in the grave.""**"
"11100","وعن أبي قتادة الأنصاري في قوله - تعالى / -: {يثبت الله الذين آمنوا بالقول الثابت في / الحياة الدنيا وفي الآخرة} [إبراهيم: 27] قال: إن / المؤمن إذا مات أجلس في قبره فيقال له: / من ربك؟ فيقول: الله ربي، فيقال له: من / نبيك؟ فيقول: محمد بن عبد الله. فيرد عليه / ثلاث مرات. Abu Qatada al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) said of the verse on the firm word that when the believer dies he is sat up in his grave and asked his Lord and his Prophet, and the questioning is repeated three times."
"11101","وعن ابن عباس في قوله: {يثبت الله الذين / آمنوا بالقول الثابت في الحياة الدنيا وفي الآخرة} / [إبراهيم: 27] قال: المخاطبة في القبر: من ربك؟ / وما دينك؟ ومن نبيك؟ وفي الآخرة مثل ذلك. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said the firm word in the verse means the interrogation of the grave — who is your Lord, what is your religion, who is your prophet — and the like of it in the Hereafter."
"11102","عن علي: {الذين بدلوا نعمة الله كفرا وأحلوا / قومهم دار البوار} [إبراهيم: 28]- الآية. قال: نزلت / في الأفخرين من بني مخزوم وبني أمية، فقطع / الله دابرهم يوم بدر، وأما بنو أمية فمتعوا / إلى حين. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said the verse about those who exchanged Allah's favor for disbelief and settled their people in ruin came down about the boastful chiefs of Makhzum and Umayya: the former were cut off at Badr while the latter were granted respite for a time."
"11103","عن عبد الله بن مسعود قال: «قال رسول / الله - صلى الله عليه وسلم - في / قول الله: {يوم تبدل الأرض غير الأرض} [إبراهيم: / 48] قال: """" أرض بيضاء كأنها فضة، لم / يسفك فيها دم حرام، ولم يعمل فيها خطيئة». Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) explained the verse on the Day the earth is changed to another earth as describing its new state. > **""A white land like silver, in which no unlawful blood was shed and no sin was committed.""**"
"11104","Abu Musa al-Ash'ari رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained the verse of Surah al-Hijr concerning those who disbelieved wishing they had been Muslims, describing what will occur when sinful Muslims are taken out of the Fire. > **""When the people of the Fire gather in the Fire, along with whomever Allah wills of the people of the qiblah, the disbelievers will say to the Muslims: Were you not Muslims? They will say: Yes. They will say: What did your Islam avail you when you are with us in the Fire? They will say: We had sins for which we were seized. Allah will hear what they said, so He will command that whoever was in the Fire of the people of the qiblah be brought out. When the remaining disbelievers see that, they will say: Would that we had been Muslims so we could come out as they came out. Then he ﷺ recited: Alif Lam Ra. These are verses of the Book and a clear Qur'an. The disbelievers will often wish they had been Muslims.""**"
"11105","Zakariyya ibn Yahya reported that he asked Abu Ghalib about the verse in Surah al-Hijr concerning those who disbelieved wishing they had been Muslims, and Abu Ghalib narrated from Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it was revealed about a particular group who will see Allah pardoning the Muslims. > **""It was revealed concerning the Khawarij, when they see Allah pardoning the Muslims, the rulers, and the community, they will say: Would that we had been Muslims.""**"
"11106","Zirr reported that he used to read the mushaf to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه, and when they reached the verse about Allah sending the winds as fertilizers, Abdullah asked him what the fertilizing winds meant, corrected his first answer, and explained how rain is produced. Ibn Mas'ud said the wind carries the water, passes by the clouds, and causes them to pour forth rain as a milk-camel pours forth milk, and then it rains."
"11107","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once passed by a group of his Companions who were laughing at something, and he ﷺ rebuked them while Paradise and Hell were before them. Concerning that occasion the verse of Surah al-Hijr was revealed. > **""Do you laugh while the mention of Paradise and Hell is before you? Inform My servants that I am the Forgiving, the Merciful, and that My punishment is the painful punishment.""**"
"11108","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the word of Allah in Surah al-Hijr, ""By your life,"" as an oath meaning ""by your life,"" referring to the life of the Prophet ﷺ."
"11109","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah had given him something great in place of the Torah, and the narrator then mentioned the full hadith. > **""I was given the seven long surahs in place of the Torah.""**"
"11110","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Hijr about the seven of the oft-repeated verses, saying it refers to the seven long surahs of the Qur'an."
"11111","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the verse of Surah al-Hijr concerning those upon whom division came down, asking who they were, and then asked about those who made the Qur'an into fragments. The Prophet ﷺ answered both questions. > **""The Jews and the Christians. They believed in part of it and disbelieved in part of it.""**"
"11112","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by some people at Makkah who mocked him behind his back, claiming he said he was a prophet, while Jibril عليه السلام was with him. Jibril gestured toward them and a painful affliction struck their bodies so they became foul-smelling and no one could approach them. Then Allah revealed the verse of Surah al-Hijr about sufficing against the mockers."
"11113","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the verse about Allah sufficing against the mockers, naming them as al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth, al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib Abu Zam'ah of Banu Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza, al-Harith ibn Aytel al-Sahmi, and al-Asi ibn Wa'il al-Sahmi. He described how the Prophet ﷺ complained of them to Jibril عليه السلام, who pointed to the fatal spot of each and said he had sufficed him against them, and then described the death of each: Walid's vein cut by an arrow, Aswad ibn al-Muttalib blinded, Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth killed by sores on his head, Harith killed by yellow fluid filling his belly until it came from his mouth, and Asi killed when a thorn entered his foot and it swelled."
"11114","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that the mockers were eight in number, including al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, Abu Zam'ah who is al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib, al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth, al-Asi ibn Wa'il, and al-Harith of the Ayatil. He said all of them were killed at Badr through death or disease. The chain is sound though the text as transmitted appears abbreviated."
"11115","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the people of Lut were destroyed at a particular time and that the Hour will likewise come at such a time, which means the time of the dawn call when people seek forgiveness and supplicate. > **""The people of Lut were destroyed only at the time of the call to prayer, and the Hour will come only at the time of the call to prayer.""**"
"11116","Zirr reported that while reading the mushaf to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه they reached the verse of Surah al-Nahl about Allah making sons and grandsons from spouses. Abdullah asked him what the grandsons meant, rejected his first answer that it meant a man's servants, and began to explain it."
"11117","In another version of the discussion about the verse of Surah al-Nahl concerning grandsons, when it was suggested that it meant a man's descendants and the descendants of his children, Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه corrected that and said it means relatives through marriage."
"11118","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Nahl about increasing punishment upon punishment, saying the disbelievers will have additional creatures of torment set upon them with enormous fangs like tall palm trees."
"11119","Shahr reported from Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting in the courtyard of his house when Uthman ibn Maz'un passed, smiled at him, and was invited to sit. The Prophet ﷺ then gazed at the sky, lowered his gaze to his right toward the earth, and moved his head as though understanding words being spoken to him while Ibn Maz'un watched. Afterward Uthman asked him about what he had seen, and the Prophet ﷺ told him a messenger of his Lord had just come to him and recited the verse of Surah al-Nahl. Uthman said it was then that faith became firm in his heart and he loved the Prophet ﷺ. > **""A messenger of my Lord just came to me while you were sitting. He said: Allah commands justice, excellence, and giving to relatives, and forbids immorality, evil, and transgression. He admonishes you that you may remember.""**"
"11120","Uthman ibn Abi al-As رضي الله عنه reported that he was sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the Prophet ﷺ raised his gaze, then lowered it until it nearly touched the ground, then raised it again and explained that Jibril عليه السلام had instructed him where to place the verse of Surah al-Nahl. > **""Jibril came to me and commanded me to place this verse in this position of this surah: Allah commands justice, excellence, and giving to relatives, and forbids immorality, evil, and transgression. He admonishes you that you may remember.""**"
"11121","Abu al-Duha reported that Masruq and Shutayr ibn Shakl met in the mosque, and Masruq asked whether he had heard Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه say that the most comprehensive verse of the Qur'an concerning lawful, unlawful, command, and prohibition is the verse of Surah al-Nahl enjoining justice and forbidding immorality. The other confirmed it, and Masruq said he had heard it too."
"11122","Masruq reported that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه said Mu'adh was a nation, devout to Allah, upright, and not of the idolaters. A man of Ashja' named Farwah objected that the verse says Ibrahim, and Ibn Mas'ud asked who had forgotten, explaining they used to liken Mu'adh to Ibrahim. Asked about the word nation, he said it means a teacher of good, and asked about the word devout, he said it means obedient to Allah and His Messenger."
"11123","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explain the verse of Surah al-Isra about fastening every person's deed to his neck. > **""The bird of every servant is in his neck.""**"
"11124","Salman رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever seeks to rise a degree in this world and attains it, Allah will lower him by even more in the Hereafter, and then the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse that the Hereafter is greater in degrees and greater in excellence. > **""There is no servant who wishes to rise a degree in this world and rises, except that Allah will lower him in the Hereafter by more than it. And the Hereafter is greater in degrees and greater in excellence.""**"
"11125","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that when the verse of Surah al-Isra about giving the relative his right was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Fatimah رضي الله عنها and granted her Fadak."
"11126","Abu al-Ubaydayn reported that he asked Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه about the verse of Surah al-Isra forbidding wasteful squandering, and he said it means spending in other than what is right."
"11127","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Isra about those who turn away in aversion when the Lord alone is mentioned in the Qur'an, saying they are the devils."
"11128","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that when the Prophet ﷺ passed by al-Hijr he warned against demanding miraculous signs, recalling that the people of Salih demanded the she-camel which came from this mountain pass, drank their water one day and gave them milk the next, but they defied their Lord's command and hamstrung her. A mighty blast seized them and destroyed all beneath the sky except one man who was in Allah's sanctuary. Asked who he was, the Prophet ﷺ named him and said what befell him when he left the sanctuary. > **""Do not ask for signs, for the people of Salih asked for them and it came from this mountain pass. They defied the command of their Lord and hamstrung her. A blast seized them and Allah destroyed all beneath the sky among them except one man who was in the sanctuary of Allah. He is Abu Righal. When he left the sanctuary, what befell his people befell him.""**"
"11129","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that the people of Makkah asked the Prophet ﷺ to turn Safa into gold for them and to remove the mountains so they could cultivate. It was said to him that he could either grant them respite or give them what they asked, but if they then disbelieved they would perish as those before them perished. The Prophet ﷺ chose respite, and Allah revealed the verse of Surah al-Isra about not sending signs because the former peoples denied them, and about Thamud and the she-camel. > **""Rather I will grant them respite.""**"
"11130","In another version of the Makkan request for Safa to become gold, the Prophet ﷺ supplicated, and Jibril عليه السلام came and conveyed from the Lord that if he wished Safa would become gold for them, but whoever disbelieved afterward would be punished as none in the worlds had been punished, and if he wished the door of repentance and mercy would be opened for them. The Prophet ﷺ chose the door of repentance and mercy. > **""Rather the door of repentance and mercy.""**"
"11131","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Isra about the night prayer as an addition for the Prophet ﷺ, saying the additional prayer was exclusive to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"11132","In another version, when Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه was asked about the additional prayer, he said it was an additional obligation for the Prophet ﷺ, while for his followers it is a merit and recommendation."
"11133","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ an explanation of the verse of Surah al-Isra about establishing prayer at the decline of the sun. > **""The decline of the sun is its passing the meridian.""**"
"11134","Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid al-Nakha'i reported that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه once led them in prayer while a man kept watching whether the sun had set. Abdullah asked what they were watching, swore by Allah that this was the appointed time of that prayer, and cited the verse of Surah al-Isra about establishing prayer from the decline of the sun to the darkness of the night, explaining that this is the decline and this is the darkness."
"11135","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه explained the phrase ""to the darkness of the night"" in the verse of Surah al-Isra as referring to the last Isha prayer."
"11136","Ka'b ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that on the Day of Resurrection people will be raised, he and his nation will be upon a hill, his Lord will clothe him in a green robe, permission will be granted to him, and he will say what Allah wills him to say, and that is the Praised Station. > **""People will be raised on the Day of Resurrection, and I and my nation will be upon a hill. My Lord will clothe me in a green robe, then permission will be granted to me and I will say what Allah wills me to say, and that is the Praised Station.""**"
"11137","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Isra about the Lord raising the Prophet ﷺ to a praised station, saying He will seat him between Himself and Jibril عليه السلام and he will intercede for his nation, and that is the Praised Station."
"11138","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Makkah on the day of the conquest while three hundred and sixty idols stood around the Ka'bah, whose feet Iblis had fastened with lead. With a stick in his hand he pointed to each idol and it fell upon its face while he recited the verse of Surah al-Isra until he had passed by all of them. > **""Truth has come and falsehood has perished. Indeed falsehood is ever perishing.""**"
"11139","Aisha رضي الله عنها explained the verse of Surah al-Isra about neither reciting prayer loudly nor inaudibly, saying it was revealed concerning supplication."
"11140","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه warned that this Qur'an will be taken away from among them. When it was said that they recite it and have secured it in their codices, he said the Qur'an will travel by night so nothing of it remains in any servant's heart or in his copy, people will wake in the morning destitute like cattle, and he recited the verse of Surah al-Isra about Allah taking away what He had revealed."
"11141","Mu'adh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ identified the verse of might as the closing verse of Surah al-Isra glorifying Allah who has taken no son, has no partner in dominion, needs no protector from humiliation, and is to be magnified greatly. > **""The verse of might is: Praise belongs to Allah who has not taken a son, has no partner in dominion, has no protector from humiliation, and magnify Him greatly.""**"
"11142","In another chain from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he used to say that might belongs to Allah with praise of Him who has not taken a son. > **""Might belongs to Allah, and praise belongs to Allah who has not taken a son.""**"
"11143","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that he went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hand in hand and they met a man of wretched appearance. The Prophet ﷺ asked him what had brought him to that state, and he said hardship and illness. When the man showed no interest in words that would remove his distress in exchange for having witnessed Badr and Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ laughed and said the people of Badr and Uhud cannot attain what the contented poor person attains. Abu Hurayrah then asked to be taught the words, and the Prophet ﷺ taught him a declaration of reliance upon the Ever-Living who does not die along with the verse of might. Later the Prophet ﷺ found him in an improved state, asked him what had happened, and he said he had continued to say the words he had been taught. > **""Say, O Abu Hurayrah: I rely upon the Ever-Living, the Sustainer, who does not die. Praise belongs to Allah who has not taken a son, has no partner in dominion, has no protector from humiliation, and magnify Him greatly.""**"
"11144","Mu'adh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that whoever recites the beginning and the end of Surah al-Kahf will have light from his feet to his head, and whoever recites all of it will have light between the earth and the sky. > **""Whoever recites the beginning of Surah al-Kahf and its end will have light from his feet to his head, and whoever recites all of it will have light between the earth and the sky.""**"
"11145","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever recites Surah al-Kahf will have light on the Day of Resurrection from his station to Makkah, and whoever recites ten verses from its end and the Dajjal then emerges will not be harmed by him. > **""Whoever recites Surah al-Kahf will have light on the Day of Resurrection from his station to Makkah, and whoever recites ten verses from its end, then the Dajjal emerges, he will not harm him.""**"
"11146","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ about the protection granted to whoever recites ten verses from the end of Surah al-Kahf. > **""Whoever recites ten verses from the end of Surah al-Kahf will be protected from the trial of the Dajjal.""**"
"11147","In another version the wording is ""the last ten verses."" The compiler notes that in Sahih the hadith of Abu al-Darda is established concerning the first verses of Surah al-Kahf."
"11148","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه held that one may make the exception of saying ""if Allah wills"" even after a year, and he recited the verses of Surah al-Kahf forbidding saying one will do something tomorrow without the will of Allah and commanding remembrance of the Lord when one forgets, meaning when one remembers."
"11149","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Kahf about remembering the Lord when one forgets, saying that if one forgets the exception then one should make it when one remembers. He said this concession was specific to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and none of them may make the exception except within his oath."
"11150","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Kahf that none knows the Sleepers except a few, saying he was among that few, and he named them and their dog. Abu Abd al-Rahman added that his father said he had heard that whoever writes these names and casts them into a fire, the fire subsides."
"11151","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reported in a raised narration that the treasure mentioned by Allah in His Book was a solid tablet of gold inscribed with marvels at those who believe in destiny yet distress themselves, remember the Fire yet laugh, and remember death yet grow heedless, ending with the testimony of faith. > **""The treasure mentioned by Allah in His Book was a solid tablet of gold. I marvel at one who believes in destiny and then distresses himself. I marvel at one who remembers the Fire and then laughs. I marvel at one who remembers death and then grows heedless. There is no deity but Allah, Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"11152","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Kahf about the treasure beneath the wall, saying treasures were lawful for them while spoils were forbidden to them, whereas spoils were made lawful for this nation while hoarding treasures was forbidden."
"11153","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the verse of Surah al-Kahf concerning the setting place of the sun. > **""In a muddy spring.""**"
"11154","Abu Salih reported that Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm was in the Damascus mosque among some Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, including Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه, and warned people that what he feared most for them was hidden idolatry. Mu'adh sought Allah's forgiveness and asked whether he had not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that fasting, charity, or prayer for show is idolatry. He said he had, but the Prophet ﷺ had also recited the verse of Surah al-Kahf about hoping for the meeting with the Lord. That distressed the people, so he comforted them by likening it to the verse of Surah al-Rum about usury: a deed done for show is recorded neither for the doer nor against him. > **""Whoever fasts for show has committed idolatry. Whoever gives charity for show has committed idolatry. Whoever prays for show has committed idolatry.""**"
"11155","Al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ an explanation of the verse of Surah Maryam in which Allah tells Maryam that her Lord placed a stream beneath her. > **""The river.""**"
"11156","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explain the stream mentioned to Maryam عليها السلام in Surah Maryam as a river Allah brought forth for her to drink from. > **""The stream which Allah mentioned to Maryam, when He said He placed a stream beneath her, is a river Allah brought forth for her to drink from.""**"
"11157","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah Maryam about meeting deviation, saying it is a valley in Hell filled with pus."
"11158","In another version, the deviation in the verse of Surah Maryam is a river in Hell into which those who followed lusts will be cast."
"11159","Abu Saminah reported that they differed here about the coming to Hell, some saying no believer will enter it and others saying all will enter it and then Allah will save the God-fearing. He met Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنه and told him of the dispute. Jabir pointed with his fingers to his ears, declared them deaf if he had not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the coming means entering, that no righteous or wicked person will remain without entering it, that it will become coolness and peace for the believers as it was for Ibrahim, until the Fire cries out from their coolness, and then Allah will save the God-fearing and leave the wrongdoers. > **""The coming means entering. No righteous or wicked person will remain except that he enters it. It will be coolness and peace for the believers as it was for Ibrahim, until the Fire cries out from their coolness. Then Allah will save those who feared Him and leave the wrongdoers in it.""**"
"11160","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whatever Allah made lawful in His Book is lawful, whatever He forbade is forbidden, and whatever He was silent about is pardon, so people should accept Allah's concession since Allah does not forget anything. He then recited the verse of Surah Maryam that the Lord is never forgetful. > **""Whatever Allah made lawful in His Book is lawful. Whatever He forbade is forbidden. Whatever He was silent about is pardon, so accept the concession of Allah, for Allah does not forget anything. And your Lord is never forgetful.""**"
"11161","Al-Nu'man ibn Sa'd reported that they were sitting with Ali رضي الله عنه when he recited the verse of Surah Maryam about gathering the God-fearing to the Most Merciful as a delegation. Ali swore they will not be gathered on their feet, for a delegation is not gathered on foot; rather they will be brought she-camels the like of which creation never saw, with saddles of gold, riding upon them until they reach the gates of Paradise."
"11162","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah Maryam about the Most Merciful placing affection for those who believe and do righteous deeds, saying it means love in the hearts of the believers."
"11163","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah recited Ta-Ha and Ya-Sin a thousand years before creating Adam, and when the angels heard the Qur'an they said blessed is the nation upon which this descends, blessed are the hearts that carry this, and blessed are the tongues that speak it. > **""Allah recited Ta-Ha and Ya-Sin a thousand years before creating Adam. When the angels heard the Qur'an they said: Blessed is a nation upon which this descends. Blessed are hearts that carry this. Blessed are tongues that speak this.""**"
"11164","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه explained the opening letters Ta-Ha of Surah Ta-Ha as meaning ""O man."""
"11165","Ali رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to alternate between his feet in standing, resting upon each foot in turn, until the verse of Surah Ta-Ha was revealed that the Qur'an was not sent down to cause him distress."
"11166","Sa'id ibn Jubayr reported from Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه concerning the verse of Surah Ta-Ha about testing Musa with severe trials. Asked what the trials were, Ibn Abbas said it was a long account and asked him to return in the morning. He then narrated at length how Pharaoh and his courtiers, recalling Allah's promise to Ibrahim of prophets and kings from his progeny, ordered newborn males of the Children of Israel to be slaughtered, then adopted killing one year and sparing the next. Harun was born in a year of sparing and Musa in a year of killing, his mother grieved, and Allah inspired her not to fear or grieve and to nurse him and cast him into the river, promising his return. The account continues through his rescue, upbringing, the killing of the Egyptian, his flight to Madyan, the fire, prophethood, confrontation with Pharaoh, and deliverance — all of which were among the trials."
"11167","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه said man was called insan only because a covenant was entrusted to him and he forgot it, referring to the verse of Surah Ta-Ha about Adam forgetting the command."
"11168","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever follows the Book of Allah will be guided from misguidance and protected from an evil reckoning on the Day of Resurrection, for Allah says whoever follows His guidance will neither stray nor suffer misery. > **""Whoever follows the Book of Allah, Allah will guide him from misguidance and protect him from an evil reckoning on the Day of Resurrection. That is because Allah says: Whoever follows My guidance will neither stray nor suffer misery.""**"
"11169","Abu al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse of Surah Ta-Ha in the singular form of guidance. > **""Whoever follows My guidance.""**"
"11170","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ an explanation of the verse of Surah Ta-Ha about a constrained life for whoever turns away from remembrance, saying Allah will set upon him snakes that tear his flesh until the Hour comes. > **""The constrained life which Allah mentioned is that ninety-nine snakes are set upon him, tearing his flesh until the Hour comes.""**"
"11171","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah Ta-Ha about a constrained life as the punishment of the grave."
"11172","Jarir رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ an explanation of the verse of Surah Ta-Ha about glorifying the Lord before sunrise and before sunset. > **""Before the rising of the sun is the dawn prayer, and before its setting is the afternoon prayer.""**"
"11173","Abdullah ibn Salam رضي الله عنه reported that when hardship befell the family of the Prophet ﷺ, he commanded them to pray and then recited the verse of Surah Ta-Ha commanding one's family to pray and to persevere in it. > **""And command your family to pray and persevere in it.""**"
"11174","Al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim reported that news reached Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه that Marwan interpreted the verse of Surah al-Anbiya about giving Ayyub his family and the like of them as meaning a different family. Ibn Mas'ud said he was given his own family in person along with the like of them."
"11175","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه explained the verse of Surah al-Anbiya about Dhu al-Nun going forth in anger, saying it was like a servant fleeing from his master."
"11176","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reported that he passed by Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه in the mosque, greeted him, and received no reply. He complained to Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه, who summoned Uthman and asked why he had not returned his brother's greeting. After oaths on both sides, Uthman recalled that he had been preoccupied with a phrase he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, which always veiled his sight and heart when he recalled it. Sa'd said he would inform him of it: the Prophet ﷺ had begun to mention the foremost supplication when a bedouin distracted him, and Sa'd followed the Prophet ﷺ until he turned to him and told him it was the supplication of Dhu al-Nun in the belly of the fish, through which no Muslim ever supplicates for anything without Allah answering him. > **""The supplication of Dhu al-Nun when he was in the belly of the fish: There is no deity but You, glory be to You, indeed I was among the wrongdoers. No Muslim ever supplicates with it to his Lord for anything except that He answers him.""**"
"11177","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that when the verse of Surah al-Anbiya was revealed that they and what they worship besides Allah are fuel for Hell which they will enter, it was clarified by the verse that those for whom good had preceded from Allah will be kept far from it, meaning Isa son of Maryam عليه السلام and those with him."
"11178","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه reported that when the verse of Surah al-Anbiya was revealed that they and what they worship besides Allah are fuel for Hell, Abdullah ibn al-Zib'ra said he would dispute with the Prophet ﷺ on behalf of his people. He asked whether Christians worship Isa, Jews worship Uzayr, and Banu Tamim worship angels, so would they all be in the Fire. The Prophet ﷺ assented to what had been revealed, and then Allah revealed the verse that those for whom good had preceded from Allah will be kept far from it."
"11179","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: when only those who will abide eternally remain in the Fire, they will be placed in coffins of fire fitted with nails of fire — he repeated this twice or thrice — so that each will see none punished in the Fire but himself. Then he recited: for them therein is groaning, and therein they hear nothing (21:100)."
"11180","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما explained His saying: We sent you not but as a mercy to the worlds (21:107). Whoever follows him, he is mercy for him in this world and the Hereafter; whoever does not follow him is spared what afflicted other nations — sinking, transformation, and pelting."
"11181","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited, O people, fear your Lord; the quake of the Hour is a mighty thing (22:1), with his Companions present, then asked if they knew what day that is. They said Allah and His Messenger know best. He ﷺ described Allah commanding Adam عليه السلام to send forth the company of the Fire — nine hundred ninety-nine of every thousand to the Fire and one to Paradise. This weighed heavily on the people, so he ﷺ gave glad tidings that they would be a quarter, then half, of Paradise, and that they are as a mole on a camel among nations, with Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj multiplying others. > **""Do you know what day that is? That is the day Allah will say: O Adam, arise and send forth the company to the Fire — from every thousand, nine hundred ninety-nine to the Fire and one to Paradise. Strive and receive glad tidings, for you are between two creations you only increase in number.""**"
"11182","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited, a Day that turns children grey, and said it is the Day of Resurrection, when Allah will command Adam عليه السلام to send from his progeny a company to the Fire — nine hundred ninety-nine of a thousand, with one saved. When this weighed on the Muslims, he ﷺ consoled them that the progeny of Adam is numerous, that Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj are from Adam's children and multiply a thousandfold, and that in them and their like is a shield for you. > **""That is the Day of Resurrection. Allah will say to Adam: arise and send from your progeny a company to the Fire — from a thousand, nine hundred ninety-nine, and one saved. The sons of Adam are many, and in them is a shield for you.""**"
"11183","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained His saying: alike are the resident therein and the visitor (22:25) — meaning the one settled and the one departing are equal in rank there. > **""The resident and the one who departs are equal.""**"
"11184","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, as conveyed without raising it as marfūʿ by Shuʿbah, said concerning His saying: whoever intends therein deviation with wrongdoing (22:25): if a man merely intended deviation therein, even were he as distant as ʿAdan, Allah would taste him a painful punishment."
"11185","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning: whoever intends therein deviation with wrongdoing, We shall taste him a painful punishment (22:25). Whoever intends a sin outside the House, it is not recorded until he acts; whoever intends a sin to commit in the House, Allah will not let him die until He tastes him a painful punishment."
"11186","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr رحمه الله named those who went to Abyssinia the first time before Jaʿfar and his companions: ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān with his wife Ruqayyah daughter of the Messenger ﷺ, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, Abū Ḥudhayfah with his wife Sahlah, al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah, Abū Salamah with Umm Salamah رضي الله عنهم, Abū Sabrah with Umm Kulthūm, and Suhayl ibn Bayḍāʾ. They returned when Sūrat al-Najm was revealed and the idolaters, hearing Satan's interpolation of the cranes, rejoiced and prostrated with the Prophet ﷺ at the end of the sūrah — al-Walīd taking dust to his forehead — until Jibrīl عليه السلام disavowed those two phrases and Allah clarified the abrogation of Satan's cast."
"11187","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dictated to me from: We created man from an essence of clay (23:12) to: then We produced him as another creation (23:14). Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه exclaimed: blessed be Allah, the Best of creators, so the Messenger ﷺ laughed and told him the verse is sealed with those very words. > **""With it it was sealed: blessed be Allah, the Best of creators.""**"
"11188","Murrah al-Zuhrī رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that al-ramlah in the verse of refuge means the elevated mound. > **""Al-ramlah is the mound.""**"
"11189","Abū Khalaf, freedman of Banū Jumaḥ, entered with ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr upon ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers رضي الله عنها, in the portico of Zamzam. He asked how the Messenger ﷺ recited: those who give what they give (23:60) — is it yuʾtūn or yaʾtūn? She asked which he preferred, he swore one is dearer to him than the world, and she testified the Prophet ﷺ recited it as yaʾtūn, as it was revealed, the spelling being a scribal matter."
"11190","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما used to read: in arrogance toward it, conversing by night with evil speech (23:67), and said the idolaters spoke insolently about the Messenger ﷺ in their poetry."
"11191","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb came to the Prophet ﷺ pleading by Allah that they had eaten al-ʿilhiz — hides and blood — from hunger, whereupon Allah revealed: We seized them with punishment, yet they did not humble themselves to their Lord nor supplicate (23:76)."
"11192","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning: the Fire scorches their faces and they therein are grimacing (23:104): have you not seen heads scorched, teeth exposed, lips shriveled back."
"11193","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: a Muslim man sought the Messenger's ﷺ permission to marry a woman called Umm Mahzūl, who committed immorality and stipulated she would spend on him. The Prophet ﷺ recited to him: the adulterer marries none but an adulteress or polytheist woman, and none marries her but an adulterer or polytheist (24:3). > **""The adulterer marries none but an adulteress or a polytheist woman.""**"
"11194","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: when those who accuse chaste women then bring not four witnesses — flog them eighty lashes (24:4) was revealed, Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah رضي الله عنه, chief of the Anṣār, asked if thus it was revealed. The Prophet ﷺ asked the Anṣār if they heard their chief. They excused his jealousy — he married only virgins and none dared marry his divorcees. Saʿd swore he knew it true from Allah, yet wondered how he could bring four witnesses before a man finished with his wife. > **""O company of the Anṣār, do you not hear what your chief says?""**"
"11195","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه what he would do if he saw a man with Umm Rūmān; he swore he would do him harm. He asked ʿUmar رضي الله عنه, who swore he would kill him and curse the wretch. Then was revealed: those who accuse their wives with no witnesses but themselves (24:6). > **""If you saw a man with Umm Rūmān, what would you do with him? And you, O ʿUmar?""**"
"11196","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما expounded the Ifk passage (24:11-19) verse by verse: those who brought the lie about ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers رضي الله عنها, were four; it was good for the Prophet ﷺ, for the mistress of believing women, for Abū Bakr, her mother, and Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal; the bearer of its greatest part was ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy, flogged eighty in this world with the Fire in the next; the believers — Zaynab, Barīrah, and the Prophet's wives — said it is manifest slander; without four witnesses they are liars; were it not for Allah's grace you would have been touched; tongues circulating what you know not is grave with Allah; and loving scandal spread brings painful punishment."
"11197","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: the one who took upon himself its greatest part (24:11) — its enormity among the accusers — is Ibn Ubayy, chief of the hypocrites, who said she was not cleared nor he of her. In this is a lesson: whoever aids a sin by act, word, insinuation, admiration, or approval shares it proportionately; whoever witnesses and detests is as the absent, and whoever is absent yet approves is as the witness."
"11198","Hishām ibn ʿUrwah رحمه الله said: those who bore its greatest part were ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl, Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah, Ḥassān, and Ḥamnah bint Jaḥsh رضي الله عنهم, with the greatest burden from Ibn Ubayy."
"11199","Qatādah رحمه الله said concerning: why, when you heard it (24:12) — you should have denied it and said it is manifest falsehood. By my life, to belie evil about your brother upon hearing it is better and safer than spreading, publicizing, and affirming it."
"11200","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: why, when you heard it (24:12) — the accusation of ʿĀʾishah and Ṣafwān رضي الله عنهما — did you not belie it? Why did not believing men and women, among them Zaynab bint Jaḥsh رضي الله عنها, think good of one another, having seen nothing, and say: this accusation is a manifest lie?"
"11201","Ibn Jurayj رحمه الله said concerning: why, when you heard it, did believing men and women think good (24:12) — meaning one saying to another: have you not heard what was said."
"11202","Abū Ṣakhr رحمه الله said concerning: why did they not bring four witnesses; since they brought none, they are liars before Allah (24:13). Everyone who accuses a Muslim then brings not four witnesses is an accuser subject to the ḥadd of qadhf."
"11203","Qatādah رحمه الله said concerning: were it not for Allah's grace and mercy, you would have been touched for what you indulged in with great punishment (24:14). This is concerning ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها; the Companions of the Messenger ﷺ nearly perished over what was said."
"11204","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: when you received it upon your tongues (24:15) — when they indulged over ʿĀʾishah, one saying I heard so-and-so say such. One narrates from another — I heard from so-and-so — saying with mouths, with tongues, what you know not to be true, reckoning accusation light while with Allah it is grave falsehood."
"11205","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: why, when you heard it (24:12) — the accusation — did you not say: it is not for us to speak of this, glory be to You, this is great slander. Why did you not speak as Saʿd ibn Muʿādh al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه spoke upon hearing it: glory be to You, this is great slander — a lie that stuns its hearer, asserting what never was."
"11206","With the same chain, Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: Allah admonishes you never to return to the like of it (24:17) — meaning accusation of adultery."
"11207","With the same chain from him رحمه الله concerning: those, meaning between the accusation of ʿĀʾishah, who love scandal to spread (24:19) — that adultery spread and appear among those who believed, meaning Ṣafwān and ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنهما. For them is painful punishment in this world and the next: Ibn Ubayy's punishment in this world was flogging, in the next the Fire; and Allah knows while you know not."
"11208","Mujāhid رحمه الله said concerning: Allah admonishes you never to return to the like of it (24:17): He forbids you."
"11209","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: O believers, follow not Satan's footsteps (24:21) — his beautification; whoever follows it, he enjoins indecency — sins — and the repudiated, like what was said of ʿĀʾishah. Were it not for Allah's bounty — His blessing — none of you would ever be purified — rectified — but Allah purifies — rectifies — whom He wills."
"11210","Mujāhid رحمه الله said concerning: let not those of bounty and means swear (24:22). Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه swore to benefit no more an orphan under his care — Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah, who spread it. When was revealed: do you not love that Allah forgive you, Abū Bakr said: yes, I love Allah to forgive me, and I shall be better to orphans. In another narration the Prophet ﷺ asked him this, commanded pardon, and he pardoned, vowing never to withhold kindness. > **""Do you not love that Allah forgive you? Then pardon and overlook.""**"
"11211","Khuṣayf رحمه الله said: I asked Saʿīd ibn Jubayr which is graver, adultery or accusing a chaste woman? He said adultery. I cited: those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women (24:23). He replied: this was revealed specifically concerning ʿĀʾishah."
"11212","Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Muzāḥim رحمه الله said: this verse — those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women (24:23) — was revealed specifically concerning the wives of the Prophet ﷺ."
"11213","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما read Sūrat al-Nūr with commentary until: those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women are cursed in this world and the next, with great punishment (24:23). He said: this is concerning ʿĀʾishah and the Prophet's ﷺ wives — no repentance assigned for their accuser — while whoever accuses other believing women may repent, as in: save those who repent thereafter and reform (24:5). Some rose to kiss his head for the excellence of his exposition."
"11214","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: those who accuse chaste women (24:23) — charging chastity with adultery; the heedless of indecency, meaning ʿĀʾishah; the truthful believers; cursed — flogged in this world, while Ibn Ubayy is tormented in the Fire as a hypocrite. The Prophet ﷺ flogged Ḥassān, Ibn Ubayy, Misṭaḥ, and Ḥamnah رضي الله عنهم eighty each for accusing ʿĀʾishah; all repented thereafter except Ibn Ubayy, chief of hypocrites, who died in hypocrisy."
"11215","Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah رضي الله عنه said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wipe his hand upon his thigh reciting: that Day Allah will pay them in full their true recompense, and they will know Allah is the Manifest Truth (24:25). > **""That Day Allah will pay them in full their true recompense, and they will know Allah is the Manifest Truth.""**"
"11216","Qatādah رحمه الله said concerning: that Day Allah will pay them in full their true recompense (24:25) — the people of truth their truth and the people of falsehood their falsehood — and they will know Allah is the Manifest Truth."
"11217","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said: that Day — in the Hereafter — Allah will pay them in full their true recompense (24:25) — their just reckoning, wronging them not — and they will know Allah is the Manifest Truth — the Manifest Just."
"11218","With the same chain, Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: evil words for evil people (24:26) — foul speech, as the accusation of ʿĀʾishah and the like, belongs to evil men and women who uttered it; evil people deserve evil speech, for it befits them. Then: good words — fine speech befits the good, for it befits them."
"11219","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam رحمه الله said concerning: evil women for evil men, evil men for evil women; good women for good men, good men for good women (24:26). It was revealed over ʿĀʾishah when the hypocrite accused her with slander, and Allah cleared her. Ibn Ubayy was evil, most deserving of the evil one; the Messenger ﷺ was good, most deserving of the good one; ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها was good, most deserving of the good one."
"11220","Mujāhid رحمه الله said concerning: evil words for evil people (24:26). Evil speech belongs to evil people and evil people to evil speech; good speech to good people and good people to good speech. In the other narration: fine speech belongs to believers and foul speech to disbelievers."
"11221","In the other narration he added: fine speech belongs to the believers and foul speech belongs to the disbelievers."
"11222","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning: evil women for evil men (24:26). Evil speech belongs to evil men and evil men to evil speech; good speech to good men. It was revealed over those who uttered slander about the Prophet's ﷺ wife. It is also said: evil deeds belong to evil people, and good deeds to good people."
"11223","Qatādah رحمه الله said concerning: evil for evil and evil for evil (24:26) — in word and deed; and good for good and good for good — in word and deed."
"11224","Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtaybah رحمه الله said: when people indulged over ʿĀʾishah, the Messenger ﷺ sent for her. She came trembling without fever. He asked what people were saying; she swore by Him who sent him with truth she would offer no excuse until her vindication descended from heaven. Allah revealed fifteen verses of Sūrat al-Nūr. Then he read to: evil women for evil men and good for good (24:26) — evil women for evil men, and good women for good men. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, what are the people saying?""**"
"11225","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr رحمه الله said concerning: those — the good men — are cleared of what they say (24:26) — what the accusers who accused ʿĀʾishah say; they are free of foul speech. For them is forgiveness for sins and noble provision — good in Paradise. When ʿĀʾishah's vindication descended, the Messenger ﷺ drew her to himself; she is among his wives in Paradise."
"11226","Mujāhid رحمه الله said concerning: those are cleared of what they say (24:26). Whoever is good is cleared of every foul word by Allah's forgiveness; whoever is evil is cleared of every righteous word — Allah returns it to him, unaccepted."
"11227","Mujāhid رحمه الله said concerning: those are cleared of what they say (24:26). Whatever good word a disbeliever utters belongs to believers; whatever foul word a believer utters belongs to disbelievers — each cleared of speech not rightfully his."
"11228","Qatādah رحمه الله said concerning: those are cleared of what they say (24:26) — in word and deed. For them is forgiveness and noble provision — forgiveness of sins, which is Paradise."
"11229","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam رحمه الله said concerning: those are cleared of what they say (24:26). Here ʿĀʾishah was cleared — for them is forgiveness and noble provision."
"11230","ʿAbdullāh — Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه — said concerning: display not their adornment (24:31). Adornment is bracelet, armlet, anklet, earring, ear ornament, necklace, and what shows thereof over clothes and cloak."
"11231","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: Ibn Ubayy had a slave girl who committed adultery in Jāhiliyyah. When adultery was forbidden, he demanded she continue; she swore by Allah never to commit it again. Then was revealed: compel not your slave girls to prostitution (24:33)."
"11232","Anas رضي الله عنه said: Ibn Ubayy had a slave girl called Muʿādhah whom he compelled to adultery. When Islam came, was revealed: compel not your slave girls to prostitution (24:33) to: Allah after their compulsion is Forgiving, Merciful."
"11233","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said concerning: as a niche wherein is a lamp (24:35). The niche is the breast of Muḥammad ﷺ, the glass his heart, the lamp the light in his heart, kindled from a blessed tree — Ibrāhīm عليه السلام — an olive neither eastern nor western — neither Jewish nor Christian — as in: Ibrāhīm was neither Jew nor Christian but upright Muslim (3:67)."
"11234","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه saw market people hear the adhān, abandon their wares, and rise to prayer, and said: these are those of whom Allah said: neither trade nor sale diverts them from remembrance of Allah (24:37)."
"11235","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: they were merchants whom neither trade nor sale diverted from remembrance of Allah."
"11236","Concerning: no blame upon them to lay aside their garments (24:60), he said: the outer cloak."
"11237","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه said: when the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions came to Madinah and the Anṣār sheltered them, the Arabs aimed at them with a single bow. Then was revealed: He will surely establish them in the earth (24:55)."
"11238","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said: Muslims wished to march with the Messenger ﷺ, entrusting keys to guardians and permitting them to eat as they liked; the guardians deemed it unlawful without willing consent. Then Allah revealed: no blame on the blind, the lame, the sick, nor yourselves to eat from your houses, your fathers', mothers', brothers', sisters', uncles', aunts', maternal uncles', maternal aunts' (24:61) to: or what you hold keys to."
"11239","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه said: I saw the Messenger ﷺ recite this verse at the end of Sūrat al-Nūr, placing his fingers beneath his eyes, saying: with all things Seeing — though the recitation is with all things Knowing. > **""With all things Seeing.""**"
"11240","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: for years in the Messenger's ﷺ era we recited: those who invoke not another god with Allah, nor kill the soul Allah forbade save by right, nor commit adultery (25:68). Then was revealed: save him who repents (25:70). I never saw the Prophet ﷺ rejoice more intensely than at it and at: We granted you a manifest victory."
"11241","ʿAbdullāh — Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه — reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited: whoever does that shall meet athāman (25:68). > **""Whoever does that shall meet athāman.""**"
"11242","Maʿdīkarib said: we came to ʿAbdullāh — Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه — asking him to recite Ṭā-Sīn-Mīm of two hundred verses. He said: it is not with me; take it from him who took it from the Messenger ﷺ — Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt رضي الله عنه. So we went to Khabbāb and he recited it to us."
"11243","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning: among them wretched and blessed, and the like in the Qur'an. The Messenger ﷺ was eager that all people believe and pledge him upon guidance, so Allah informed him only he believes for whom happiness preceded in the first Decree, and only he strays for whom wretchedness preceded. Then Allah said to His Prophet ﷺ: perhaps you will destroy yourself that they believe not — if We willed We would send a sign from heaven before which their necks bow (26:3-4)."
"11244","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to read: this is naught but the custom of the ancients — everything they contrived — meaning everything they invented."
"11245","Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām رضي الله عنه said: when warn your nearest kin (26:214) was revealed, the Messenger ﷺ cried out atop Abū Qubays: O family of ʿAbd Manāf, I am a warner. Quraysh came; he warned them. They demanded miracles like Solomon's wind, Moses' sea, Jesus' raising the dead — move mountains, gush rivers, raise our dead, turn this rock to gold. Revelation descended; when lifted he swore he had been granted what they asked if he willed, but chose mercy's door that believers believe — warning if granted then disbelieved they would suffer unmatched punishment. Then was revealed: nothing withheld Us from sending signs but ancients belied them (17:59), and: if a Qur'an moved mountains (13:31). > **""O family of ʿAbd Manāf, I am a warner. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I was granted what you asked, but I chose the door of mercy that your believers may believe.""**"
"11246","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: when warn your nearest kin (26:214) was revealed, the Messenger ﷺ gathered Banū Hāshim at the door and his wives and household inside, then addressed them: O Banū Hāshim, ransom yourselves from the Fire, liberate your necks, save yourselves from Allah, for I own nothing for you against Allah. Then to his household — ʿĀʾishah, Ḥafṣah, Umm Salamah, Fāṭimah, and Umm al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهن: ransom yourselves, for I avail you nothing. ʿĀʾishah wept asking if a day comes he avails nothing; he named three stations — the just Scales (21:47), the Light, and the Path sharp as a razor's edge with angels snatching by hooks crying Lord save, save. > **""O Banū Hāshim, ransom yourselves from the Fire, for I own nothing for you against Allah. O ʿĀʾishah, Ḥafṣah, Umm Salamah, Fāṭimah — ransom yourselves, for I avail you nothing against Allah.""**"
"11247","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning: and your turning among those who prostrate (26:219). From the loin of a prophet to the loin of a prophet until you became a prophet."
"11248","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: do not leave the mosque until I teach you a verse from a sūrah none received before me save Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd عليهما السلام. At the threshold he asked with what he opens prayer and recitation; he said: with In the Name of Allah, the Merciful, the Compassionate. He said: it is it — then stepped out. > **""Do not leave the mosque until I teach you a verse none received before me save Sulaymān. With what do you open your prayer? It is it.""**"
"11249","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning: peace upon His servants whom He chose. They are the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, whom Allah chose for His Prophet."
"11250","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I asked Jibrīl عليه السلام which of the two terms Mūsā fulfilled; he said: the more complete and perfect of them. > **""I asked Jibrīl which of the two terms Mūsā fulfilled; he said: the more complete and perfect.""**"
"11251","ʿUtbah ibn al-Nuddar رضي الله عنه reported: the Messenger ﷺ was asked which term Mūsā fulfilled; he said: the most pious and complete. When Mūsā عليه السلام would part from Shuʿayb عليه السلام, his wife asked her father for sheep to live by; he granted what his sheep bore that year of altered color. Mūsā struck each ewe's side with his staff, all bore altered colors — thirty-two — with no defective milker among them. He ﷺ said: when you conquer Syria you will find their remnants — the Sāmiriyyah. > **""The most pious and complete of them. When you conquer Syria you will find their remnants — the Sāmiriyyah.""**"
"11252","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported: the Messenger ﷺ was asked which term Mūsā fulfilled; he said: the most complete and pious. And if asked which of the two women he married, say: the younger of them. > **""The most complete and pious of them. If asked which woman he married, say: the younger.""**"
"11253","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه raised to the Prophet ﷺ: Allah did not destroy a people by punishment from heaven or earth save after Mūsā. Then he recited: We gave Mūsā the Book after We destroyed the former generations (28:43) — meaning no village was transformed after the Torah. > **""Allah did not destroy a people by punishment from heaven or earth save after Mūsā.""**"
"11254","Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir said: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reciting this verse as *sāḥirāni taẓāharā* — two magicians supporting one another — in reference to Mūsā and Hārūn عليهما السلام."
"11255","Rifāʿah al-Quraẓī رضي الله عنه said: This verse was revealed concerning ten men, and I am one of them: *And We have conveyed the Word to them that they might remember* [al-Qaṣaṣ: 51]."
"11256","Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī Abū Jaʿfar said: I asked Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه about the saying of Allah, *Indeed He who imposed the Qurʾān upon you will return you to a place of return* [al-Qaṣaṣ: 85]. He said: His return is his Hereafter."
"11257","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning *Indeed He who imposed the Qurʾān upon you will return you to a place of return* [al-Qaṣaṣ: 85]: Your place of return is Paradise."
"11258","In another narration from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما concerning *to a place of return*: It means death."
"11259","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, So-and-so prays at night, but in the morning he steals. He ﷺ said: > **""What you mention will soon forbid him.""**"
"11260","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Al-biḍʿ is between seven and ten.""** This explains the word *biḍʿa sinīn* in Sūrat al-Rūm."
"11261","Niyār ibn Mukram رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Al-biḍʿ is between three and nine.""**"
"11262","Abū Razīn said: Nāfiʿ ibn al-Azraq disputed with Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, asking, Do you find the five prayers in the Book of Allah? He said: Yes. Then he recited to him: *So glorify Allah when you enter evening* — this is Maghrib; *and when you enter morning* — this is Fajr; *and in the late afternoon* — this is ʿAṣr; *and when you enter noon* — this is Ẓuhr; and *after the ʿIshāʾ prayer* — this is ʿIshāʾ."
"11263","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning *Allah is He who sends the winds, stirring cloud, then spreading it in the sky as He wills and making it fragments* [al-Rūm: 48]: *Kisafan* means pieces, one above another. *And you see the rain emerging from within it* means emerging from between it."
"11264","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Five are known only to Allah: Indeed with Allah is knowledge of the Hour, He sends down rain, He knows what is in the wombs, no soul knows what it will earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in which land it will die. Indeed Allah is Knowing, Aware.""**"
"11265","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited *Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope* [al-Sajdah: 16], and said: > **""It is the standing of the servant at night.""**"
"11266","Bilāl رضي الله عنه said: When this verse was revealed, *Their sides forsake their beds* [al-Sajdah: 16], we were sitting in a gathering while some Companions of the Prophet ﷺ prayed voluntary prayer from Maghrib until ʿIshāʾ, and this verse was revealed concerning them."
"11267","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: It is written in the Torah that for those whose sides forsake their beds there is what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never occurred to a human heart. And in the Qurʾān it is: *No soul knows what is hidden for them of comfort of eyes* [al-Sajdah: 17]."
"11268","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning *And We shall surely make them taste the nearer punishment before the greater punishment, that they may return* [al-Sajdah: 21]: It refers to those among them who remain, or who repent and return."
"11269","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Three deeds, whoever does them has committed a crime: whoever raises a banner without right, whoever disobeys his parents, or whoever walks with a tyrant has committed a crime. Allah says: Indeed We shall take retribution from the criminals.""** [al-Sajdah: 22]"
"11270","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning *And We made him a guidance for the Children of Israel* [al-Sajdah: 23]: > **""He made Mūsā a guidance for the Children of Israel.""** And concerning *So be not in doubt of meeting Him*: > **""Of Mūsā meeting his Lord, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"11271","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: Allah took the covenant of the prophets over their peoples, concerning *And when We took from the prophets their covenant* [al-Aḥzāb: 7]."
"11272","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه said: This verse, *Allah intends only to remove impurity from you, O people of the Household, and to purify you thoroughly* [al-Aḥzāb: 33], was revealed concerning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ʿAlī, Fāṭimah, al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn رضي الله عنهم."
"11273","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The women said, O Messenger of Allah, why are believing men mentioned while believing women are not mentioned? So this was revealed: *Indeed the Muslim men and Muslim women, and the believing men and believing women* [al-Aḥzāb: 35]."
"11274","Qatādah said concerning *When you said to him upon whom Allah bestowed favor and upon whom you bestowed favor* [al-Aḥzāb: 37]: He is Zayd ibn Ḥārithah رضي الله عنه; Allah favored him with Islam and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ favored him by freeing him. His saying *Keep your wife and fear Allah, while you concealed within yourself what Allah would reveal* means he concealed his wish that Zayd would divorce her. Al-Ḥasan said: No verse was harder upon him than *while you concealed within yourself*; had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concealed anything of revelation he would have concealed it. *And you feared people while Allah is more worthy that you fear Him* means he feared people's talk. *When Zayd had concluded from her* means when Zayd divorced her, *We married her to you*. Zaynab bint Jaḥsh رضي الله عنها used to boast over the wives of the Prophet ﷺ: Your fathers married you, while the Lord of the Throne married me. When Zayd said, O Prophet of Allah, her character is hard upon me and I shall divorce her, the Prophet ﷺ said to him: *Keep your wife and fear Allah*."
"11275","Qatādah said: The Prophet ﷺ proposed to Zaynab رضي الله عنها, daughter of his paternal aunt, intending her for Zayd. She thought he intended her for himself; when she learned he intended her for Zayd, she refused. Then Allah revealed *It is not for a believing man or woman, when Allah and His Messenger decree a matter, to have choice in their affair* [al-Aḥzāb: 36]. So she accepted and submitted."
"11276","Ibn Jurayj said concerning *And the command of Allah is a decree already ordained* [al-Aḥzāb: 38]: This is from His way concerning Dāwūd عليه السلام and the woman, and concerning the Prophet ﷺ and Zaynab رضي الله عنها."
"11277","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: When *O Prophet, indeed We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings and a warner* [al-Aḥzāb: 45] was revealed, the Prophet ﷺ called ʿAlī رضي الله عنه and Muʿādh رضي الله عنه, whom he had ordered to go to Yemen, and said: > **""Go forth, give glad tidings and do not repel, make easy and do not make difficult. For there has been revealed to me: O Prophet, indeed We have sent you as a witness over your nation, and a bearer of glad tidings of Paradise, and a warner of the Fire, and a caller to Allah — to the testimony that there is no deity but Allah — by His permission, and as an illuminating lamp by the Qurʾān.""**"
"11278","ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn said concerning *And a believing woman if she offers herself to the Prophet* [al-Aḥzāb: 50]: She is Umm Sharīk al-Azdiyyah رضي الله عنها, who offered herself to the Prophet ﷺ."
"11279","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: In Jāhiliyyah, wife-exchange meant a man saying to another, Exchange your wife with me for my wife — you leave yours for me and I leave mine for you. So Allah revealed *Nor that you exchange them for other wives, even if their beauty pleases you* [al-Aḥzāb: 52]. ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها was with him, without permission. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Where is seeking permission?""** He said, O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I have never sought permission from any man of Muḍar since I grew up. Then he asked, Who is this fair-skinned woman beside you? He ﷺ said: > **""This is ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers.""** He said, Shall I not leave for you the most beautiful in character? He ﷺ said: > **""O ʿUyaynah, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has forbidden that.""** When he left, ʿĀʾishah asked, Who is this? He ﷺ said: > **""A foolish man who is obeyed, yet as you see he is the chief of his people.""**"
"11280","Ziyād al-Anṣārī said: I asked Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه, If all the wives of the Prophet ﷺ had died, would it be lawful for him to marry? He asked, What would forbid him that? I said, His saying *No women are lawful for you after this* [al-Aḥzāb: 52]. He said: Only a certain class of women was made lawful for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"11281","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said: I was eating with the Prophet ﷺ from one bowl when ʿUmar passed by. He called him and he ate; his finger touched my finger, and he said, O how sensitive — if you were obeyed concerning them, no eye would ever see you. Then the verse of hijab was revealed."
"11282","Anas رضي الله عنه said: When the verse of hijab was revealed, I came to enter as I used to enter, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: > **""Remain behind, O my son.""**"
"11283","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما said: They asked, O Messenger of Allah, what of the saying of Allah, *Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet* [al-Aḥzāb: 56]? He ﷺ said: > **""This is among the concealed matters; had you not asked me I would not have informed you. Indeed Allah has entrusted me with two angels: I am never mentioned before a Muslim servant who then sends blessings upon me except that those two angels say, May Allah forgive you, and Allah and His angels say in answer to them: Āmīn.""**"
"11284","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Mūsā was a shy man. He came to water to bathe and placed his clothes upon a rock, hardly ever exposing his nakedness. The Children of Israel said: Mūsā is afflicted with a swelling, meaning that is why he does not remove his clothes. So the rock carried his clothes until it stood before the gatherings of the Children of Israel, and they saw Mūsā as the most handsome of men. That is His saying: So Allah cleared him of what they said, and he was honored before Allah.""** [al-Aḥzāb: 69]"
"11285","ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer, then said: > **""Remain in your places.""** Then he came to the men and said: > **""Indeed Allah has commanded me to command you to fear Allah and to speak upright speech.""** Then he went among the women and said to them: > **""Allah has commanded me to command you to fear Allah and to speak upright speech.""** This is concerning *O you who believe, fear Allah and speak upright speech* [al-Aḥzāb: 70]."
"11286","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ about Sabāʾ: Is it a man, a woman, or a land? He ﷺ said: > **""Rather it is a man who fathered ten. Six of them settled in Yemen and four in al-Shām. As for the Yemenis: Madhḥij, Kindah, al-Azd, al-Ashʿariyyūn, Anmār and Ḥimyar — all Arabs. As for the Syrians: Lakhm, Judhām, ʿĀmilah and Ghassān.""**"
"11287","Yazīd ibn Ḥuṣayn al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه reported that a man asked, O Messenger of Allah, what is Sabāʾ — a prophet or a woman? He ﷺ said: > **""He was a man from the Arabs.""** He asked, What children did he have? He ﷺ said: > **""He fathered ten: six settled in Yemen and four in al-Shām. Those in Yemen: Kindah, Madhḥij, al-Azd, al-Ashʿariyyūn, Anmār and Ḥimyar. In al-Shām: Lakhm, Judhām, ʿĀmilah and Ghassān.""**"
"11288","Al-Nawwās ibn Samʿān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When Allah intends to reveal a matter, He speaks the revelation. When He speaks it, the heaven is seized with violent trembling from fear of Allah. When its people hear it they fall unconscious and prostrate. The first to raise his head is Jibrīl عليه السلام. Allah addresses him from His revelation with what He wills. Jibrīl passes with it over the angels; whenever he passes a heaven its people ask, What did our Lord say, O Jibrīl? He says: The truth, and He is the Most High, the Most Great. They all repeat what Jibrīl said. Then Jibrīl carries it to where he was commanded in heaven and earth.""** This is concerning *They said: The truth, and He is the Most High, the Most Great* [Sabāʾ: 23]."
"11289","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning *Among them is one wronging himself, among them is one moderate, and among them is one foremost in good deeds by Allah's permission* [Fāṭir: 32]: > **""Those who are foremost are those who enter Paradise without reckoning. As for the moderate, they are those judged with an easy reckoning. As for those who wronged themselves, they are detained long in the gathering, then Allah meets them with His mercy. They are those who will say: Praise be to Allah who has removed sorrow from us; indeed our Lord is Forgiving, Appreciative — who has settled us in the abode of permanence by His bounty; no toil touches us therein nor weariness.""** [Fāṭir: 34-35]"
"11290","ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Azdī reported from a Syrian that he entered the Mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, prayed two rakʿahs, and said, O Allah, comfort my loneliness, have mercy on my estrangement, join my solitude, and bring me a righteous man to benefit me. A man sat beside him. When he finished, the Syrian asked, Who are you? He said, Abū al-Dardāʾ. Why did you do what I saw? So he informed him of his supplication. He said, If you are truthful, I am happier with your supplication than you. Shall I not narrate to you a hadith as a gift? I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Allah said: Then We caused to inherit the Book those We chose from Our servants. As for those who are foremost, they are those who enter Paradise without reckoning.""** Then he mentioned the like of the preceding hadith."
"11291","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning *Among them is one wronging himself, among them is one moderate, and among them is one foremost in good deeds* [Fāṭir: 32]: > **""The foremost in good and the moderate enter Paradise without reckoning, while the wrongdoer is judged with an easy reckoning and then enters Paradise.""**"
"11292","ʿAwf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My nation is in three thirds: a third enter Paradise without reckoning, a third are judged with an easy reckoning then enter Paradise, and a third are sifted and examined. Then the angels come and say: We found them saying, There is no deity but Allah alone. He says: They have spoken truly; there is no deity but I. Admit them to Paradise by their saying, There is no deity but Allah alone, and lay their sins upon the deniers. That is His saying: And they shall surely bear their burdens and other burdens with their burdens [al-ʿAnkabūt: 13]. Its confirmation is where the angels are mentioned. Allah said: Then We caused to inherit the Book those We chose from Our servants [Fāṭir: 32]. So He made them three groups: among them the self-wronger — he who is examined and sifted; among them the moderate — he who is judged easily; among them the foremost in good — he who enters Paradise without reckoning or punishment, by Allah's permission. They all enter it together, unseparated, adorned therein with bracelets of gold and pearl, their garments therein of silk. And they will say: Praise be to Allah who removed sorrow from us; indeed our Lord is Forgiving, Appreciative — who settled us in the abode of permanence by His bounty. But those who disbelieve, for them is the Fire of Hell; no decree of death for them nor lightening of its torment. Thus We recompense every ingrate.""** [Fāṭir: 34-36]"
"11293","Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited *Among them is one wronging himself and among them is one moderate* [Fāṭir: 32], and said: > **""All of them are from this nation.""**"
"11294","ʿUqbah ibn Ṣuhbān said: I asked ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها about *Then We caused to inherit the Book those We chose from Our servants; among them is one wronging himself, among them is one moderate, and among them is one foremost in good deeds by Allah's permission* [Fāṭir: 32]. She said: As for the foremost, they passed in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he bore witness to Paradise for them. As for the moderate, they are those who follow their traces and act like them until joining them. As for the self-wronger, he is like me and you and those who follow us — yet all of them are in Paradise."
"11295","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the Day of Resurrection comes, it will be called out: Where are the sons of sixty? And that is the age concerning which Allah said: Did We not give you life long enough that whoever would remember might remember therein.""** [Fāṭir: 37]"
"11296","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The beetle nearly perishes in its hole due to the sins of the children of Adam. Then he recited *And if Allah were to seize people for what they earn, He would leave no creature upon its back* [Fāṭir: 45]."
"11297","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites Yā-Sīn in a day and night, seeking the Face of Allah, is forgiven.""**"
"11298","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever persists in reciting Yā-Sīn every night and then dies, dies as a martyr.""**"
"11299","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Anṣār lived far from the mosque and wished to move nearer to it. Then *And We record what they send forth and their traces* [Yā-Sīn: 12] was revealed, so they remained in their homes."
"11300","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""While the people of Paradise are in their bliss, a light shines upon them. They raise their heads, and behold their Lord, Blessed and Exalted, has looked upon them. He says: Peace be upon you, O people of Paradise. That is His saying: Peace — a word from a Merciful Lord. He looks to them and they look to Him, turning to no other bliss while they gaze at Him, and His light remains in their dwellings.""** [Yā-Sīn: 58]"
"11301","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning *By those ranged in ranks* [al-Ṣāffāt: 1]: They are the angels. *By those driving sternly*: They are the angels. *By those reciting remembrance* [al-Ṣāffāt: 3]: They are the angels."
"11302","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""When Allah intended to confine Yūnus in the belly of the fish, He revealed to the fish: Do not scratch his flesh nor break his bone. It took him down to its dwelling in the sea. When it reached the depths, Yūnus heard a sound and wondered within himself, What is this? So Allah revealed to him while in the belly of the fish: This is the glorification of the creatures of the earth. So he glorified while in the belly of the fish. The angels heard his glorification and said, Our Lord, we hear a faint voice in a strange land. He said: That is My servant Yūnus; he disobeyed Me so I confined him in the belly of the fish in the sea. They said, The righteous servant from whom righteous deeds ascended to You day and night? He said: Yes. So they interceded for him, and He commanded the fish, which cast him upon the shore, as Allah said: While he was ill.""** [al-Ṣāffāt: 145]"
"11303","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: In the seven heavens there is no heaven wherein there is a handspan without the forehead of an angel or his feet standing. Then he recited *And indeed we are those ranged, and indeed we are those who glorify* [al-Ṣāffāt: 165-166]."
"11304","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning *And he overcame me in speech* [Ṣād: 23]: Dāwūd عليه السلام added nothing beyond saying, Entrust her to me."
"11305","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: I used to pass this verse without knowing what *the evening and the ishrāq* meant, until Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنها narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her, called for ablution water in a bowl as though I see the trace of dough in it, performed ablution, then stood and prayed the forenoon prayer, and said: > **""O Umm Hāniʾ, this is the prayer of ishrāq.""** This is concerning *They glorified with him in the evening and at sunrise* [Ṣād: 18]."
"11306","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning *So he began striking their legs and necks* [Ṣād: 33]: > **""He cut their legs and necks.""**"
"11307","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A son was born to Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd عليهما السلام, so he said to the devils: Where shall we hide him from death? They said: Let us take him to the east. He said: Death will reach him. They said: To the west. He said: Death will reach him. They said: To the seas. He said: It will reach him. They said: Let us place him between heaven and earth. Then the Angel of Death descended and said, O son of Dāwūd, I was commanded to seize a soul; I sought it in the east and found it not, in the west and found it not, in the seas and the ends of the earth and found it not. While ascending I found it, so I seized it. Its body came until it fell upon his throne. That is the saying of Allah: And We tried Sulaymān and placed upon his throne a body; then he turned.""** [Ṣād: 34]"
"11308","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning *Gentle wherever it struck*: *Rukhāʾ* means obedient, and *wherever it struck* [Ṣād: 36] means wherever he intended."
"11309","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning *Our Lord, whoever brought this upon us, increase him double punishment in the Fire* [Ṣād: 61]: They are vipers and serpents."
"11310","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: For a time we thought this verse was revealed concerning us and the People of the Book before us: *Indeed you will die and indeed they will die; then on the Day of Resurrection you will dispute before your Lord* [al-Zumar: 30-31]. We said, How shall we dispute when our Prophet is one and our Book is one? Until I saw some of us striking the faces of others with the sword, and I knew it was revealed concerning us."
"11311","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said: When *Indeed you will die and indeed they will die; then on the Day of Resurrection you will dispute before your Lord* [al-Zumar: 30-31] was revealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, al-Zubayr asked, Will what occurred between us in this world be repeated for us? He ﷺ said: > **""Yes, it shall be repeated until every possessor of a right is given his right.""** Al-Zubayr said, By Allah, then the matter is severe."
"11312","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning *Allah takes souls at their death* [al-Zumar: 42]: The souls of the living and the dead meet in sleep and question one another; Allah withholds the souls of the dead and sends the souls of the living back to their bodies."
"11313","Thawbān, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""I would not love to have the world and all within it in exchange for this verse: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, despair not of the mercy of Allah; indeed Allah forgives all sins; indeed He is the Forgiving, the Merciful.""** [al-Zumar: 53] A man asked, Even one who associated partners? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Except one who associated partners.""**"
"11314","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb, killer of Ḥamzah رضي الله عنه, inviting him to Islam. He replied, O Muḥammad, how do you invite me while you claim that whoever kills, associates partners, or commits adultery shall meet punishment, multiplied for him on the Day of Resurrection wherein he abides humiliated — and I have done that? Is there any concession for me? So Allah revealed *Except whoever repents, believes and does righteous work — Allah will change their evils into good, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful* [al-Furqān: 70]. Waḥshī said, O Muḥammad, this is a hard condition — except whoever repents and believes and does righteous work — perhaps I cannot do it. So Allah revealed *Indeed Allah does not forgive association with Him, but forgives less than that for whom He wills* [al-Nisāʾ: 48]. Waḥshī said, O Muḥammad, this depends upon His will; I know not whether I am forgiven. Is there other than this? So Allah revealed *O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, despair not of the mercy of Allah; indeed Allah forgives all sins* [al-Zumar: 53]. Waḥshī said, This is good — and he embraced Islam. The people asked, O Messenger of Allah, we committed what Waḥshī committed? He ﷺ said: > **""It is for the Muslims generally.""**"
"11315","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: We used to say there is no repentance for one tempted who abandons his religion after Islam and knowledge. Then Allah revealed concerning them *O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, despair not of the mercy of Allah* [al-Zumar: 53]."
"11316","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting *Yes, My signs came to you but you denied them, were arrogant and were among the disbelievers* [al-Zumar: 59] with lowering of the final vowel."
"11317","Jarīr رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to a group of his Companions: > **""I shall recite to you verses from the end of Sūrat al-Zumar; whoever among you weeps, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""** So he recited from *And they did not esteem Allah His true esteem* [al-Zumar: 67] to the end of the sūrah. Some of us wept and some did not. Those who did not weep said, O Messenger of Allah, we strove to weep but could not. He ﷺ said: > **""I shall recite it to you; whoever cannot weep should feign weeping.""**"
"11318","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said concerning *Forgiver of sin, Acceptor of repentance, Severe in punishment, Owner of bounty; there is no deity but He; to Him is the return* [Ghāfir: 3]: Forgiver of sin for whoever says there is no deity but Allah; Acceptor of repentance for whoever says it; Severe in punishment for whoever says it not; Owner of bounty and riches; *There is no deity but He* — the disbelievers of Quraysh did not affirm His Oneness, so He affirmed it Himself; *To Him is the return* — the return of whoever says it, admitting him to Paradise, and the return of whoever says it not, admitting him to Hell."
"11319","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning *He knows the treachery of the eyes*: When you look at her, do you intend betrayal or not? *And what breasts conceal*: When you gain power over her, would you commit adultery with her or not? Shall I not inform you of what follows? *And Allah judges with truth*: He can reward good with good and evil with evil. *Indeed Allah is the Hearing, the Seeing* [Ghāfir: 19-20]."
"11320","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning *Our Lord, You caused us to die twice and gave us life twice* [Ghāfir: 11]: It is like that in Sūrat al-Baqarah: *You were dead and He gave you life, then He causes you to die, then gives you life, then to Him you return* [al-Baqarah: 28]."
"11321","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه said concerning *Among them are those whose stories We related to you, and among them are those whose stories We did not relate to you* [Ghāfir: 78]: Allah sent an Abyssinian servant as a prophet, and he is among those He did not relate to Muḥammad ﷺ."
"11322","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said concerning *And the day the enemies of Allah are gathered to the Fire, driven in ranks* [Fuṣṣilat: 19]: Their first shall be gathered with their last."
"11323","Maymūnah رضي الله عنها said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited *Ḥā-Mīm-ʿAyn-Sīn-Qāf* [al-Shūrā: 1], then said: > **""O Maymūnah, do you recite Ḥā-Mīm-ʿAyn-Sīn-Qāf? I had forgotten what is between its beginning and end.""** So she recited it, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited it with her."
"11324","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: We used to recite this verse: *The heavens almost split from above them* [al-Shūrā: 5]."
"11325","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning *Say: I ask you no reward for it* — for the proofs and guidance I brought you — *except* that you love Allah and seek nearness to Allah through obedience to Him: > **""Say: I ask you no reward for what I brought you of proofs and guidance, except that you love Allah and seek nearness to Him through obedience.""**"
"11326","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: When *Say: I ask you no reward except love of the near kin* [al-Shūrā: 23] was revealed, they asked, O Messenger of Allah, who are these kin whose love is obligatory upon us? He ﷺ said: > **""ʿAlī, Fāṭimah and their two sons.""**"
"11327","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Anṣār said among themselves, If only we gathered wealth for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that when he extends his hand none stands between him and it. They came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, we wish to gather for you from our wealth. So Allah revealed *Say: I ask you no reward except love of the near kin* [al-Shūrā: 23]. They left disagreeing; some said, Did you not see what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said? Others said, He said this only that we fight for his household and aid them. So Allah revealed *Or do they say he forged a lie against Allah* [al-Shūrā: 24] until *And He who accepts repentance from His servants* [al-Shūrā: 25]. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ presented repentance to them, until *And He answers those who believe and do righteous deeds and increases them from His bounty* [al-Shūrā: 26]."
"11328","ʿAlī عليه السلام said: Shall I not inform you of the most excellent verse in the Book of Allah, which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated to us: *Whatever calamity befalls you is for what your hands earned, and He pardons much* [al-Shūrā: 30]? And he ﷺ explained to me: > **""O ʿAlī, whatever befalls you of illness, punishment or trial in this world is for what your hands earned. Allah is too Noble to repeat the punishment upon them in the Hereafter, and whatever Allah pardons in this world, Allah is too Forbearing to return to it after His pardon.""**"
"11329","Amr ibn Hurayth reported: This verse was revealed concerning the People of Suffa, ""And if Allah extended provision for His servants they would transgress in the earth,"" because they had wished for worldly life."
"11330","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained Allah's saying, ""And indeed it is a remembrance for you and your people"": It means honor for you and your people."
"11331","Abu Yahya, freedman of Ibn Aqil al-Ansari, reported: Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said, I know a verse of the Quran about which no one ever asked me, and I do not know whether people knew it and did not ask, or did not notice it so as to ask. Then he began speaking to us, and when he rose we blamed one another for not asking him about it. I said, I will take care of it when he comes tomorrow. The next day I said, O Ibn Abbas, yesterday you mentioned a verse none ever asked you about, and you did not know whether people knew it or did not notice it; inform me of it. He said, Yes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Quraysh: > **""There is none worshipped besides Allah in whom there is any good.""** Quraysh knew that the Christians worship Isa ibn Maryam عليه السلام, and knew what Muhammad ﷺ said about him, so they said, O Muhammad, do you not claim that Isa son of Maryam was a prophet and a righteous servant among Allah's servants; if you are truthful then their gods must be as they say. Then Allah revealed, ""And when the son of Maryam is cited as an example, your people cry out,"" I asked, what is yasiddun. He said, they clamor. And, ""And indeed he is knowledge of the Hour."" He said, it is the coming forth of Isa ibn Maryam عليه السلام before the Day of Resurrection."
"11332","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""There is no servant except that he has two gates in heaven, a gate through which his work enters and a gate through which his work and speech go out. When he dies they miss him and weep over him.""** Then he recited, ""So heaven and earth did not weep over them."" He explained that they used to do no righteous work on earth over which it would weep for them, and no good speech or righteous work of theirs ascended to heaven that would miss them and weep over them."
"11333","Al-Dahhak reported: Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه melted silver from the treasury, then sent to the people of the mosque and said, whoever wishes to look at al-muhl, let him look at this."
"11334","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught me to recite a sura from the Ha-Mim family, meaning Al-Ahqaf. A sura with more than thirty verses used to be called thirty."
"11335","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning, ""Or a remnant of knowledge"": > **""It is writing.""** In another wording, he was asked about writing and said it is a remnant of knowledge."
"11336","In a narration in Al-Awsat, Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained His saying, ""Or a remnant of knowledge"": It means excellence in handwriting."
"11337","Awf ibn Malik al-Ashjai رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ went out one day, I with him, until we entered the Jews' synagogue on their festival day. They disliked our entering upon them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: > **""O company of Jews, show me twelve men among you who will testify that there is no deity but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; Allah will remove from every Jew under the sky's surface the anger upon him.""** They fell silent and none answered him. He repeated it and none answered, then a third time and none answered. He said: > **""You refuse; by Allah, I am the Gatherer, I am the Last, I am the Follower, whether you believe or deny.""** Then he turned away with me until we were about to leave, when a man called from behind him, wait, O Muhammad. He came forward and said, O company of Jews, what kind of man do you know me to be among you. They said, by Allah, we know none among us more learned in Allah's Book nor more understanding than you, nor than your father before you, nor your grandfather before your father. He said, I bear witness by Allah that he is Allah's prophet whom you find in the Torah. They said, you lie, then rejected him and spoke evil of him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You have lied; we will not accept your word.""** We left, three of us: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, myself, and Ibn Salam. Then Allah revealed, ""Say, have you considered, if it is from Allah and you disbelieve in it, while a witness from the Children of Israel testifies to its like and believes, while you are arrogant; indeed Allah does not guide the wrongdoers."""
"11338","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained Allah's saying, ""Until when he reaches his full strength"": It is thirty-three years, and it is the age at which Isa ibn Maryam عليه السلام was raised up."
"11339","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained, ""And when We turned toward you a company of jinn listening to the Quran"": They were nine persons from Nasibin, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made them messengers to their people."
"11340","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said in Al-Awsat: The jinn were turned toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ twice, and the nobles of the jinn were at Nasibin."
"11341","Also from him in Al-Awsat: The jinn who came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him while he was at Nakhlah."
"11342","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said in Al-Bazzar: The nobles of the jinn were at Mosul."
"11343","Zirr ibn Hubaysh explained, ""And when We turned toward you a company of jinn listening to the Quran, and when they attended it they said, listen quietly"": It means be silent. They were seven, one of them Zawbaa."
"11344","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained His saying, ""That they may increase in faith along with their faith"": Allah sent His Prophet ﷺ with testimony that there is no deity but Allah. When they affirmed it, He added Hajj; when they affirmed it, He added jihad; then He completed their religion for them and said, ""This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed upon you My favor and approved Islam for you as religion."" Ibn Abbas said, the firmest, truest, most complete faith of heaven's and earth's people is testimony that there is no deity but Allah."
"11345","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه said: I used to write for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with the pen upon my ear, when he ordered fighting. A blind man came and said, what of me, while my sight is gone. Then was revealed, ""There is no harm upon the blind."""
"11346","Abu Juma al-Ansari, Junayd ibn Sabu رضي الله عنه, said: I fought the Prophet ﷺ in the first part of the day as a disbeliever, and fought with him in the last part of the day as a Muslim. We were three men and nine women, and concerning us was revealed, ""Were it not for believing men and believing women."""
"11347","Ubayy ibn Kab رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained Allah's saying, ""Their mark is upon their faces from the trace of prostration"": > **""It is light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"11348","Al-Juayd ibn Abd al-Rahman said: I was with Al-Saib ibn Yazid when Al-Zubayr ibn Suhayl ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came with a mark of prostration on his face. When he saw him he said, who is this. It was said, Al-Zubayr. He said, he has ruined his face. By Allah, this is not the mark Allah named. I have prayed upon my face for eighty years and prostration has left no mark between my eyes."
"11349","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه said: When this verse was revealed, ""O you who believe, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet,"" I said, O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I will not speak to you except as one speaking confidentially."
"11350","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه said: Some Arabs came and said, come with us to this man; if he is a prophet we shall be the most fortunate of people by him, and if he is a king we shall live under his protection. I went to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of what they said. Then they came to the Prophet's chambers calling, O Muhammad, O Muhammad. Allah revealed, ""Indeed those who call you from behind the chambers, most of them do not understand."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took my ear and said: > **""Allah has confirmed your word, O Zayd.""**"
"11351","Al-Aqra ibn Habis called the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from behind the chambers, saying, O Messenger of Allah, but he did not answer him. He said, O Muhammad, my praise is adornment and my blame is disgrace. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, as Abu Salama narrated: > **""That is Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"11352","Harith ibn Dirar al-Khuzai رضي الله عنه said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he invited me to Islam, so I affirmed it and entered it, and invited me to zakat, so I affirmed it. I said, O Messenger of Allah, I shall return to my people and call them to Islam and payment of zakat; whoever responds I shall gather his zakat, and you send a messenger to me at such-and-such time to receive what I gathered. When Harith gathered the zakat and the appointed time the Messenger ﷺ intended to send arrived, the messenger was withheld and did not come. Harith thought some anger from Allah and His Messenger had occurred, so he called the chiefs of his people and said, the Messenger ﷺ fixed a time to send his messenger to collect what I hold, and breaking promise is not of him; I see his messenger's detention only as some anger, so let us go to the Messenger ﷺ. Meanwhile the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Al-Walid ibn Uqba to Harith to collect the gathered zakat. When Walid set out partway he became frightened and returned, came to the Messenger ﷺ and said, Harith withheld zakat and sought to kill me. The Messenger ﷺ dispatched a force to Harith. Harith came forward with his companions, met the force outside Medina, and they said, this is Harith. When he reached them he said, to whom were you sent. They said, to you. He asked why. They said, the Messenger ﷺ sent Walid to you and he claimed you withheld zakat and sought to kill him. He said, no, by the One who sent Muhammad with truth, I never saw him at all nor did he come to me. When Harith entered upon the Messenger ﷺ, he said: > **""Did you withhold zakat and seek to kill my messenger?""** He said, no, by the One who sent you with truth, I never saw him at all nor did he come to me; I held back only when your messenger was withheld from me, fearing anger from Allah and His Messenger. Then were revealed the verses of Al-Hujurat, ""O you who believe, if a corrupt one comes to you with news, verify, lest you strike a people in ignorance and become regretful over what you did,"" up to ""favor from Allah and blessing, and Allah is Knowing."""
"11353","Alqama ibn Najiya رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us Al-Walid ibn Uqba ibn Abi Muayt to collect our wealth after the battle of Muraysi. He traveled until near us, then returned. I rode after him. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, I came to a people in their ignorance; they took up arms and withheld charity. The Prophet ﷺ did nothing until was revealed, ""O you who believe, if a corrupt one comes to you with news."" Then the Mustaliq came after Walid to the Prophet ﷺ with a portion of their charities driving them and provisions carrying them. They told him they had gone out seeking Walid with their charities but did not find him, and handed over what they had, saying, O Messenger of Allah, we heard of your envoy's departure and rejoiced and went to meet him; when we heard of his return we feared it was from anger against us. They offered that he buy from them what remained. He accepted the obligations from them and said: > **""Return with your provisions; we do not sell anything of charity until we take possession of it.""** So they returned to their families, and he sent to them one to collect the rest of their charities."
"11354","In a narration also from Alqama, concerning the delegation of Banu Abd al-Mustaliq to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the affair of Al-Walid ibn Uqba, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Depart, neither detained nor confined.""**"
"11355","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Al-Walid ibn Uqba to Banu Walia, between whom there was hatred in pre-Islamic times. When he reached them they came out to meet him to see what was in his heart, so he feared the people and returned to the Messenger ﷺ saying, Banu Walia sought to kill me and withheld charity. When what Walid said reached them, they came to the Messenger ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, Walid lied; there was old hatred between us and him, and we feared he would punish us for it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said: > **""Banu Walia shall desist, or I shall send to them a man like myself, who will kill their fighters and take captive their offspring, and it is this one.""** Then he struck with his hand upon the shoulder of Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه. And Allah revealed concerning Walid, ""O you who believe, if a corrupt one comes to you with news,"" the verse."
"11356","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها reported: The Prophet ﷺ returned to her house and prayed in it two rakas after Asr. Aisha sent to Umm Salama asking, what is this prayer the Prophet ﷺ prayed in your house. She said, the Prophet ﷺ used to pray two rakas after Zuhr. The delegation of Banu Mustaliq came concerning what their collector Walid ibn Uqba had done with them, and kept excusing themselves to the Prophet ﷺ until the caller came summoning him to Asr prayer. He prayed the obligatory prayer, then prayed with me in my house those two rakas, which he had prayed neither before nor after."
"11357","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها reported that there was revealed concerning Banu Mustaliq, regarding what their collector Walid ibn Uqba did with them, ""O you who believe, if a corrupt one comes to you with news, verify"": The Prophet ﷺ had sent him to them to collect their wealth. When they heard of him a party rode out saying, we shall travel with the Messenger's messenger and carry him. When he heard that he thought they rode out to kill him, so he returned and said, O Messenger of Allah, Banu Mustaliq withheld their charities. The people came on until Medina, lined behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in prayer, and when he finished they said, we seek refuge in Allah from Allah's anger and His Messenger's anger. O Messenger of Allah, we heard of your envoy to collect our wealth and rejoiced and our eyes were cooled; we wished to meet him and travel with him, then heard he returned, so we thought his return was from anger of Allah and His Messenger upon us. They kept excusing themselves to the Prophet ﷺ until this verse was revealed concerning them."
"11358","Mujahid reported: The Prophet ﷺ sent Al-Walid ibn Uqba ibn Abi Muayt to Banu Mustaliq as collector."
"11359","Abu Jubayra ibn al-Dahhak reported from his uncles: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to Medina, and there was none among us except he had one or two nicknames. When one was called by it we said, O Messenger of Allah, he dislikes it. Then Allah revealed, ""Nor insult one another with nicknames,"" to the end of the verse."
"11360","Al-Dahhak ibn Abi Jubayra رضي الله عنه said: They had nicknames in pre-Islamic times. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called a man by his nickname, so it was said, O Messenger of Allah, he dislikes it. Then Allah revealed, ""Nor insult one another with nicknames,"" to the end of the verse."
"11361","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه reported that some Arabs said, O Messenger of Allah, we embraced Islam and did not fight you, while such-and-such tribe fought you. Then Allah revealed, ""They remind you of favor that they embraced Islam,"" the verse."
"11362","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Paradise and Hell boasted. Hell said, O Lord, the tyrants, arrogant, kings and nobles shall enter me. Paradise said, the weak, poor and needy shall enter me. Allah said to Hell, you are My punishment, I afflict through you whom I will. And He said to Paradise, you are My mercy, encompassing all things, and each of you shall have its fill. Its people shall be cast into Hell and it will say, is there more. Again they shall be cast and it will say, is there more, until Allah comes to it and places His foot upon it and it contracts and says, enough, enough. As for Paradise, there shall remain in it what Allah wills to remain, and Allah shall create for it whatever creation He wills.""**"
"11363","Anas رضي الله عنه explained His saying, ""And with Us is more"": He manifests Himself to them every Friday."
"11364","Jarir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning, ""And glorify with praise of your Lord before sunrise and before sunset"": > **""Before sunrise is the dawn prayer, and before sunset is the Asr prayer.""**"
"11365","Said ibn al-Musayyab said: Asbagh al-Tamimi came to Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه and said, O Commander of the Believers, inform me of, ""By those scattering far."" He said, they are the winds; had I not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say it I would not say it. He said, inform me of, ""And those bearing weight."" He said, they are the clouds; had I not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say it I would not say it. He said, inform me of, ""And those apportioning command."" He said, they are the angels; had I not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say it I would not say it. He said, inform me of, ""And those running with ease."" He said, they are the ships; had I not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say it I would not say it. Then he ordered him struck a hundred lashes and housed him; when he recovered he called him and struck him another hundred, carried him upon a saddle, and wrote to Abu Musa al-Ashari, prevent people from sitting with him. He remained so until he came to Abu Musa and swore solemn oaths that he found nothing in himself of what he used to find. Abu Musa wrote of that to Umar, and Umar wrote, I think he has spoken truthfully, so leave him to sit with people."
"11366","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah did not release upon Ad of the wind by which they were destroyed except like the place of a ring. It passed by the desert people carrying their livestock and wealth between heaven and earth. When the townspeople saw it they said, this is a cloud bringing us rain. Then it cast the desert people and their livestock upon the townspeople.""**"
"11367","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah did not release upon Ad of the wind except like the place of a ring. It was sent upon them carrying the desert folk to the settled folk. When the settled folk saw it they said, this is a cloud bringing us rain, coming toward our valleys, while the desert people were in it, and the desert people were cast upon the settled people until they perished. It rebelled against its keepers until it came out through the openings of the gates.""**"
"11368","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Frequented House is in heaven, called Al-Surah, upon the likeness of the Sacred House facing it; were it to fall it would fall upon it. Seventy thousand angels enter it every day never having seen it before, and it has sanctity in heaven equal to Makkah's sanctity. Seventy thousand angels enter the Frequented House every day never to return to it again.""**"
"11369","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When a man enters Paradise he asks about his parents, wife and children. It is said, they did not reach your rank and work. He says, O Lord, I worked for myself and for them. Then command is given to join them with him.""** And Ibn Abbas recited, ""And those who believed and their offspring followed them in faith,"" the verse."
"11370","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما raised to the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Indeed Allah raises the believer's offspring to him in his rank, though they are below him in work, so that his eye is cooled by them.""** Then he recited, ""And those who believed and their offspring followed them,"" the verse, then said, ""And We did not diminish the fathers by what We gave the sons."""
"11371","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained, ""Then he drew near and came down"": It is Muhammad ﷺ who drew near and came down to his Lord."
"11372","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained His saying, ""And he was at a distance of two bows or nearer"": Al-qab means the shackle, and al-qawsayn means the two forearms."
"11373","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained, ""When there covered the Lote Tree what covered"": > **""I saw it until I examined it closely, then golden moths came between me and it.""**"
"11374","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said: The Prophet ﷺ asked Jibril عليه السلام to see him in his true form. He said: > **""Supplicate your Lord.""** So he supplicated his Lord, and he rose upon him from the east, rising and pointing. When he saw him he fell unconscious, then he came to him."
"11375","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said, Muhammad ﷺ saw his Lord. Ikrima said, O Abu Abbas, does not Allah say, ""Sights do not grasp Him while He grasps sights."" Ibn Abbas said, may you be bereft of your mother, that is only when He manifests in a manner no sight can withstand."
"11376","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما, as reckoned by Said ibn Jubayr, reported: The Prophet ﷺ was in Makkah and recited Surah Al-Najm until he reached, ""Have you considered Al-Lat and Al-Uzza and Manat the third other."" There ran upon his tongue: those are the high cranes whose intercession is hoped for. The Makkan polytheists heard it and rejoiced, and it weighed heavily upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then Allah revealed, ""And We did not send before you any messenger or prophet but that when he recited, Satan cast into his recitation; then Allah abolishes what Satan casts, then Allah confirms His verses."""
"11377","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said: Al-Uzza was at the belly of Nakhlah, Al-Lat was at Taif, and Manat was at Qudayd. Ali ibn al-Jad said, the belly of Nakhlah is the orchard of Banu Amir."
"11378","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained, ""Those who avoid grave sins and shameful acts except lamam"": Lamam is a brush with adultery. Ibn Abbas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, if You forgive, forgive plentifully; and which servant of Yours has not brushed sin.""**"
"11379","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained His saying, ""Those who avoid grave sins and shameful acts"": The greatest of grave sins is associating with Allah, for Allah says, ""Whoever associates with Allah, Allah has forbidden him Paradise."" And despair of Allah's mercy, for Allah says, ""None despairs of Allah's mercy except the disbelieving people."" And security from Allah's plan, for Allah says, ""None feels secure from Allah's plan except the losing people."" Among them is disobedience to parents, for Allah made the disobedient overbearing, wretched. And killing the soul Allah forbade except by right, for Allah says, ""His recompense is Hell."" And slandering chaste women, for Allah says, they are cursed in this world and the Hereafter and for them is great punishment. And consuming the orphan's wealth, for Allah says, ""They only consume fire in their bellies and shall roast in blaze."" And fleeing from battle, for Allah says, ""Whoever turns his back to them that day, except maneuvering for combat or joining a band, has returned with anger from Allah and his refuge is Hell."" And consuming usury, for Allah says, ""Those who consume usury stand only as one stands whom Satan has confounded by touch."" And sorcery, for Allah says, they knew whoever purchased it has no share in the Hereafter. And adultery, for Allah says, ""Whoever does that meets punishment, multiplied for him on the Resurrection Day, abiding therein humbled."" And the immersing wicked oath, for Allah says, ""Indeed those who purchase with Allah's covenant and their oaths a small price."" And misappropriation, for Allah says, ""Whoever misappropriates shall bring what he misappropriated on the Resurrection Day."" And withholding obligatory zakat, for Allah says, ""Their foreheads shall be branded therewith."" And false testimony, for Allah says, ""Whoever conceals it, his heart is sinful."" And drinking wine, for Allah equated it with idols. And deliberately abandoning prayer or anything Allah obligated, for the Messenger ﷺ says: > **""Whoever abandons prayer deliberately, the protection of Allah and His Messenger is free of him.""** And breaking covenants and severing kinship."
"11380","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained, ""While you are samidun"": They would pass by the Prophet ﷺ strutting arrogantly; have you not seen how the calf struts haughtily."
"11381","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained, ""While you are samidun"": It means singing."
"11382","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained, ""While you are samidun"": Turned away, heedless."
"11383","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما said: This verse was revealed only concerning the people of qadar, ""Indeed the criminals are in error and madness, the Day they are dragged in Fire upon their faces: taste the touch of Saqar. Indeed everything We created with measure."""
"11384","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said: This verse was revealed concerning the Qadariyya, ""The Day they are dragged in Fire upon their faces: taste the touch of Saqar. Indeed everything We created with measure."""
"11385","Zurarah reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning, ""Taste the touch of Saqar. Indeed everything We created with measure"": > **""It was revealed concerning people of my nation at the end of time who deny Allah's decree.""**"
"11386","Asma bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنها said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying toward the Corner before he proclaimed what he was commanded, while the polytheists listened, ""So which of your Lord's favors do you deny."""
"11387","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited Surah Al-Rahman to his Companions and they fell silent, so he said: > **""The jinn responded better than you. Whenever I recited to them, 'So which of your Lord's favors do you deny,' they said, 'We deny nothing of Your favors, our Lord; to You belongs praise.'""**"
"11388","Abdullah ibn Munib رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited to us, ""Every day He is engaged."" We said, O Messenger of Allah, what is that engagement. He said: > **""That He forgives a sin, relieves a distress, raises a people and lowers others.""**"
"11389","Al-Bazzar narrated like it from Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه with the addition: > **""And answers a supplicant.""**"
"11390","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه heard the Prophet ﷺ saying upon the pulpit, ""And for whoever feared his Lord's standing are two gardens."" I said, even if he commits adultery and steals, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said it a second time, ""And for whoever feared his Lord's standing are two gardens."" I said, even if he commits adultery and steals. He said it a third time, then said: > **""Yes, even if Abu al-Darda's nose is rubbed in dust.""** In another wording, Amr ibn al-Aswad left his house with Abu al-Darda toward the mosque, Abu al-Darda reciting the verse. Amr asked, even if he commits adultery and steals. He repeated it twice or thrice, then said, yes, even if your nose is rubbed in dust, O Amr. Perhaps you find something in yourself, O Amr; I said to you only what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me, ""even if your nose is rubbed in dust, O Uwaymir."""
"11391","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said: Al-marjan is red beads."
"11392","Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about Allah's saying, ""Two dark green"": > **""Two green.""**"
"11393","Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه said, I said, O Messenger of Allah, gray hair has hastened upon you. He said: > **""Al-Waqia, Amma yatasaalun, and When the sun is folded have made me gray.""**"
"11394","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه said: When was revealed, ""A multitude from the former and a few from the later,"" it weighed upon the Muslims. Then was revealed, ""A multitude from the former and a multitude from the later."""
"11395","Abu Bakra رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning, ""A multitude from the former and a multitude from the later"": > **""Both of them are from this nation.""**"
"11396","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها said, I said, O Messenger of Allah, inform me of Allah's saying, ""And wide-eyed houris."" He said: > **""Hur means white, een means huge-eyed, the eyelash of the houri like an eagle's wing.""** I said, inform me of, ""Like hidden pearls."" He said, their purity is like the pearl in shells untouched by hands. I said, inform me of, ""In them good, beautiful."" He said, good in character, beautiful in face. I said, inform me of, ""As though they were sheltered eggs."" He said, their delicacy like the skin you see inside the egg next to the shell. I said, inform me of, ""Loving, contemporaries."" He said, they are those taken in this world old, bleary, gray-haired; Allah creates them after old age as virgins, loving, affectionate, contemporaries of one birth. I said, O Messenger of Allah, are worldly women superior or the houris. He said: > **""Worldly women are superior to the houris as the outer garment is superior to the lining.""** I said, why. He said, by their prayer, fasting and worship of Allah. Allah clothes their faces with light, their bodies with silk, white in color, green in garments, yellow in ornaments, their censers pearls, their combs gold. They say, we are eternal never to die, we are blissful never to suffer, we are resident never to depart, we are pleased never to anger; blessed is he for whom we are and who is for us. I said, O Messenger of Allah, a woman among us marries two, three or four husbands then dies and enters Paradise with them entering with her; who is her husband. He said: > **""O Umm Salama, she is given choice and chooses the best of them in character, saying, O Lord, this was best in character in this world, so marry me to him. O Umm Salama, good character has taken the good of this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"11397","Salama ibn Yazid al-Jufi رضي الله عنه said, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting, ""Indeed We created them anew, and made them virgins, loving"": > **""From the previously married and the unmarried.""**"
"11398","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited, ""And the companions of the right,"" and, ""And the companions of the left,"" then closed two handfuls with his hands and said: > **""This is in Paradise and I do not care, and this is in Fire and I do not care.""**"
"11399","Abu Umama رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the raised couches. He said: > **""Were a couch thrown from its top it would fall to its bottom in a hundred autumns.""**"
"11400","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained, ""So I swear by the settings of the stars"": The Quran descended whole at once to the lowest heaven, then descended in portions afterward to the Prophet ﷺ."
"11401","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Surah Al-Hadid was revealed on Tuesday, and Allah created iron on Tuesday.""**"
"11402","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه said, while we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a cloud passed, and he said: > **""Do you know what this is?""** We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, the clouds and waterers of the earth, which Allah drives to those of His servants who neither thank Him nor invoke Him. Do you know what is above you. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, the Raqi, a restrained wave and guarded roof; do you know how far between you and it. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, five hundred years' journey. Then, what is above it. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, another heaven; do you know how far between you and it. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, five hundred years' journey, until he counted seven heavens. Then, do you know what is above that. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, the Throne. Do you know how far between it and the seventh heaven. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, five hundred years' journey. Then, do you know what is beneath you. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, earth; do you know what is beneath it. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, another earth; do you know how far between them. We said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, seven hundred years' journey, until he counted seven earths. Then he said: > **""By Allah, were you to lower one of you with a rope to the lowest seventh earth, it would descend.""** Then he recited, ""He is the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden, and He knows all things."""
"11403","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported that Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه informed him: There were only four years between their Islam and the revelation of this verse by which Allah reproached them, ""And let them not be like those given the Book before, whose term grew long so their hearts hardened, and many of them are corrupt."""
"11404","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: Forty of the Najashi's companions came to the Prophet ﷺ and fought alongside him at Uhud. They were wounded but none was killed. When they saw the need of the believers, they said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we are people of means, so permit us to fetch our wealth and share it with the Muslims."" Then Allah revealed concerning them the verse of Al-Qasas, ""Those to whom We gave the Book before it believe in it,"" and, ""They will be given their reward twice for their patience,"" and, ""They repel evil with good,"" meaning the charity with which they aided the Muslims. They then said, ""O Muslims, whoever of us believes in your Book has two rewards, and whoever does not believe has one reward like yours."" Then Allah revealed Al-Hadid, ""O you who believe, fear Allah and believe in His Messenger; He will give you two shares of His mercy and appoint for you a light by which you walk and forgive you,"" adding light and forgiveness, and, ""So that the People of the Book may know they have no power over any of Allah's bounty."""
"11405","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported: The Jews used to say to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ""Sam upon you,"" meaning death, and then say among themselves, ""Why does Allah not punish us for what we say?"" Then Allah revealed: > **""And when they come to you, they greet you with what Allah has not greeted you with.""**"
"11406","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reported: The verse was revealed concerning me, ""O you who believe, when you confer privately with the Messenger, offer charity before your private counsel."" So I offered a handful of barley, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You are indeed ascetic.""** Then the other verse was revealed, ""Are you afraid to offer charities before your private counsel,"" the whole verse, abrogating it."
"11407","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting in the shade of his chamber, the shade barely covering him, and said to his Companions: > **""A man will come to you looking at you with the two eyes of Satan, so when you see him do not speak to him.""** A blue-eyed man then came. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him he called him and said: > **""Why do you and your companions revile me?""** The man said, ""Stay as you are until I bring them to you."" He went and brought them, and they swore by Allah that they had neither said nor done anything. Then Allah revealed, ""On the Day Allah resurrects them all, they will swear to Him as they swear to you,"" to the end of the verse."
"11408","In another narration, the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""A man will enter upon you looking with the two eyes of Satan.""** A blue-eyed man entered and said, ""O Muhammad, on what basis do you insult me?"" or words to that effect, and began swearing. Then the verse of Al-Mujadila was revealed, ""And they swear to falsehood while they know,"" and the other verse."
"11409","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported: Permission was given to cut the date-palms, then it was made strict upon them. They came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, is there sin upon us for what we cut or for what we left?"" Then Allah revealed: > **""Whatever you cut of the palms or left standing upon their roots was by Allah's permission.""**"
"11410","Al-Aswad ibn Hilal reported: A man came to Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه and asked about the verse, ""Whoever is protected from the shuhh of his soul, they are the successful."" The man said, ""I am one from whom nothing can come out, and I fear this verse applies to me."" Ibn Masud said, ""You mention miserliness, and miserliness is an evil thing, but what Allah mentions in the Quran is not what you say. It is that you turn to the wealth of another, or he said your brother, and consume it."""
"11411","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported: Qutaylah bint al-Uzza of Bani Malik ibn Hisl came to her daughter Asma bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنها with gifts of lizards, cheese, and ghee, while she was a polytheist. Asma refused to accept her gift or admit her into her house. Aisha asked the Prophet ﷺ about it, and Allah revealed, ""Allah does not forbid you concerning those who did not fight you for religion,"" to the end of the verse. So he commanded her to accept her gift and admit her into her house."
"11412","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported concerning Allah's saying, ""When the believing women come to you as emigrants, test them; Allah knows best their faith"": When a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ, Umar رضي الله عنه would make her swear by Allah that she had not left out of desire to move from one land to another, by Allah that she had not left seeking worldly gain, and by Allah that she had left only out of love for Allah and His Messenger."
"11413","Abdullah ibn Abi Ahmad رضي الله عنه reported: Umm Kulthum bint Uqba ibn Abi Muayt emigrated during the truce. Her two brothers, Umara and al-Walid, sons of Uqba, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and spoke to him about returning her to them. Then Allah annulled the treaty between him and the polytheists specifically regarding women, forbidding that they be returned to the polytheists, and Allah revealed the verse of examination."
"11414","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning, ""Nor disobey you in what is right"": > **""It is wailing.""**"
"11415","Musab ibn Nuh al-Ansari reported: I knew an old woman of ours who was among those who pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ. She said, ""We came to him one day and he took our pledge that we would not wail."" The old woman said, ""O Messenger of Allah, some people had helped me upon a calamity that befell me, and a calamity has now befallen them, and I wish to help them."" Then she came to him and pledged allegiance to him. She said, ""This is the right conduct of which Allah says, 'Nor disobey you in what is right.'"""
"11416","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported concerning His saying, ""O you who believe, do not befriend a people with whom Allah is angry; they have despaired of the Hereafter"": They do not believe in it nor are rewarded for it. This is the disbeliever who, when he dies and beholds his recompense and looks upon it, despairs."
"11417","Ibrahim reported: Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه recited, ""When the call is made for prayer on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah."" Abdullah said, ""Had I read it as 'run,' I would run until my cloak fell."" And he used to recite it as ""famdu,"" meaning proceed."
"11418","Qatada reported: In the section of Ibn Masud it is ""famdu ila dhikr Allah,"" proceed to the remembrance of Allah, and it is like His saying, ""Indeed your striving is diverse."""
"11419","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: The Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon on Friday when Dihya ibn Khalifa arrived selling his goods. Nearly all in the mosque left except a few, while the Prophet ﷺ was standing. Then Allah revealed, ""When they saw trade or amusement they dispersed toward it and left you standing."""
"11420","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه reported: I was sitting with Abdullah ibn Ubayy when the Messenger ﷺ passed with some of his Companions, and Abdullah ibn Ubayy said, ""If we return to Medina, the mightiest will surely expel the humblest."" I went to Sad ibn Ubada and informed him, and he went to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned it to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Abdullah ibn Ubayy, who swore by Allah that he had not said it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at Sad ibn Ubada, and Sad said, ""O Messenger of Allah, the boy Zayd ibn Arqam informed me of it."" Then Sad came, took my hand, and set out with me, saying, ""This is what he reported to me."" Abdullah ibn Ubayy scolded me. We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and I wept and said, ""Yes, by the One who sent down the light upon you, he indeed said it."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned away from him, and Allah revealed, ""When the hypocrites come to you,"" to the end of the sura."
"11421","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""O people, take the fear of Allah as merchandise; provision will come to you without goods or trade.""** Then he recited, ""Whoever fears Allah, He appoints for him a way out and provides for him from where he does not expect."""
"11422","Abu al-Duha reported: Masruq and Shutayr ibn Shakl gathered in the mosque, and the circles of the mosque drew toward them. Masruq said, ""I do not see these people sitting with us except to hear good from us. Either you narrate from Abdullah and I confirm you, or I narrate from Abdullah and you confirm me."" He said, ""Narrate to us, Abu Aisha."" Masruq said, ""I heard Abdullah ibn Masud say: The two eyes commit adultery, the two feet commit adultery, the two hands commit adultery, and the private part confirms it or denies it."" He said, ""Yes, and I too heard it."" He said, ""Did you hear Abdullah ibn Masud say: The most comprehensive verse in the Quran concerning lawful, unlawful, command, and prohibition is, 'Indeed Allah commands justice, excellence, and giving to kin, and forbids indecency and wrongdoing,' to the end of the verse?"" He said, ""Yes, and I too heard it."" He said, ""Did you hear Abdullah ibn Masud say: The greatest verse in Allah's Book in entrusting affairs to Him is, 'Whoever fears Allah, He appoints for him a way out and provides for him from where he does not expect'?"" He said, ""Yes, and I too heard it."" He said, ""Did you hear Abdullah ibn Masud say: The most joyful verse in the Quran is, 'O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy,' to the end of the verse?"" He said, ""Yes, and I too heard it."""
"11423","In another narration, Shutayr was the one who narrated, saying, ""Abdullah ibn Masud reported to us that the greatest verse in the Book of Allah is, 'Allah, there is no deity but He, the Living, the Sustainer.'"" Masruq said, ""You spoke the truth."" The rest is similar."
"11424","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported concerning, ""O Prophet, why do you prohibit what Allah made lawful for you"": This verse was revealed concerning his concubine."
"11425","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered with Mariya the Copt, his concubine, into the house of Hafsa bint Umar رضي الله عنها. She found her with him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, in my house apart from the houses of your wives?"" He said: > **""She is forbidden for me to touch, O Hafsa, and conceal this from me.""** She went out to Aisha and said, ""O daughter of Abu Bakr, shall I not give you glad tidings?"" She said, ""What?"" She said, ""I found Mariya with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in my house, and I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, in my house apart from the houses of your wives?' The first joy was that he forbade her for himself. Then he said to me, 'O Hafsa, shall I not give you glad tidings?' I said, 'Yes, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah.' He informed me that your father will assume authority after me and that my father will assume it after your father, and he asked me to keep it secret, so keep it secret."" Then Allah revealed, ""O Prophet, why do you prohibit what Allah made lawful for you,"" meaning Mariya, ""seeking the pleasure of your wives, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful,"" for what came from you, ""Allah has ordained for you the dissolution of your oaths, and Allah is your Protector, and He is the Knowing, the Wise. And when the Prophet confided a statement to one of his wives,"" meaning Hafsa, ""then when she informed of it,"" meaning Aisha, ""and Allah exposed it to him,"" meaning through the Quran, ""he made known part of it,"" he informed Hafsa of what was exposed concerning Mariya, ""and turned away from part,"" concerning what she had disclosed about Abu Bakr and Umar, and did not reveal it to her, ""then when he informed her of it she said, 'Who informed you of this?' He said, 'The Knowing, the Aware informed me.'"" Then he turned to her reproaching her with, ""If you two repent to Allah, for your hearts have inclined,"" to the end of the passage."
"11426","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to drink honey at Sawda's. He entered upon Aisha, who said, ""I notice an odor from you."" Then he entered upon Hafsa, who said, ""I notice an odor from you."" He said: > **""I think it is from a drink I drank at Sawda's. By Allah, I shall not drink it again.""** Then the verse was revealed, ""O Prophet, why do you prohibit what Allah made lawful for you."""
"11427","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning Allah's saying, ""For Allah is his Protector, and Jibril, and the righteous of the believers"": > **""The righteous of the believers are Abu Bakr and Umar.""**"
"11428","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported concerning His saying, ""Whose fuel is men and stones"": They are stones of brimstone which Allah places with Him as He wills and when He wills."
"11429","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I wish it were in the heart of every person of my nation,""** meaning, ""Blessed is He in whose Hand is dominion."""
"11430","Anas رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is a sura of the Quran, only thirty verses, which disputed on behalf of its companion until it admitted him into Paradise, and it is the sura Tabarak.""**"
"11431","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported: In the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ we used to call it al-Maniah, the Preventer. It is a sura in the Book of Allah; whoever recites it in his night has done much and well."
"11432","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported: A man is approached in his grave. His feet are approached and they say, ""You have no way against what is before us; he used to recite Surat al-Mulk over us."" Then his belly is approached and it says, ""You have no way against me; he used to recite Surat al-Mulk within me."" Then he is approached from his head and it says, ""You have no way against what is before me; he used to recite Surat al-Mulk."" Abdullah said, ""So it is al-Maniah; it prevents the punishment of the grave. In the Torah it is Surat al-Mulk; whoever recites it in his night has done much and well."""
"11433","In another narration: A man died and the angels of punishment came and sat at his head. He said, ""You have no way against him; he used to recite Surat al-Mulk."" The rest is similar."
"11434","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The first things Allah created were the Pen and the Fish. It said, 'What shall I write?' He said, 'Everything that will be until the Day of Resurrection.'""** Then he recited, ""Nun, by the Pen and what they inscribe."" So Nun is the Fish and the Pen is the Pen."
"11435","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the utull zanim, and said: > **""He is harsh in character, bulky, a great eater and drinker, finding food and drink in plenty, oppressive to people, wide in the belly.""**"
"11436","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning, ""The Day the shin is uncovered"": > **""It is a great light before which they fall prostrate.""**"
"11437","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported concerning His saying, ""husuman"": It means consecutive days."
"11438","Yazid ibn Amir al-Suwai reported: While they were circumambulating the idol they heard a speaker saying, ""Had he forged against Us any sayings, We would have seized him by the right, then cut from him the life-vein."" We were alarmed at that and said, ""What is this speech we do not know?"" Then we looked and found the Prophet ﷺ going along."
"11439","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported concerning, ""The Day the sky becomes like al-muhl"": It is like the dregs of oil. And concerning His saying about the hours of the night, he said, ""The middle of the night."""
"11440","Al-Qasim and al-Hasan ibn Sad reported: It was said to Abdullah, ""Allah mentions prayer abundantly in the Quran: 'Those who are constant upon their prayer,' and, 'Those who guard their prayers.'"" He said, ""That refers to its appointed times."" They said, ""We thought it meant only abandoning it."" He said, ""Abandoning it is disbelief."""
"11441","Kardus Abu al-Saib reported: I set out with my father intending Mecca, when mention of the Prophet ﷺ had first appeared. We sheltered with a shepherd. At midnight a wolf came and seized a lamb from his flock. The shepherd sprang up and said, ""O amir of the valley, your neighbor."" We heard a voice whose speaker we did not know, ""O Sirhan, release it."" The lamb came running with no bite upon it until it entered the flock. And there was revealed to the Prophet ﷺ at Medina, ""There were men of mankind who sought refuge with men of the jinn,"" the verse."
"11442","Ikrima and others reported: A company of the jinn listened to the Quran at Nakhla while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying the last Isha, ""They almost crowded upon him in multitudes."" Sufyan said, ""Libad means some upon others, like felted wool piled together."""
"11443","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported: Quraysh gathered in Dar al-Nadwa and said, ""Name this man a name that turns people from him."" Some said soothsayer. They said, ""He is no soothsayer."" Some said madman. They said, ""He is no madman."" Some said magician. They said, ""He is no magician."" The polytheists dispersed upon that. When it reached the Prophet ﷺ, he wrapped himself in his garments and covered himself, and Jibril came to him and said, ""O you wrapped up, O you enshrouded."""
"11444","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported: When there was revealed, ""Leave Me with the deniers, possessors of ease, and respite them a little,"" only a short time passed before the Battle of Badr occurred."
"11445","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported: When revelation descended upon the Prophet ﷺ, he experienced what Allah describes: ""We shall cast upon you a heavy word."""
"11446","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited, ""A Day that makes children grey,"" and said: > **""That is the Day of Resurrection. It is the day Allah will say to Adam, 'Rise and send forth from your offspring a delegation to the Fire.' He will say, 'From how many, my Lord?' He will say, 'From every thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine, and one is saved.'""** It weighed heavily upon the Muslims, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perceived it in them. When he saw it in their faces, he said: > **""The children of Adam are many. Gog and Magog are from the children of Adam, and no man of them dies until a thousand men of his own loins inherit from him. Among them and their likes is a shield for you.""**"
"11447","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning, ""So recite what is easy of it"": > **""One hundred verses.""**"
"11448","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: Al-Walid ibn al-Mughira prepared food for Quraysh. When they ate he said, ""What do you say about this man?"" Some said magician, some said he is no magician, some said soothsayer, some said he is no soothsayer, some said poet, some said he is no poet, some said transmitted magic. Their word united upon it being transmitted magic. When it reached the Prophet ﷺ he grieved, covered his head, and wrapped himself. Then Allah revealed, ""O you enshrouded, arise and warn, magnify your Lord, purify your garments, abandon the idols, do not confer favor seeking more, and be patient for your Lord."""
"11449","Al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazza reported concerning His saying, ""Nor confer favor seeking more"": Do not give anything seeking more than it in return."
"11450","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported: Do not give a man a gift hoping he gives you more than it."
"11451","Yahya ibn Zakariyya reported: I said to al-Amash, ""From whom did you read, 'And abandon the idols'?"" He said, ""I read to Yahya ibn Waththab, Yahya read to Alqama, Alqama read to Abdullah, and Abdullah read to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."""
"11452","Abu Said رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning His saying, ""I shall burden him with saud"": > **""It is a mountain of fire in the Fire. He is charged to climb it. When he places his hand upon it, it melts; when he lifts it, it returns. When he places his foot upon it, it melts; when he lifts it, it returns.""**"
"11453","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported concerning His saying, ""When the Trumpet is blown"": The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""How can I enjoy life when the bearer of the Trumpet has seized the Trumpet and bowed his forehead, listening for when he will be commanded?""** His Companions said, ""What should we say?"" He said: > **""Say: Allah suffices us, and He is the bestGuardian.""**"
"11454","In another narration: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then recited, ""When the Trumpet is blown."""
"11455","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported concerning Allah's saying, ""Fleeing from a qaswara"": It is the lion."
"11456","Said ibn Jubayr reported: I asked Ibn Abbas about Allah's saying, ""Nearer to you and nearer,"" whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it or Allah revealed it. He said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it, and Allah revealed it."""
"11457","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites 'By those sent forth in succession' until reaching 'In what speech after it will they believe,' let him say: We believe in Allah. Whoever recites 'By the fig and the olive,' let him say: Yes, and I am among the witnesses to that. Whoever recites 'Is He not Able to give life to the dead,' let him say: Yes.""** Ismail said, ""I went to see whether he had memorized, as he was a Bedouin, and he said, 'O son of my brother, did you think I did not memorize it? I performed sixty pilgrimages, and there is none in which I do not know the camel upon which I performed it.'"""
"11458","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported concerning His saying, ""Its seal is musk"": It is not a seal with which it is sealed, but its mixture is musk. Have you not seen a woman among your women say, ""Mix it with such-and-such perfume""?"
"11459","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported concerning Allah's saying, ""It throws sparks like a palace"": They are not like trees or mountains, but like cities and fortresses."
"11460","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported concerning His saying, ""And We sent down from the mu'sirat a pouring water"": Al-mu'sirat are the winds, and pouring means gushing forth."
"11461","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported concerning, ""Abiding therein for huqbs"": The huqb is eighty years."
"11462","Abu Umama رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning, ""Abiding therein for huqbs"": > **""The huqb is thirty thousand years.""**"
"11463","Mahdi ibn Maymun reported: I heard al-Hasan ibn Dinar ask al-Hasan, ""Which verse is hardest upon the people of the Fire?"" He said, ""I asked Abu Barza, and he said, 'The hardest verse revealed is: Taste, for We shall increase you only in punishment.'"""
"11464","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما used to read this word as ""nakhirah,"" meaning decayed bones: ""When we are decayed bones?"""
"11465","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept asking about the Hour until there was revealed, ""What have you to do with its mention? To your Lord is its finality."""
"11466","Tariq ibn Shihab reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to mention the Hour abundantly until there was revealed, ""What have you to do with its mention? To your Lord is its finality."""
"11467","Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه reported: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, grey hair has come upon you quickly?"" He said: > **""Al-Waqia, Amma yatasa'alun, and When the sun is folded have turned me grey.""**"
"11468","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever wishes to look upon the Day of Resurrection as though seeing it with the eye, let him recite: When the sun is folded, When the sky splits open, and When the sky bursts apart.""** I think he said, ""And Sura Hud."""
"11469","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه was asked about His saying, ""When the buried girl is asked"": He said, ""Qays ibn Asim came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I buried daughters of mine in the pre-Islamic age.' He said: > **""Free a slave for each one of them.""** He said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I own camels.' He said: > **""Then sacrifice a camel for each one of them.""**"
"11470","Khalifa ibn Husayn reported: Qays ibn Asim said to the Prophet ﷺ, ""I buried twelve or thirteen daughters in the pre-Islamic age."" The Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""Free a soul for each one of them.""**"
"11471","Amr ibn Shurahbil al-Hamdani Abu Maysara reported from Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud: He asked, ""O Amr, what are 'the retreating, the moving, the hiding'?"" I said, ""The cows."" He said, ""And I hold the same view."""
"11472","Malik ibn al-Huwayrith رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When Allah intends to create a soul, and a man has intercourse with a woman, his fluid spreads through every vein and sinew of hers. On the seventh day Allah brings forth for it every lineage between it and Adam.""** Then he recited, ""In whatever form He willed, He composed you."""
"11473","Musa ibn Ali reported from his father from his grandfather: The Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""What has been born to you?""** He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what should be born to me but a boy or a girl?"" He said: > **""Whom will he resemble?""** He said, ""Whom should he resemble but his mother or his father?"" The Prophet ﷺ said to him then: > **""Stop, do not say so. When the drop settles in the womb, Allah brings forth for it every lineage between it and Adam. Have you not read this verse in Allah's Book: In whatever form He willed, He composed you?""**"
"11474","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Siba ibn Urfuta over Medina, and he recited, ""Woe to the defrauders."" I said, ""So-and-so is ruined; he has two measures, one by which he gives and one by which he takes."""
"11475","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, reported: Wayl is a valley in Hell of pus."
"11476","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite this verse, ""The Day mankind stands before the Lord of the worlds."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""How will it be with you when Allah gathers you as arrows are gathered in a quiver, for fifty thousand years, and He does not look toward you?""**"
"11477","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, reported concerning, ""You shall surely ride stage upon stage"": Muhammad reported to us: ""Heaven after heaven."""
"11478","Abdullah also reported concerning, ""You shall surely ride stage upon stage"": O Muhammad, meaning stage after stage."
"11479","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning the verse about riding onward from stage to stage that it refers to Muhammad ﷺ and his ascent through successive ranks and states."
"11480","Abu Malik al-Ashari رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained the promised day, the witness, the witnessed, the Friday gift, and the middle prayer. > **""The promised day is the Day of Resurrection, and the witness is Friday, and the witnessed is the day of Arafah. Friday was stored up for us by Allah, and the middle prayer is after the afternoon prayer.""**"
"11481","Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنه said concerning the verse of the witness and the witnessed that the witness is his grandfather the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the witnessed is the Day of Resurrection, and he recited the verse about being sent as a witness and bearer of glad tidings and a warner, along with the verse about the day when mankind shall be gathered, a day witnessed."
"11482","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning the verse of the witness and the witnessed that the witness is Muhammad ﷺ and the witnessed is the Day of Resurrection."
"11483","Abdurrahman ibn Khalid al-Adwani reports from his father that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the eastern quarter of Thaqif, standing leaning upon a bow or a staff, having come to them seeking support. He heard him recite the chapter of the Night Star to its end and memorized it while still a polytheist in the days of ignorance, then recited it again after entering Islam. When Thaqif asked him what he had heard from that man and he recited it before them, some Quraysh present with them said they knew their companion best, and that had they known what he said was truth they would have followed him."
"11484","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved the chapter beginning with glorification of the name of the Most High Lord."
"11485","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when Jibril عليه السلام would come to the Prophet ﷺ with revelation, the Prophet ﷺ would begin repeating its opening before Jibril finished, fearing it might escape him. Jibril asked him why he did so. > **""I fear that I may forget.""** Then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, sent down the verse that He shall teach him so that he shall not forget."
"11486","Awf ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he would command the charity of breaking the fast to be paid before the Eid prayer, and would recite the verses about the success of the one who purifies himself, remembers the name of his Lord, and prays."
"11487","Husaylah bint Wathilah reports that she heard her father explain the verses about the success of the one who purifies himself and remembers his Lord and prays as referring to giving wheat before the prayer at the prayer ground on the day of breaking the fast."
"11488","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ his explanation of the verse about the success of the one who purifies himself and the verse about remembering the name of the Lord and praying. > **""Whoever testifies that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, casts off all rivals, and testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah has purified himself. And the remembrance of the name of the Lord with prayer refers to the five prayers and guarding them.""**"
"11489","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the verses stating that this is in the former scriptures, the scriptures of Ibrahim and Musa, were revealed, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed their presence there. > **""All of this was in the scriptures of Ibrahim and Musa.""**"
"11490","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ his explanation of the ten nights and of the even and the odd. > **""The ten are the ten of sacrifice, the even is the day of sacrifice, and the odd is the day of Arafah.""**"
"11491","Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about the even and the odd and answered with two days and a night. > **""They are two days and a night: the day of Arafah, the day of sacrifice, and the odd is the night of sacrifice, the night of the gathering at Muzdalifah.""**"
"11492","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explained the opening oaths of the chapter of the City: the city is Makkah, the dwelling therein is Makkah, the father is Adam عليه السلام, and the creation of man in toil means in upright and erect form."
"11493","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning the verse swearing by this city that it is an oath laid upon an oath, an emphatic swearing."
"11494","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said concerning the verse about guiding man to the two highways that they are the path of good and the path of evil."
"11495","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Prophet ﷺ recited the verses swearing by the soul and its fashioning and its inspiration with wickedness and piety, he would pause and supplicate. > **""O Allah, grant my soul its piety. You are its Guardian and the best to purify it.""**"
"11496","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said that the closing verses of the chapter of the Night, about the one with no favor to be repaid who spends only seeking the countenance of his Most High Lord and shall surely be pleased, were revealed concerning Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه."
"11497","Hafs ibn Maysarah al-Qurashi reports from his mother, from her mother, who had served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that a puppy entered the house, crept beneath the bed, and died there. Revelation was withheld for some days, and the Prophet ﷺ asked his servant Khawlah what had happened in his house that Jibril did not come to him. She answered that no day had passed upon them better than their present day. He took his cloak and went out. She then thought to tidy the house and sweep, and sweeping beneath the bed she felt something heavy there and kept at it until she drew it out, and it was the dead puppy. She took it in her hand and cast it behind the house. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned, his beard trembling as it would when revelation came to him, he asked to be covered, and Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down the opening verses of the chapter of the Morning Light. > **""O Khawlah, what has happened in the house of the Messenger of Allah that Jibril does not come to me? Has anything new happened in the house of the Messenger of Allah? Cover me.""**"
"11498","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that there was displayed before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what would be opened for his community after him, conquest upon conquest, and he rejoiced at it. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down the verse promising that his Lord shall give him until he is pleased, and Allah the Exalted shall grant him in Paradise a thousand palaces, in each palace youths and servants as befits him."
"11499","In another narration Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said what would be opened for his community after him had been displayed to him and had pleased him, whereupon Allah the Exalted sent down the verse that the Hereafter is better for him than the first, followed by the like of the preceding report. > **""What will be opened for my community after me was displayed to me and it pleased me.""**"
"11500","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that were hardship to enter a hole, ease would enter after it until it expelled it, and then he recited the verse that with hardship comes ease. > **""Were hardship to be in a hole, ease would come upon it until it expelled it.""**"
"11501","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated, looking toward a burrow before his face, and saying that were hardship to come until it entered that burrow, ease would come until it expelled it. Then he recited the two verses that with hardship comes ease, with hardship comes ease. > **""Were hardship to come until it entered this burrow, ease would come until it expelled it.""**"
"11502","Abu Raja al-Utaridi reports that Abu Musa رضي الله عنه would teach them the Quran, seating them in circles, wearing two white garments. When he recited the chapter commanding reading in the name of the Lord who created, he said that this was the first chapter sent down upon Muhammad ﷺ."
"11503","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that Abu Jahl declared that if Muhammad returned to praying toward the qiblah he would surely kill him. When he returned to prayer, Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down the opening of the chapter of the Clot down to the verses commanding him to call his assembly while He shall call the guards. When it was said to Abu Jahl that he had returned, he claimed a barrier had come between him and the Prophet. Ibn Abbas swore by Allah that had he moved, the angels would have seized him while the people looked on."
"11504","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that Abu Jahl passed by and said he had forbidden the Prophet ﷺ, who rebuked him. Abu Jahl boasted that none had a greater assembly than he, whereupon Jibril عليه السلام spoke the verse commanding him to call his assembly. Ibn Abbas swore by Allah that had he called his assembly, the stern guards would have seized him with punishment. Part of the report occurs in the sound collection."
"11505","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Quran was sent down on the Night of Decree in the month of Ramadan to the lowest heaven all at once, and then sent down thereafter in portions."
"11506","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning the verse about sending down on the Night of Decree that the Quran was sent down all at once until it was placed in the House of Might in the lowest heaven, and Jibril brought it down upon Muhammad ﷺ in answer to the speech and deeds of men."
"11507","Abu Waqid al-Laythi رضي الله عنه reports that they would come to the Prophet ﷺ when revelation descended and he would inform them of it, and that one day he conveyed a divine word about wealth sent down for prayer and charity and the hunger of man for more. > **""Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: We sent down wealth for the establishment of prayer and the giving of charity. Were the son of Adam to have a valley, he would love to have a second to it, and were he to have two valleys, he would love to have a third to them. Nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam except dust, then Allah turns to whoever repents.""**"
"11508","Ubayy ibn Kaab رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah had commanded him to recite before him, and then recited before him the opening verses of the chapter of the Clear Proof, explaining that the true religion with Allah is the upright way, free of association, Judaism and Christianity, and that no good deed shall be denied, followed by further verses and the parable of the valleys of wealth, and then the remainder of the chapter. > **""Allah has commanded me to recite to you: Those who disbelieved among the People of the Book and the polytheists would not desist until the clear proof came to them, a messenger from Allah reciting purified pages wherein are upright writings. The religion with Allah is the upright way, neither associating nor Jewish nor Christian, and whoever does good shall never be denied it. Were the son of Adam to have two valleys of wealth, he would ask for a third, and nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam except dust.""**"
"11509","In another narration from Ubayy ibn Kaab رضي الله عنه, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah the Blessed and Exalted had commanded him to recite the Quran before him, and he mentioned the like of the preceding report, with the parable of a man given valley after valley yet asking for more. Part of it occurs in the collection of al-Tirmidhi and part in the sound collection. > **""Allah the Blessed and Exalted has commanded me to recite the Quran to you. Were the son of Adam to ask for a valley of wealth and be given it, he would ask for a second, and were he to ask for a second and be given it, he would ask for a third.""**"
"11510","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that a man came to Umar رضي الله عنه asking for aid, and Umar looked him over from head to foot for signs of hardship, then asked about his wealth. The man answered forty camels, whereupon Ibn Abbas declared the truth of Allah and His Messenger with the parable of two valleys of gold and a third sought, and of dust filling the belly, and of repentance accepted. Umar asked its source, and Ibn Abbas traced it to Ubayy, whom they then visited. Ubayy confirmed that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had taught him thus, and affirmed that it be established in the codex. > **""Were the son of Adam to have two valleys of gold, he would seek a third. Nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam except dust, and Allah turns to whoever repents.""**"
"11511","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that a man came to Umar saying drought had consumed them. Umar asked his tribe and kept tracing his lineage until he recognized him as a man of means, and then spoke the parable of one valley and two valleys and the third sought, and of dust and repentance. > **""Were the son of Adam to have a valley and two valleys, he would seek a third to them. Nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam except dust, then Allah turns to whoever repents.""**"
"11512","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the verse of the quaking of the earth was revealed while Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه sat nearby, and Abu Bakr wept. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him what made him weep, and he answered that this chapter had made him weep, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ spoke of error, sin and forgiveness. > **""What makes you weep, O Abu Bakr? Were you never to err and never to sin, Allah the Exalted would surely create a nation after you who would err and sin, and He would forgive them.""**"
"11513","Sasasah ibn Muawiyah, the uncle of al-Farazdaq, reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who recited before him the verses that whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it and whoever does an atom's weight of evil shall see it. He answered that this was sufficient for him and that he cared not if he heard nothing else."
"11514","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq was eating with the Prophet ﷺ when the verses of the atom's weight of good and evil were revealed, and Abu Bakr withdrew his hand and asked whether he would truly see every speck of evil he had done. The Prophet ﷺ answered that worldly hardships reckon against evil specks while good specks are stored until full payment on the Day of Resurrection. > **""O Abu Bakr, what you see in this world of what you dislike reckons for the specks of evil, and the specks of good are stored up for you until you are paid them in full on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"11515","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched horsemen who remained a month without news, whereupon the chapter of the charging steeds was revealed. He explained their panting with their legs, their striking fire from stones with their hooves, their morning raid upon the enemy, their raising of dust with their hooves, and their penetrating into the gathered host at dawn."
"11516","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the ingrate was mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ, who described him by solitary eating, withholding of aid, and beating of his servant. > **""He is the one who eats alone, withholds his help, and beats his servant.""**"
"11517","Mahmud ibn Labid رضي الله عنه reports that when the chapter of rivalry in increase was revealed and recited down to the verse that you shall surely be questioned that day about blessing, the people asked about which blessing they would be questioned, possessing only dates and water, with swords upon their necks and the enemy present. He answered that it would come to be. > **""That shall surely come to be.""**"
"11518","Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports that when the verse that you shall surely be questioned that day about blessing was revealed, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam asked which blessing they would be questioned about when they had only water and dates. He answered that it would come to be. > **""It shall surely come to be.""**"
"11519","Hasan reports that when the verse that you shall surely be questioned that day about blessing was revealed, the people asked which blessing they would be questioned about with swords upon their shoulders, and then the narrator mentioned the rest of the report. > **""About which blessing shall we be questioned?""**"
"11520","Asma bint Yazid رضي الله عنها reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the verses of the accustomed security of Quraysh in their winter and summer journeys that he called them to worship the Lord who fed them against hunger and secured them against fear. > **""Woe to you, O Quraysh. Worship your Lord who fed you against hunger and secured you against fear.""**"
"11521","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ they counted small kindness as the bucket, the axe and the cooking pot. Part of the report was narrated by Abu Dawud without mention of the axe."
"11522","Hafsah bint Sirin reports that Umm Atiyyah said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them not to withhold small kindness, and when asked what small kindness meant, she explained it as what people commonly pass among themselves. > **""Do not withhold small kindness.""**"
"11523","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said concerning the verse about withholding small kindness that it means the loan of things for use."
"11524","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that he asked the Prophet ﷺ about the verse concerning those heedless in their prayer, who answered that they are those who postpone it beyond its proper time. Other routes of this report have preceded in the book of prayer. > **""They are those who delay it beyond its time.""**"
"11525","Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه reports that when Ibrahim, the son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, died, the polytheists went about to one another saying the renegade had been cut off that night, whereupon Allah the Exalted sent down the chapter of Abundance to its end. The report comes within a long narration distributed across its proper places."
"11526","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه said concerning the verse of granting Abundance that it is a hollow river in Paradise with vessels of gold and silver, known to none but Allah."
"11527","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse of granting Abundance. Other reports of the Basin shall come in the book of resurrection."
"11528","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the chapter of divine help and victory was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his soul had been announced to him that he would be taken that year. In the longer narration he called Fatimah رضي الله عنها and told her his soul had been announced to him, and she wept, and then the narrator mentioned the rest of the report. > **""My soul has been announced to me that I shall be taken in this year.""**"
"11529","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the chapter of the ruin of the hands of Abu Lahab was revealed, the wife of Abu Lahab came to the Prophet ﷺ while Abu Bakr was with him. Abu Bakr feared her foul tongue might harm him and suggested he rise, but the Prophet ﷺ answered she would not see him. She asked Abu Bakr where his companion who had lampooned them was, and Abu Bakr answered he spoke no poetry, which she accepted before departing. The Prophet ﷺ explained that an angel had kept covering him from her with his wings, and in the version of al-Bazzar that a barrier would come between him and her. > **""She shall never see me. An angel kept covering me from her with his two wings.""**"
"11530","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports in raised form that the Solid is the One without hollow. > **""The Solid is the One who has no hollow.""**"
"11531","Dahhak ibn Muzahim reports that Nafi ibn al-Azraq asked Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما about the divine word the Solid, saying the One they knew but asking what the Solid meant. Ibn Abbas answered that He is the One resorted to in all matters, and when asked whether the Arabs knew this before the Book came down upon Muhammad ﷺ, he cited the verse of the Asadiyyah woman praising Amr ibn Masud as the master resorted to, and Nafi conceded his truth. The full report precedes in the chapter of how the Quran is explained."
"11532","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a man reciting the chapter of devout sincerity and declared that Paradise had become binding for him. > **""This one has made it binding, Paradise has become binding for him.""**"
"11533","An elder who met the Prophet ﷺ reports that he went out with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey and they passed a man reciting the chapter of the disbelievers, of whom he said this one had been cleared of association, and then another reciting the chapter of devout sincerity, of whom the Prophet ﷺ said Paradise had thereby become binding for him. > **""As for this one, he has been cleared of association. By this, Paradise has become binding for him.""**"
"11534","In another narration of the same journey, the wording is that as for this reciter, he has been forgiven. > **""As for this one, he has surely been forgiven.""**"
"11535","Muadh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that whoever recites the chapter of devout sincerity ten times shall have a house built for him in Paradise. When Umar ibn al-Khattab answered that they would then multiply such houses, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered that Allah is more abundant and more gracious. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, ten times, Allah shall build for him a house in Paradise. Allah is more abundant and more gracious.""**"
"11536","Ibn al-Daylami, the nephew of the Negus who had served the Prophet ﷺ, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever recites the chapter of devout sincerity a hundred times, in prayer or elsewhere, shall have immunity from the Fire written for him. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, a hundred times in prayer or otherwise, Allah shall write for him immunity from the Fire.""**"
"11537","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that ten recitations build a palace in Paradise, twenty build two palaces, and thirty build three. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, ten times, a palace shall be built for him in Paradise. Whoever recites it twenty times, two palaces shall be built for him, and whoever recites it thirty times, three shall be built for him.""**"
"11538","Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever recites the chapter of devout sincerity during the illness in which he dies shall suffer no trial in his grave, shall be spared the compression of the grave, and shall be carried by the angels upon their palms on the Day of Resurrection until they bring him across the Bridge into Paradise. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, during the illness in which he dies shall not be tried in his grave, shall be spared the compression of the grave, and the angels shall carry him on the Day of Resurrection upon their palms until they bring him across the Bridge into Paradise.""**"
"11539","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever recites the chapter of devout sincerity twelve times after the dawn prayer is as one who recited the Quran four times, and shall be the most excellent of the people of the earth that day, provided he guards himself. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, twelve times after the dawn prayer, it is as if he recited the Quran four times, and he shall be the most excellent of the people of the earth that day, when he guards himself.""**"
"11540","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said reciting the chapter of devout sincerity is as reciting a third of the Quran, and reciting the chapter of the disbelievers is as reciting a quarter of the Quran. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, it is as if he recited a third of the Quran, and whoever recites Say, O disbelievers, it is as if he recited a quarter of the Quran.""**"
"11541","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever recites the chapter of devout sincerity fifty times each day shall be called on the Day of Resurrection from his grave to rise and enter Paradise. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, fifty times each day shall be called on the Day of Resurrection from his grave: Rise, O praiser of Allah, and enter Paradise.""**"
"11542","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the people asked for the lineage of their Lord, whereupon the chapter of devout sincerity was revealed to its end. In the version of Abu Yala a desert Arab came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to genealogize Allah."
"11543","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said every thing has a lineage, and the lineage of Allah is the chapter of devout sincerity. > **""Every thing has a lineage, and the lineage of Allah is Say, He is Allah, the One.""**"
"11544","Hamzah ibn Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam reports that Abdullah ibn Salam رضي الله عنه told the rabbis he had taken a covenant at the mosque of their father Ibrahim and Ismail, then set out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Makkah, finding him at Mina with the people around him after their return from the pilgrimage. The Prophet ﷺ recognized him and bade him draw near, then adjured him whether he found him in the Torah as the Messenger of Allah. He answered by asking him to describe their Lord, whereupon Jibril came and stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the full chapter of devout sincerity, which the Messenger recited before them, and Abdullah ibn Salam testified that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that he is the Messenger of Allah. The complete report belongs in the merits of Abdullah ibn Salam. > **""I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"11545","Salamah ibn Wardan reports that Anas ibn Malik, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, told him the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked one of his companions whether he had married. The man answered he had nothing with which to marry, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked him in turn about the chapters of devout sincerity, the disbelievers, the quaking, divine help, and the verse of the Throne, assigning each its weight of the Quran, and then bade him marry thrice. > **""Have you not with you Say, He is Allah, the One? It is a quarter of the Quran. Have you not with you Say, O disbelievers? It is a quarter of the Quran. Have you not with you When the earth is shaken? It is a quarter of the Quran. Have you not with you When the help of Allah comes? It is a quarter of the Quran. Have you not with you the verse of the Throne? It is a quarter of the Quran. Marry, marry, marry.""**"
"11546","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari was in a gathering asking whether they could not stand a third of the Quran each night. When they asked whether they could bear that, he answered that the chapter of devout sincerity is a third of the Quran. The Prophet ﷺ then arrived hearing Abu Ayyub and confirmed his truthfulness. > **""Abu Ayyub has spoken the truth.""**"
"11547","Ubayy ibn Kaab رضي الله عنه, or a man of the Ansar, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever recites the chapter of devout sincerity has recited as a third of the Quran. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, it is as if he recited a third of the Quran.""**"
"11548","Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the chapter of devout sincerity equals a third of the Quran. > **""Say, He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Quran.""**"
"11549","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether one of them could not recite the Quran in the night with the chapter of devout sincerity, for it equals the whole Quran, and then the narrator mentioned the rest of the report. > **""Can one of you not recite the Quran in the night with Say, He is Allah, the One, for it equals the whole Quran?""**"
"11550","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ask whether one of them could not recite the chapter of devout sincerity three times in a night, for it equals a third of the Quran. The complete report shall come in the chapter of evil workers and helpers of tyrants in the book of the caliphate. > **""Can one of you not recite Say, He is Allah, the One, three times in a night, for it equals a third of the Quran?""**"
"11551","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say whoever recites the chapter of devout sincerity has recited as a third of the Quran. > **""Whoever recites Say, He is Allah, the One, it is as if he recited a third of the Quran.""**"
"11552","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether one of them could not recite a third of the Quran in a night. When they asked who could bear that, he asked whether they could not recite the chapter of devout sincerity, for it equals a third of the Quran. > **""Can one of you not recite a third of the Quran in a night? Can he not recite Say, He is Allah, the One, for it equals a third of the Quran?""**"
"11553","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the chapter of devout sincerity equals a third of the Quran. > **""Say, He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Quran.""**"
"11554","On the authority of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported concerning the merit of Surah al-Ikhlas that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about its equivalence to a portion of the Quran. > **""Say: He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Quran.""**"
"11555","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the reward of two surahs and mentioned his practice in the two rakahs of Fajr, adding a remark about the lasting merit of those rakahs. > **""Say: He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Quran, and Say: O disbelievers, equals a fourth of the Quran. These two rakahs contain the devotion of a lifetime.""**"
"11556","From Abu al-Ala, that is Yazid ibn Abdillah ibn al-Shikhkhir, who conveyed a report from a man who was traveling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when mounts were few and people took turns riding. When the turn of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the turn of that man coincided, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came up behind him, struck his shoulder, directed him to recite Surat al-Falaq and then Surat al-Nas, reciting each along with him, and then gave him guidance about prayer. > **""Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak. Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind. When you pray, recite them both.""**"
"11557","On the authority of Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, who met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and was addressed about certain surahs whose like was never sent down in the Tawrah, the Zabur, the Injil, or the Furqan, and about the nightly practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with them. > **""O Uqbah ibn Amir, shall I not teach you surahs the like of which was not sent down in the Tawrah, nor in the Zabur, nor in the Injil, nor in the Furqan? No night comes but that I recite in it: Say: He is Allah, the One, and Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak, and Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind.""**"
"11558","On the authority of Abu Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning verses newly sent down upon him and their unmatched status, identifying them as the two refuges. > **""Verses have been sent down upon me the like of which has never been sent down upon me: the two refuges.""**"
"11559","On the authority of Abdillah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, who described an Umrah journey with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when mist near Batn Waqim caused them to lose the road until they came upon a mountain pass. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned aside to a sand dune, halted his mount, stood in prayer until dawn with whoever Allah willed, then took hold of his she-camel's head and walked with Abdillah al-Aslami alone beside him, placed his hand on his chest, and instructed him in seeking refuge. > **""Say. Say: He is Allah, the One. Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak, from the evil of what He created. Say. Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind. Seek refuge in this manner, for servants have never sought refuge with anything like them.""**"
"11560","On the authority of Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how Satan attaches himself to the heart of the son of Adam, withdraws upon remembrance and seizes the heart upon forgetfulness, identifying that reality. > **""Satan places his snout upon the heart of the son of Adam. If he remembers Allah, he withdraws, and if he forgets, he devours his heart. That is the whisperer who withdraws.""**"
"11561","From Zirr, who said to Ubayy, may Allah be pleased with him, that his brother removes the two refuges from the mushaf. When asked whether Sufyan meant Ibn Masud, he did not deny it. Ubayy replied that he had asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about them and described the answer he received, affirming continued adherence to that practice. > **""It was said to me, so I said it. So we say as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said.""**"
"11562","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid al-Nakhai, who reported that Abdillah would erase the two refuges from his copies of the mushaf and state his view that they were not part of the Book of Allah, the Blessed and Exalted."
"11563","On the authority of Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, who would erase the two refuges from the mushaf and explain his position by saying that the Prophet ﷺ had commanded seeking refuge through them, while Abdillah himself would not recite them."
"11564","On the authority of Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about these two surahs and explained that he had been commanded to recite them, directing others to follow his recitation. > **""It was said to me, so I said it. So say as I said.""**"
"11565","On the authority of Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak about the modes in which the Quran was sent down. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf.""**"
"11566","With the chain of Ahmad, on the authority of Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met Jibril at Ahjar al-Mira, mentioned that he had been sent to an unlettered community of men, women, boys, girls, and aged persons who had never read a book, and received an answer concerning the modes of the Quran. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf.""**"
"11567","On the authority of Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met Jibril at Ahjar al-Mira, mentioned being sent to an unlettered people who had never read a book, and that Jibril conveyed Allah's command, with Mikail repeatedly requesting an increase until the number reached seven. > **""Read the Quran upon one harf. Read upon two ahruf. Read upon seven ahruf.""**"
"11568","On the authority of Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about the seven modes of the Quran, the correctness of whichever mode is recited, and the prohibition of disputing about it. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf. Upon whichever harf you recite, you are correct, so do not dispute, for disputation about it is disbelief.""**"
"11569","From Abu Qays, the freedman of Amr ibn al-As, who reported that Amr heard a man reciting a verse of the Quran, questioned who had taught him, was told it was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had taught it to him differently. Both went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, each recited in turn, each recitation was confirmed as revealed, and then a general principle about the seven modes and disputation was stated. > **""Thus it was sent down. Thus it was sent down. This Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf, so whichever of them you recite, you have done correctly, and do not dispute about it, for disputation about it is disbelief, or it is disbelief in it.""**"
"11570","On the authority of Abu Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that a man recited before Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who objected, but the man said he had recited before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without objection. Both gathered before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, one recited before the Prophet ﷺ and was praised, Umar felt unease, and the Prophet ﷺ addressed Umar with a principle about the correctness of the Quran and its limits. > **""You have done well. O Umar, the whole Quran is correct so long as mercy is not made punishment or punishment mercy.""**"
"11571","On the authority of Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that Jibril, peace be upon him, addressed Muhammad ﷺ about reciting upon one harf, that Mikail repeatedly asked for an increase until seven ahruf were reached, and that every mode was described as healing and sufficient within a stated limit, with synonymous expressions given as illustration. > **""Read the Quran upon one harf. Read upon two ahruf. Read upon three ahruf. Read upon seven ahruf. Each is healing and sufficient so long as a verse of punishment is not sealed with mercy, nor mercy with punishment, like your saying: come and approach, hasten and go, hurry and quicken.""**"
"11572","On the authority of Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met Jibril at Ahjar al-Mira and that Jibril conveyed guidance about the community reciting upon seven ahruf, about holding to what one learned, and, in the wording of Ibn Mahdi, about the weak and not shifting away out of dislike. > **""Your community will recite the Quran upon seven ahruf, so whoever among them recites upon a harf should recite as he learned and not turn away from it. Among your community are the weak, so whoever recites upon a harf should not move to another out of dislike for it.""**"
"11573","On the authority of Abu al-Juhaym, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that two men differed over a verse of the Quran, each claiming to have received it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and that they asked the Prophet ﷺ, who gave them a ruling about the modes and about disputing. > **""The Quran is recited upon seven ahruf, so do not dispute about the Quran, for disputation about the Quran is disbelief.""**"
"11574","On the authority of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported from the Prophet ﷺ a statement about the seven modes, repeated three times, concerning disputation, followed by instruction about acting upon what is known and referring back what is unknown to one who knows it. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf. Disputation about the Quran is disbelief. What you know of it, act upon it, and what you do not know of it, refer it back to one who knows it.""**"
"11575","On the authority of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in one narration describing the seven modes with paired divine attributes, and on the authority of Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Quran was presented before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ three times, with the narrator adding that people considered their own recitation to be the final one, while expressing uncertainty whether that remark belonged to the hadith itself. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf: Knowing, Wise, Forgiving, Merciful.""**"
"11576","On the authority of Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about the modes upon which the Quran was sent down. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf.""**"
"11577","In another narration from the same authority, the number of modes was mentioned differently, stating three instead of seven. > **""Upon three ahruf.""**"
"11578","From Abu al-Minhal, that is Sayyar ibn Salamah, who reported that it reached him that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, stood one day upon the minbar, adjured by Allah anyone who had heard the Prophet ﷺ speak about the seven modes and their sufficiency to stand, whereupon so many stood that they could not be counted and bore witness to it, and Uthman added his own testimony with them. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf, all of them healing and sufficient.""**"
"11579","On the authority of Abdillah, that is Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ spoke about the seven modes and about every verse having an outer and an inner aspect, and forbade a man to recline in the mosque placing one foot over the other, with the narrator expressing slight uncertainty about the exact wording of the place. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf, and for every verse of it there is an outer aspect and an inner aspect.""**"
"11580","On the authority of Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command reciting the Quran as he had taught it and explained that it had been sent down upon three modes, describing it as wholly blessed and directing adherence to what was taught without contention. > **""It was sent down upon three ahruf, so do not contend about it, for it is all blessed, so recite it as you were taught it.""**"
"11581","From Fulfulah al-Jufi, who hastened with others to Abdillah concerning the copies of the mushaf, entered upon him, and heard from one of the company that they had come not as visitors but because a report had alarmed them. Abdillah then explained that the Quran came down to their Prophet ﷺ through seven gates upon seven ahruf, while the Book before it used to come down through one gate upon one harf."
"11582","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Abis, who reported from an unnamed man of Hamdan among the companions of Abdillah that when Abdillah intended to go to Madinah he gathered his companions, expressed hope about their superiority in religion, understanding, and knowledge of the Quran, described the Quran as without discrepancy, never worn out, and never rendered stale by much repetition, and instructed each to hold to the harf he had learned and to what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had taught, warning that denial of one verse is denial of all, and illustrating synonymous expressions of hastening."
"11583","On the authority of Umar ibn Abu Salamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that earlier books came down from heaven through one gate while the Quran came down through seven gates upon seven ahruf, comprising the lawful, the unlawful, the decisive, the ambiguous, parables, command, and prohibition, and then gave him instruction about declaring lawful, declaring unlawful, acting, pausing, and reflecting, since all is from Allah and only people of understanding take heed. > **""Books used to come down from heaven through one gate, and the Quran was sent down through seven gates upon seven ahruf: lawful, unlawful, decisive, ambiguous, striking of parables, command, and prohibition. So declare its lawful lawful, declare its unlawful unlawful, act upon its decisive verses, pause at its ambiguous ones, and take heed from its parables, for all of it is from Allah, and none takes heed except people of understanding.""**"
"11584","On the authority of Abdillah, that is Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said that no letter of this Quran is without a limit, and for every limit there is a point of ascent."
"11585","On the authority of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about the seven modes and about disputation concerning the Quran. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf, and disputation about the Quran is disbelief.""**"
"11586","On the authority of Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the modes of the Quran and their sufficiency. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf, all of them healing and sufficient.""**"
"11587","On the authority of Sulayman ibn Surad, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that two angels came to Muhammad ﷺ, one said to recite upon one harf, the other kept asking for an increase, and the increase continued until seven was reached. > **""Recite the Quran upon one harf. Increase him. Recite upon seven ahruf.""**"
"11588","From Zayd al-Qassar, from Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, with whom people were sitting in the mosque when he narrated at length and then reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said that Abdillah ibn Masud, Zayd, and Ubayy had each taught him the same surah with differing recitations, and asked whose recitation to adopt. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained silent while Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was at his side, and Ali instructed that every person should recite as he had learned, for all of it is good and beautiful."
"11589","On the authority of Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, who stated that the Quran was sent down through seven gates upon seven modes, all of them healing and sufficient."
"11590","On the authority of Umm Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with her, who reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the seven modes and the correctness of whichever one is recited. > **""The Quran was sent down upon seven ahruf; whichever of them you recite, you are correct.""**"
"11591","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who used to recite the phrase about hearts being wrapped with emphasis, explaining it to mean heavy vessels of wisdom, and questioning how one who is a teacher could be otherwise when hearts are vessels of wisdom."
"11592","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that two men of the Ansar recited a surah taught to them by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and stood one night praying with it, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them it was among what had been abrogated or caused to be forgotten and that they should turn away from it. The narrator also noted the recitation of al-Zuhri with lightening and nominative form in the verse about abrogation and causing to forget. > **""It is among what was abrogated or caused to be forgotten, so turn away from it.""**"
"11593","From Amr ibn Rafi, the freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who used to write copies of the mushaf in the time of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ. He reported that Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, asked him to write a mushaf for her, instructed him not to write the verse of Surah al-Baqarah about prayers until he brought her the sheet so she could dictate it as she had memorized it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he brought it she dictated it with an explicit mention of the Asr prayer alongside the middle prayer and standing devoutly for Allah."
"11594","From Abu Khalid al-Kinani, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who used to recite the divine names in Ayat al-Kursi with the form indicating the Ever-Living, the Sustainer."
"11595","On the authority of Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse about what was decreed for them therein, life for life and eye for eye, pronouncing the word for life in the accusative and the word for eye in the nominative. > **""And We decreed for them therein: life for life, and the eye for the eye.""**"
"11596","From Masud ibn Yazid al-Kindi, who reported that Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, was teaching a man to recite, and the man read the verse about charities being for the poor and needy without prolongation. Ibn Masud told him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not taught it to him in that manner, and when asked how he had been taught, he recited it with prolongation and instructed that it be prolonged."
"11597","On the authority of Abdillah, that is Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who used to recite the verse about the ship with the wording for its course and its anchorage."
"11598","On the authority of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the verse about Nuh's son with the meaning that he was an act not righteous. > **""Indeed, he is an act not righteous.""**"
"11599","From Shaqiq, who reported that in the presence of Abdillah they read the verse of seduction with one vocalization, whereupon Abdillah corrected them to another vocalization, said they had already asked about it, and stated that to recite as he was taught was dearer to him."
"11600","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the verse about the one with whom is knowledge of the Book. > **""And from Him is knowledge of the Book.""**"
"11601","On the authority of Abdillah, that is Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who recited the verse about the dependent one with the meaning that wherever he is directed, he brings no good."
"11602","From al-Amash, who reported that Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to recite the verse about the decree of the Lord with the wording that He decreed that they worship none but Him."
"11603","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse about the setting of the sun with the wording indicating a muddy spring. > **""In a muddy spring.""**"
"11604","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said he had memorized the whole Sunnah but did not know how a particular word about reaching extreme old age should be read, whether with one consonant or another."
"11605","On the authority of Tamim ibn Hadlam, may Allah be pleased with him, who read the whole Quran before Abdillah and was corrected in only two letters: in the verse about all coming to Him in humility, and in the verse about the messengers despairing and thinking they had been denied, where Abdillah affirmed the passive intensive form."
"11606","On the authority of Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, who recited the verse about wonder and mockery with the first-person form for wondering."
"11607","On the authority of Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse about the signs coming and being denied and met with arrogance with feminine address. > **""Yes indeed, My signs came to you, but you denied them and were arrogant.""**"
"11608","On the authority of Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite the verse about reclining with the wording indicating green cushions and fair carpets. > **""Upon green cushions and fair carpets.""**"
"11609","On the authority of Qutbah ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting the verse about tall palm trees with the letter sad. > **""And tall palm trees.""**"
"11610","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse in Surah al-Waqiah about rest and fragrance. > **""Rest and fragrance.""**"
"11611","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who recited Surah al-Waqiah before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he reached the verse about rest and fragrance, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him by name in correction. > **""O Ibn Umar.""**"
"11612","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited the verse about drinking like the intensely thirsty camel. > **""Drinking the drink of the intensely thirsty.""**"
"11613","From al-Amash, who heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, explain the words about most upright speech to mean most truthful. When told that it is read as most upright, he replied that most upright and most truthful are one and the same."
"11614","From Salim ibn Marwan, who used to send to Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, asking for the mushaf from which the Quran had been copied, but she refused to give it to him. After her burial, Marwan sent to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, requesting that mushaf, and he sent it to him."
"11615","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that two men of the Ansar sought to recite a surah taught to them by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which they used to recite, but one night when they stood praying with it they could not recall a single letter of it. In the morning they came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned it to him, and he informed them of its status. > **""It is among what was abrogated and caused to be forgotten.""**"
"11616","From Abu Ishaq, who reported that Umayyah ibn Abdillah ibn Khalid ibn Usayd led them in prayer in Khurasan and recited from the two surahs containing the words about seeking help and seeking forgiveness, after which the narrator mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"11617","On the authority of Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade saying Surah al-Baqarah, Surah Al Imran, or Surah al-Nisa in that form, extending the ruling to the whole Quran, and instead instructed naming each surah by what is mentioned in it. > **""Do not say Surah al-Baqarah, nor Surah Al Imran, nor Surah al-Nisa, and likewise the whole Quran. Rather say: the surah in which the cow is mentioned, and the surah in which Al Imran is mentioned, and likewise the whole Quran.""**"
"11618","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who reported that the Quran was separated from the Remembrance, placed in the House of Might in the lowest heaven, and Jibril, peace be upon him, then conveyed it to the Prophet ﷺ in measured recitation."
"11619","On the authority of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, who reported that Jibril, peace be upon him, used to dictate to the Prophet ﷺ."
"11620","On the authority of Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about the three places in which the Quran was sent down. > **""The Quran was sent down in three places: Makkah, Madinah, and Sham.""**"
"11621","On the authority of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said that the Mufassal was sent down at Makkah and that they remained for years reciting it with nothing else coming down."
"11622","On the authority of Ali, that is Ibn Abu Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning four surahs that no hypocrite memorizes. > **""No hypocrite memorizes the surahs of Baraah, Ya Sin, al-Dukhan, and Amma yatasaalun.""**"
"11623","From Masruq, who reported that Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to dislike explanatory commentary within the Quran."
"11624","From Abu al-Zara, who reported that Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, said to keep the Quran unadorned and not to clothe it with what is not part of it."
"11625","On the authority of Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about what he was given in place of the Tawrah, the Zabur, and the Injil, and about being favored with the Mufassal. > **""I was given the Seven in place of the Tawrah, I was given the Miin in place of the Zabur, I was given the Mathani in place of the Injil, and I was favored with the Mufassal.""**"
"11626","On the authority of Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about what his Lord had granted him in place of the Tawrah and the Injil, and about being favored with the Mufassal. > **""My Lord gave me the Seven Long surahs in place of the Tawrah, and the Miin in place of the Injil, and I was favored with the Mufassal.""**"
"11627","On the authority of Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about the Quran placed in a skin and then cast into fire, stating that it would not burn. > **""If the Quran were placed in a skin and then cast into fire, it would not burn.""**"
"11628","On the authority of Ismah, may Allah be pleased with him, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke about the Quran gathered in a skin and the effect of fire upon it. > **""If the Quran were gathered in a skin, fire would not burn it.""**"
"11629","On the authority of Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If the Quran were in a skin, the Fire would not touch it.""**"
"11630","On the authority of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed the Quran is a richness after which there is no poverty, and there is no richness apart from it.""**"
"11631","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Quran: there is no poverty after it, and no richness apart from it.""**"
"11632","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""Whoever recites the Quran, it is as if prophethood has been enfolded between his two sides, except that no revelation comes to him. And whoever recites the Quran and then considers that anyone has been given something better than what he has been given has magnified what Allah deemed small and belittled what Allah deemed great. And it does not befit the bearer of the Quran to act foolishly with those who act foolishly, nor to grow angry with those who grow angry, nor to rage with those who rage; rather he should pardon and overlook, out of honour for the Quran.""**"
"11633","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was sitting with the Prophet ﷺ and I heard him say: > **""Learn Al-Baqarah, for taking it is blessing and leaving it is regret, and the idle sorcerers cannot withstand it.""** Buraydah said: Then he fell silent for a while, then he said: > **""Learn Al-Baqarah and Āl ʿImrān, for they are the two radiant ones. They will shade their companion on the Day of Resurrection as though they were two clouds, or two canopies, or two flocks of birds in ranks. And indeed the Quran will meet its companion on the Day of Resurrection when his grave is opened for him, in the form of a pale man, and it will say: Do you know me? He will say: I do not know you. It will say: I am your companion, the Quran, who kept you thirsty in the midday heat and kept your night awake. And indeed every trader is behind his trade, and today you are beyond every trade. So dominion will be given to him in his right hand and eternity in his left, and the crown of dignity will be placed upon his head, and his two parents will be clothed with two garments to which the world cannot compare. They will say: For what have we been clothed with this? It will be said: For your child's taking of the Quran. Then it will be said: Recite and ascend through the steps of Paradise and its chambers. So he will continue ascending as long as he recites, whether swiftly or with measured recitation.""**"
"11634","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Everything has a hump, and the hump of the Quran is Sūrah Al-Baqarah. And indeed everything has a core, and the core of the Quran is the Mufaṣṣal. And indeed the devils leave the house in which Sūrah Al-Baqarah is recited. And indeed the smallest of houses is the hollow interior in which there is nothing of the Book of Allah."
"11635","On the authority of Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to learn the Quran and urged us toward it, and he said: > **""Indeed the Quran will come to its people on the Day of Resurrection when they are most in need of it. It will say to the Muslim: Do you know me? He will say: Who are you? It will say: I am the one whom you used to love, and whom you disliked to part from, the one who used to draw you along and keep you close. He will say: Perhaps you are the Quran. So it will proceed with him to his Lord, mighty and majestic, and he will be given dominion in his right hand and eternity in his left, and tranquillity will be placed upon his head, and his two parents will be laden with two garments to which the world cannot compare, manifold. They will say: For what have we been clothed with this when our deeds did not reach it? He will say: This is for your child's taking of the Quran.""**"
"11636","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Quran will come on the Day of Resurrection like a pale man, saying to its companion: Do you know me? I am the one who kept your night awake and kept you thirsty in the midday heat. And indeed every trader is behind his trade, and I am for you today beyond every trader. So he will be given dominion in his right hand and eternity in his left, and the crown of dignity will be placed upon his head, and his two parents will be clothed with two garments to which the world and all it contains cannot compare. They will say: O Lord, whence is this for us? It will be said to them: For your child's teaching of the Quran.""**"
"11637","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who said: > **""Whoever recites the Quran and acts upon what is in it, and dies within the community, Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection with the scribes and the rulers. And whoever recites the Quran while it slips away from him and he does not abandon it, he has his reward twice. And whoever is eager for it but cannot master it and does not abandon it, Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection with the nobles of his people, favoured over creation as eagles are favoured over all other birds, and as a spring in a meadow is favoured over what surrounds it. And a caller will call: Where are those whom tending livestock did not distract from reciting My Book? So they will stand, and each of them will be clothed with the crown of honour, and triumph will be given in his right hand and eternity in his left. And if his two parents are Muslims they will be clothed with a garment better than the world and all it contains. They will say: Whence is this for us? It will be said: For what your child used to recite.""**"
"11638","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A man will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and the Quran will be presented to him, though he had neglected its obligations, transgressed its limits, opposed its obedience, and committed its prohibitions. So it will say: O Lord, You entrusted Your verses to a miserable bearer; he transgressed my limits, neglected my obligations, abandoned my obedience, and committed disobedience to me. So it will keep presenting proofs against him until it is said: Then deal with him as you wish. So it will take him by his hand and will not part from him until it casts him headlong upon his nostrils into the Fire. And a man will be brought who had guarded its limits, acted upon its obligations, acted in its obedience, and avoided its disobedience, so it will become a defender on his behalf. It will say: O Lord, You entrusted Your verses to an excellent bearer; he observed my limits out of piety, acted upon my obligations, followed my obedience, and avoided disobedience to me. So it will keep presenting proofs for him until it is said: Then deal with him as you wish. So it will take him by his hand and remain with him until it clothes him with a garment of brocade, places upon him the crown of dominion, and gives him drink from the cup of dominion.""**"
"11639","On the authority of Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever learns a verse from the Book of Allah, it will meet him on the Day of Resurrection smiling in his face.""**"
"11640","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The nobles of my Ummah are the bearers of the Quran.""**"
"11641","On the authority of Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The bearers of the Quran are the chiefs of the people of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"11642","ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet ﷺ sent a delegation to Yemen and appointed over them a commander from among them who was the youngest of them. Days passed and they did not set out. The Prophet ﷺ met a man from among them and said to him: > **""O so-and-so, what is the matter with you? Have you not departed?""** He said: O Messenger of Allah, our commander complains of pain in his leg. So the Prophet ﷺ came to him and breathed upon him, saying seven times: > **""In the name of Allah, and by Allah, I seek refuge in the might of Allah and His power from the evil of what is in it.""** So the man was healed. Then an elder said to him: O Messenger of Allah, will you appoint him over us when he is the youngest of us? So the Prophet ﷺ mentioned his recitation of the Quran. The elder said: O Messenger of Allah, were it not that I fear I would lie back upon it and not uphold it by standing with it, I would have learned it. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Learn it, for indeed the parable of the Quran is like a sack you filled with musk and then tied its mouth. If you open it, the scent of musk spreads from it, and if you leave it, it remains musk laid aside. Likewise is the parable of the Quran when you recite it or when it is in your breast.""**"
"11643","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever says: Glory be to Allah the Magnificent, a seedling is planted for him in Paradise. And whoever recites the Quran and completes it and acts upon what is in it, his two parents will be clothed with a crown more beautiful than the light of the sun in houses of this world, were it in them. Then what do you think of the one who acted upon it himself?""**"
"11644","Kulayb ibn Shihāb, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ʿAlī was in the mosque — I think he said: in the mosque of Kūfah — and he heard a loud clamour. He said: Who are these? It was said: A people reciting the Quran or learning the Quran. So he said: Indeed, they were the most beloved of people to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"11645","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: A man was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with praise, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Have you not seen him learning the Quran?!""**"
"11646","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who said: > **""Whoever recites the Quran in the path of Allah, blessed and exalted, will be recorded with the truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous, and excellent are those as companions.""**"
"11647","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah or on the authority of Abū Saʿīd — Al-Aʿmash was uncertain — who said: It will be said to the companion of the Quran on the Day of Resurrection: Recite and ascend, for your station is at the last verse you recite."
"11648","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""Whoever takes the seven long ones is excellent.""**"
"11649","He also narrated it with another chain from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, the like of it, except that a man dropped out of the chain."
"11650","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever listens to a verse from the Book of Allah, a multiplied good deed is recorded for him, and whoever recites it, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"11651","On the authority of Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""For the reciter of the Quran, when he deems its lawful lawful and its unlawful unlawful, is that he intercede for ten of his household, all of whom had deserved the Fire.""**"
"11652","On the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said — or he said: whoever gathers the Quran: > **""Whoever recites the Quran will have with Allah an answered supplication: if he wishes He hastens it for him in this world, and if He wishes He stores it for him in the Hereafter.""**"
"11653","On the authority of ʿUmar ibn Al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Quran is a thousand thousand letters and twenty-seven thousand letters. So whoever recites it patiently and seeking reward, for every letter he will have a spouse from the wide-eyed maidens.""**"
"11654","On the authority of ʿAwf ibn Mālik Al-Ashjaʿī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites a letter of the Quran, a good deed is recorded for him. And I do not say Alif Lām Mīm Dhālika Al-Kitāb is one, but Alif is a letter, Lām is a letter, Mīm is a letter, Dhāl is a letter, and Kāf is a letter.""**"
"11655","On the authority of Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Recite the Quran with correct articulation, for whoever recites the Quran and articulates it correctly, for every letter is ten good deeds, and expiation of ten evil deeds, and raising by ten degrees.""**"
"11656","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites the Quran in whatever manner, Allah records for him ten good deeds, erases from him ten evil deeds, and raises him ten degrees. And whoever recites and articulates part and errs in part, twenty good deeds are recorded for him, twenty evil deeds are erased from him, and he is raised twenty degrees. And whoever recites it and articulates all of it correctly, forty good deeds are recorded for him, forty evil deeds are erased from him, and he is raised forty degrees.""**"
"11657","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Articulate the Quran correctly and seek out its uncommon words.""**"
"11658","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Articulate the Quran correctly, for it is Arabic. And there will come peoples who will strain it elaborately, and they are not the best of you."
"11659","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by while a man was reciting Sūrah Al-Ḥijr or Sūrah Al-Kahf, then he fell silent, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""This is the gathering with whom I was commanded to restrain myself.""**"
"11660","ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him — said: Indeed this Quran is Allah's banquet, so learn from Allah's banquet what you can. Indeed this Quran is the rope of Allah which He commanded, and it is the clear light and the beneficial healing, a protection for whoever holds fast to it and salvation for whoever clings to it. It does not deviate so as to need straightening, nor does it go astray so as to need reproof. Its wonders do not end, and it does not wear out from repetition. Recite it, for Allah, mighty and majestic, rewards you for every letter of it with ten good deeds. I do not say to you that Alif Lām Mīm is a letter, but Alif is a letter, Lām is a letter, and Mīm is a letter."
"11661","On the authority of Abū Al-Aḥwaṣ, who said: Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said: This Quran is Allah's banquet, so whoever of you is able to learn something from it, let him do so. For indeed the most barren of houses in good is the one in which there is nothing of the Book of Allah. And indeed the house in which there is nothing of the Book of Allah is like a ruined house with no inhabitant. And indeed Satan leaves the house in which he hears Sūrah Al-Baqarah."
"11662","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: My husband is poor and cannot afford anything. So the Prophet ﷺ said to her husband: > **""Do you recite anything of the Quran?""** He said: I recite such-and-such a sūrah and such-and-such a sūrah. So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Excellent, excellent; your husband is rich.""** Then the woman urged her husband on, then she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Prophet of Allah, Allah has now extended our provision."
"11663","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Quran is an intercessor whose intercession is accepted and a pleader whose word is believed. Whoever puts it before him, it will lead him to Paradise, and whoever puts it behind him, it will drive him to the Fire.""**"
"11664","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned tribulations and magnified and emphasized them. So ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Messenger of Allah, then what is the way out of them? He said: > **""The Book of Allah: in it is the account of those before you, and the tidings of those after you, and the decisive word between you. Whoever abandons it out of arrogance, Allah will crush him, and whoever seeks guidance in other than it, Allah will misguide him. It is the firm rope of Allah, and the wise remembrance, and the straight path. It is that which, when the jinn heard it, they said: Indeed we have heard an amazing Quran. It is that in which tongues do not differ, and which much repetition does not wear out.""**"
"11665","On the authority of Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, I bought the apportioned land of so-and-so and profited therein such-and-such. He said: > **""Shall I not inform you of what is greater in profit than that?""** He said: And does such exist? He said: > **""A man who learns ten verses.""** So the man went and learned ten verses, then came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him."
"11666","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him — who said: Whoever loves to know that he loves Allah and His Messenger, let him look: if he loves the Quran, then he loves Allah and His Messenger."
"11667","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Whoever desires knowledge, let him stir up the Quran thoroughly, for in it is the knowledge of the former and the latter peoples."
"11668","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Aws Al-Thaqafī, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A man's recitation apart from the Muṣḥaf is a thousand degrees, and his recitation in the Muṣḥaf is multiplied over that to two thousand degrees.""**"
"11669","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Keep looking continually at the Muṣḥaf."
"11670","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites the Quran by heart or by looking, Allah will give him a tree in Paradise: were a crow to hatch in a branch of its branches and then fly, old age would overtake it before it traverses its leaf.""** In another wording: were a crow to hatch beneath a leaf of it, then that chick grew and rose, old age would overtake it before that leaf is traversed."
"11671","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever teaches his son the Quran by looking, he is forgiven what preceded of his sin and what followed. And whoever teaches it to him by heart, Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection in the image of the moon on the night of fullness, and it will be said to his son: Recite. So each time he recites a verse, Allah, mighty and majestic, raises the father a degree by it, until he ends at the last of what he has of the Quran.""**"
"11672","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: > **""There is no man who teaches his child the Quran in this world except that his father will be crowned on the Day of Resurrection with a crown in Paradise by which the people of Paradise will recognize him, for his teaching his child the Quran in this world.""**"
"11673","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of you are those who learn the Quran and teach it.""**"
"11674","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it, who said: > **""The best of you are those who recite the Quran and cause it to be recited.""**"
"11675","On the authority of Kulayb ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, heard a clamour in the mosque of people reciting the Quran and causing it to be recited, so he said: Glad tidings for these. These were the most beloved of people to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"11676","On the authority of Saʿd ibn Junādah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was among the first of the people of Ṭāʾif to come to the Prophet ﷺ. I left my family in Al-Sarāh in the morning and reached Minā at ʿAṣr. I climbed the mountain then descended, then came to the Prophet ﷺ and accepted Islam. And he taught me Say: He is Allah, the One, and When the earth is shaken with its quaking, and he taught me these words: Glory be to Allah, and praise be to Allah, and there is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest. And he said: > **""They are the lasting righteous deeds.""**"
"11677","And in a narration: And say: O you disbelievers — meaning he also taught him Sūrah Al-Kāfirūn."
"11678","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, used to teach a man a verse, then say: It is better than all upon which the sun has risen, or than anything upon the earth, until he would say that regarding the whole of the Quran."
"11679","And in a narration: When morning came, the people would come to Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, in his house, and he would say: Keep to your places. Then he would pass by those whom he taught the Quran, and say: O so-and-so, which sūrah are you at? He would inform him, then ask in which verse, then dictate to him the verse that follows it, then say: Learn it, for it is better for you than all between heaven and earth. He said: So the man would think there is no verse in the Quran better than it. Then he would pass by the next and say the like to him, until he said that to all of them."
"11680","On the authority of Abū Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Were it said to one of you: Were you to go in the morning to the village you would have four she-camels, he would say: How I wish the morning would come so I may go. Yet were one of you to go in the morning and learn a verse from the Book of Allah, it would be better for him than four, and four, and four, until he counted a great many."
"11681","On the authority of Abū Burdah Al-Ẓafarī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""There will emerge from the two Kāhins a man who will study the Quran with a study that none after him will study.""**"
"11682","On the authority of ʿUbādah ibn Al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no commander of ten except that he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with his hand chained to his neck, until truth frees him or binds him fast. And whoever learns the Quran then forgets it will meet Allah, exalted, as a leper.""**"
"11683","ʿAbd Al-Raḥmān ibn Shibl Al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Muʿāwiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: When I come to my pavilion, stand and inform the people of what you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Recite the Quran, and do not go to excess in it, nor turn away from it, nor eat by it, nor seek to amass by it.""**"
"11684","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Recite the Quran, and do not eat by it, nor amass much by it, nor go to excess in it, nor turn away from it. Learn the Quran, for it is an intercessor for its companion on the Day of Resurrection. Learn Al-Baqarah, for taking it is blessing and leaving it is regret, and the idle sorcerers cannot withstand it.""**"
"11685","Al-Qāsim said: ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The parable of one who recites the Quran and does not act upon it is like sweet basil: its scent is pleasant but it has no taste. And the parable of one who acts upon the Quran and does not recite it is like a date: its taste is pleasant but it has no scent. And the parable of one who acts upon the Quran and recites it is like a citron: its taste is pleasant and its scent is pleasant. And the parable of one who neither recites the Quran nor acts upon it is like colocynth: its taste is foul and its scent is foul."
"11686","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, was approached by a man who said: O Abū ʿAbd Al-Raḥmān, what do you think of a man who recites the Quran reversed? He said: That is one whose heart is reversed. Then a Muṣḥaf was brought that had been adorned and gilded, so ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: Indeed the finest with which the Muṣḥaf is adorned is its recitation in truth."
"11687","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us saying: > **""Seek refuge with Allah from the pit of sorrow.""** They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the pit of sorrow? He said: > **""A pit in a valley in the depths of Hell from which Hell itself seeks refuge four hundred times every day. Allah, exalted, has prepared it for the ostentatious reciters for their deeds. And indeed the most hateful of creation to Allah, mighty and majestic, is a reciter who visits the officials.""**"
"11688","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed this Quran has an eagerness, and people have a lapse away from it. So whoever's lapse is toward moderation, how excellent it is. And whoever's lapse is toward turning away, those are a ruined people.""**"
"11689","On the authority of ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Learn the Book of Allah and guard it and recite it melodiously, for by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it slips away more intensely than camels from their halters.""** In another wording: it slips free more intensely than a milch-camel from its halter."
"11690","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""Guard the Quran, for by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it slips free from the breasts of men more intensely than hobbled camels to their resting places.""**"
"11691","On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""Guard the Quran, for it slips free from the breasts of men more intensely than migratory birds to their homelands.""** In another wording in the major collection: Guard the Quran, for it is wild."
"11692","On the authority of Abū Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The recitation of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was prolonged with lengthening, without trilling."
"11693","On the authority of Ḥudhayfah ibn Al-Yamān, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: > **""Recite the Quran in the melodies of the Arabs and their voices, and beware the melodies of the people of the two Books and the people of immorality. For there will come after me peoples who will trill the Quran like singing, monastic chanting, and wailing. It will not pass their throats. Their hearts are beguiled, and the hearts of those who admire their affair.""**"
"11694","On the authority of Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Recite the Quran with sorrow, for it was sent down with sorrow.""**"
"11695","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed the best of people in recitation is one who, when he recites the Quran, is seen to be sorrowful.""**"
"11696","On the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah never listens to one chanting the Quran.""**"
"11697","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: Which people are best in voice with the Quran? He said: > **""One whose recitation, when you hear it, you see that he fears Allah, mighty and majestic.""**"
"11698","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""He is not from us who does not chant the Quran.""**"
"11699","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""He is not from us who does not chant the Quran.""**"
"11700","On the authority of Ibn Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""He is not from us who does not chant the Quran.""**"
"11701","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Beautify your voices with the Quran.""**"
"11702","And in a narration: > **""Make your voices excellent with the Quran.""**"
"11703","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Nahīk said: While I was standing with ʿAbdullāh ibn Al-Sāʾib, Abū Lubābah, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by us, so we followed him until he entered his house. We sought permission and he permitted us. There was a man, the owner of the goods, who said: Who are you? So we gave him our lineage. He said: Welcome to the neighbour of his ram. Then I heard him say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""He is not from us who does not chant the Quran.""** Ibn Abī Mulaykah said: I said: O Abū Muḥammad, what do you think if he is not blessed with a good voice? He said: He beautifies it as much as he can."
"11704","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a command concerning recitation: > **""Beautify the Quran with your voices.""**"
"11705","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the adornment of the Quran: > **""Everything has an adornment, and the adornment of the Quran is a beautiful voice.""**"
"11706","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the adornment of all things and of the Quran: > **""Everything has an adornment, and the adornment of the Quran is a beautiful voice.""**"
"11707","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, states that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, a beautiful voice is an adornment for the Quran.""**"
"11708","ʿAlqamah, may Allah have mercy upon him, states that Allah had granted him a beautiful voice for the Quran, and that Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, used to send for him so that he would recite the Quran before him, and that when he finished his recitation Ibn Masʿūd would say to him, may my father and mother be ransom for you, recite more to us, for he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""A beautiful voice is an adornment for the Quran.""**"
"11709","On the authority of Abū Mūsā, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning a night recitation in his house overheard by the Prophet ﷺ and ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and what took place between him and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when they met the next morning: > **""O Abū Mūsā, I passed by you last night, and ʿĀʾishah was with me, while you were reciting in your house, and we stood and listened to your recitation. And Abū Mūsā said to him, O Messenger of Allah, had I known, I would have made it most beautiful for you.""**"
"11710","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ explained the effect of recitation upon a house: > **""Indeed, the house in which the Quran is recited, its good increases, and the house in which the Quran is not recited, its good decreases.""**"
"11711","Saʿd ibn al-Mundhir al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, asked about completing the recitation of the Quran in three days and received an affirmative reply, and he continued upon that practice until he passed away: > **""O Messenger of Allah, shall I recite the Quran in three days? Yes.""**"
"11712","Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned an accepted supplication granted for two deeds: > **""Whoever performs an obligatory prayer, he has an accepted supplication, and whoever completes the Quran, he has an accepted supplication.""**"
"11713","Thābit, may Allah have mercy upon him, reports that Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, whenever he completed the Quran, would gather his family and his children and supplicate for them."
